1 NOTE Donald Wilson was born in Trinidad, CO, in 1927.  He was the oldest chi
2 CONC ld of three.  His family moved to Clovis, NM, in late 1933 or early 193
2 CONC 4.  They moved to Lamar, CO, in 1936 and then to La Junta, CO, in 1937.  H
2 CONC e finished his schooling through high school in La Junta, graduating in 1
2 CONC 945.  World War II ended with the surrender of Japan in September 1945.  H
2 CONC owever, many young men were still being drafted, and Don was drafted in
2 CONC to the U.S. Army after he turned 18 the following month.  He went into t
2 CONC he Army Air Corps weather service and ended up being stationed at an ai
2 CONC r base in Goose Bay, Labrador.  He did not serve long, though, because m
2 CONC ost draftees were required to be released from service by February of 1
2 CONC 948 as the nation moved away from war footing.  Don was discharged in J
2 CONC anuary 1947, but with leave due he was home before Christmas 1946 (thou
2 CONC gh he served in the National Guard until 1952).  He went to La Junta Ju
2 CONC nior College, and then was able to transfer to the Colorado School of M
2 CONC ines (in Golden, CO) starting in the fall of 1947.  After some problems i
2 CONC n his Junior year, he took a year off from school and worked briefly fo
2 CONC r the Oklahoma Geophysical Company and then for Century Geophysical Com
2 CONC pany.  It was while he was working in Oklahoma that he met his future w
2 CONC ife, Audrey Gathright, who was working as a nurse in Elk City at the ti
2 CONC me.  He went back to Mines for his Senior year after taking some make-u
2 CONC p courses in Summer 1951.  He and Audrey were married in December 1951, t
2 CONC he day after Christmas.  After he graduated from Mines with a degree in G
2 CONC eophysical Engineering in September 1952, he went to work for Sohio Pet
2 CONC roleum Company in Houston, TX.  Don and Audrey only lived there for six m
2 CONC onths, but their son Michael was born in Houston in 1953.  In the sprin
2 CONC g of 1953, they moved to Wyoming, where Don worked on a seismic crew, e
2 CONC xploring for oil.  They moved often, living in a number of small Wyomin
2 CONC g towns.  Their daughter Catherine was born in Worland, WY, in 1956.  T
2 CONC hings settled down a little bit after Don was promoted to Staff Geophys
2 CONC icist in September 1956, and stationed in Casper, WY.  He resigned from S
2 CONC ohio in December 1958 and moved to Albuquerque, NM, to work for his fat
2 CONC her (Leonard) at Walker Radio Company, a small wholesaler of electronic p
2 CONC arts.  He quit Sohio mainly because the oil business was coming to one o
2 CONC f its periodic busts, and he thought he would probably be laid off soon a
2 CONC nyway.  Sohio eliminated its exploration department six months later.  H
2 CONC e and Audrey lived the rest of their lives in Albuquerque.  She died in J
2 CONC une 1998 at age 68 and he died in July 2009 at age 81.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two most notable aspects of Don's life are probably his long time a
2 CONC t Walker Radio Company (later renamed Walker Electronic Supply Company) a
2 CONC nd his love of working with young people (mainly boys).  He worked at W
2 CONC alker for over 50 years, and was still working up till the time of his d
2 CONC eath.  He was the main driving force at Walker for many years.  In his l
2 CONC ater years, much of the day-to-day running of Walker was taken over by h
2 CONC is daughter Cathy and her husband, Tom Bynon, but Don was still very im
2 CONC portant to the business.  He was able to take it a little easier becaus
2 CONC e of them, and spend a lot of his time on projects he enjoyed, such as d
2 CONC esigning custom intercom systems for customers.  As for working with bo
2 CONC ys, that started while he was in college, when he worked for his room a
2 CONC nd board for three years at the Colorado Industrial School for Boys as a h
2 CONC andicraft counselor and night watchman.  In Albuquerque, he combined hi
2 CONC s love of chess with his love of working with kids, and helped form the A
2 CONC lbuquerque Schools Chess League, which he ran for nearly 30 years.  Whe
2 CONC n his son Mike was old enough to go into Cub Scouts and then into Boy S
2 CONC couts, Don became heavily involved with them, including becoming first a C
2 CONC ubmaster of Pack 189 and later a Scoutmaster of Troop 189 (both sponsor
2 CONC ed by Our Lady of Fatima Catholic Church and its school, where Mike and C
2 CONC athy went for grades 1-8).  Don continued working with the Boy Scouts f
2 CONC or many years, even after Mike had gone on to college.  He also was act
2 CONC ive in other ways with the Church, including doing much work on its pub
2 CONC lic address system over the years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don was always quite active.  For a long time, he was heavily involved i
2 CONC n the Albuquerque Chess Club, including running tournaments and playing r
2 CONC egularly (his USCF rating was 1900 at its highest, and he was the Albuq
2 CONC uerque city champion in 1963).  He loved hiking and camping and did the
2 CONC m a lot, mainly with the Boy Scout troop.  For many years, he used to b
2 CONC icycle to work.  He also enjoyed working with dogs, and got very involv
2 CONC ed in dog obedience training and competitions, and the Sandia Dog Obedi
2 CONC ence Club.  His dogs were all dachshunds, which are not known for being e
2 CONC asy to train.  However, Don was quite successful at it, and one of his d
2 CONC ogs, Coco, was a top-scoring obedience competitor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don always had trouble with his back and knees (going back to injuries i
2 CONC n college), and in his later years they got bad enough that he could no l
2 CONC onger get out and hike or bicycle.  It got even worse in his last few y
2 CONC ears, when he developed neuropathy, and a loss of feeling and control i
2 CONC n his legs made it difficult for him to walk.  He became known in his n
2 CONC eighborhood for riding around on an electric scooter -- often with his d
2 CONC og, Shado, along on a leash.  He developed adult-onset diabetes late in l
2 CONC ife.  Diabetes can cause neuropathy, but Don was convinced that his sev
2 CONC ere back and neuropathy problems were caused by an incident in which he w
2 CONC as hit by a car while he was riding his bike.  He had back surgery and n
2 CONC eck surgery to try to lessen his problems, with some limited success.  H
2 CONC is last back surgery led to his death after the incision in his lower b
2 CONC ack became infected.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate is Donald Everett Wilson.  Cause of d
2 CONC eath was staphlacoccus sepsis (presumably this should be staphylococcus
2 CONC ).  He died at Presbyterian Hospital.
2 CONT Headstone of his grave says Donald E. WILSON, 1927 - 2009.
2 CONT
