1 NOTE Leonard Wilson was born in Denver, CO, in 1904.  He was an only child. H
2 CONC is parents were divorced and his father left when he was only two.  His m
2 CONC other had a series of husbands and he practically supported himself fro
2 CONC m an early age.  He and Elizabeth Trinaistich were both working for the T
2 CONC rinidad Creamery Company in Trinidad, CO, when they married in 1926.  T
2 CONC hey lived in several towns in Colorado and New Mexico before settling f
2 CONC or good in Albuquerque, NM, in 1947.  He died in Albuquerque in 1981 at t
2 CONC he age of 77.
2 CONT
2 CONT Len worked as a traveling salesman for the Creamery, with an area that w
2 CONC ent all the way to Phoenix, AZ.  Betty worked as a bookkeeper.  Two of t
2 CONC heir children, Donald and Dorothy, were born during this period in Trin
2 CONC idad, in 1927 and 1930.  Mr. Jacobsen, owner of the Creamery, became in
2 CONC terested in radio as a way to get his pricing for cream out to the farm
2 CONC ers much faster.  Len was already an active amateur radio operator ("ha
2 CONC m"), so Jacobsen approached him about building a radio station.  This i
2 CONC nquiry led to building radio station KGIW about 1930. The station did n
2 CONC ot pan out to Jacobsen's satisfaction and he wanted to shut it down.  L
2 CONC en was a believer in this new technology and offered to buy the station f
2 CONC rom him.  Len and Betty remained good friends with the Jacobsen family f
2 CONC or as long as they lived.  Shortly after buying the station, he moved i
2 CONC t to Alamosa, CO, and also applied for a permit to build a station in C
2 CONC lovis, NM.  This permit was granted and so Len built radio station KICA i
2 CONC n Clovis, which went on the air some time in 1932 or '33.  He moved his f
2 CONC amily to Clovis at the end of 1933 or beginning of 1934.  The station i
2 CONC n Clovis never did well, so he sold it, probably in the spring of 1936.  H
2 CONC e also sold KGIW in Alamosa.  He started another station in Lamar, CO, w
2 CONC hich he operated until he sold it in 1937.  He applied for a permit fro
2 CONC m the FCC to build a station in La Junta, CO, which was granted with th
2 CONC e call letters KOKO.  (The KOKO letters had previously been used by the N
2 CONC avy.  A few years after this the Navy wanted the call letters back but L
2 CONC en would not give them up.  The Navy got them back after he sold the st
2 CONC ation.)  The family moved from Lamar to La Junta after school was out i
2 CONC n the spring of 1937.  KOKO went on the air in the spring or summer of 1
2 CONC 937.  Their third child, James, was born in 1939 in La Junta.  Also, Be
2 CONC tty's sister Mary died in 1937 shortly after her son John was born.  Be
2 CONC tty and Len took in the baby and raised him with their own children. Ma
2 CONC ry's young daughter Elsie (six years old at the time of her mother's de
2 CONC ath) also lived with them for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Len was on his ham radio with a friend who was on a ship at Pearl Harbo
2 CONC r at the time of the Japanese bombing.  Len broadcast the news about th
2 CONC e bombing before anyone else knew, and then got a visit from the govern
2 CONC ment, wondering how he knew about it!  It was along time before he knew i
2 CONC f his friend was alive or not.
2 CONT
2 CONT Len had taken up flying in the late 1930's and earned his private licen
2 CONC se. He tried to join the Air Corps when World War II broke out, but cou
2 CONC ld not pass the physical because of his eyesight.  He also tried to joi
2 CONC n the Canadian Air Force, but crash-landed in North Dakota on the way u
2 CONC p.  He never went back.  Fortunately, the plane was not badly damaged a
2 CONC nd he was able to fly back to La Junta.  He ended up flying courier ser
2 CONC vice in the Civil Air Patrol for the Army Air Corps during most of the w
2 CONC ar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Len sold KOKO in the fall of 1946.  After selling the station he stayed o
2 CONC n, managing it for the new owners while he started looking for somethin
2 CONC g else to do.  During this search he had occasion to talk to Larry Walk
2 CONC er, who owned the L. B. Walker Radio Company in Pueblo, CO.  He had bee
2 CONC n a customer of Walker's for a number of years, buying electronic parts f
2 CONC or his various radio stations.  Walker suggested opening a branch somew
2 CONC here.  (At this time Walker had no other branches.  He eventually opene
2 CONC d several other stores in Colorado.) They decided to open a branch in A
2 CONC lbuquerque, NM, which resulted in the opening of the L. B. Walker Radio C
2 CONC ompany in Albuquerque on 6 May 1947.  There were originally seven peopl
2 CONC e involved in the store: Len and Betty Wilson, Larry and Mildred Walker
2 CONC , Bill Thomas and his wife, and Margaret Johnson.  Bill had worked at t
2 CONC he station in Alamosa and Margaret had worked at KOKO.  After a few yea
2 CONC rs, Walker sold his interest to Len because he wanted to open a store i
2 CONC n Denver, which he did in 1952 or '53.  The name of the business was sh
2 CONC ortened to Walker Radio Company.  Margaret and Bill worked at Walker Ra
2 CONC dio for many years, but they eventually sold their shares to Len as wel
2 CONC l, leaving Walker Radio Company owned entirely by the family.  (There w
2 CONC as a rider on the stock giving Len the first right to purchase the stoc
2 CONC k, which he did.) Years later, after Len's death, the name of the busin
2 CONC ess was further changed to Walker Electronic Supply Company.  Even thou
2 CONC gh Larry Walker was involved in the business for only a few years, it c
2 CONC ontinued to carry his name because of concern that customers would be c
2 CONC onfused if the name were changed.  Len ran Walker Radio until the time o
2 CONC f his death. His son Don joined him in the business in 1958 and carried o
2 CONC n after his death.  Betty was also active in the business, acting as th
2 CONC e bookkeeper and president of the company for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through all of this, Len remained active in amateur radio.  His final c
2 CONC all letters were W5NVT.  His original two-letter call was lost during W
2 CONC WII, when all hams were shut down and the government bought all their e
2 CONC quipment.  In his younger years, he was also quite an outdoorsman and l
2 CONC oved to fish.  In addition, he was active in the Knights of Columbus fr
2 CONC aternal organization for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT      -- Compiled from e-mails and conversations with my father, Donald E
2 CONC . Wilson
2 CONT
2 CONT
