1 NOTE Betty loved to cook, and made lots of wonderful things to eat.  Most me
2 CONC morable were the desserts -- pies, angel food cake, nut roll (povitica, a C
2 CONC roatian pastry).  She cooked dishes from the Old Country of her parents
2 CONC , and New Mexican food from her adopted state.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was an excellent seamstress, and made many of her own and her daugh
2 CONC ter's clothes.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a gardener.  She always had a small vegetable garden, and had f
2 CONC ruit and nut trees. But most important were her flower gardens, includi
2 CONC ng a fine rose garden.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had lots of friends and was very active socially.  She was a member o
2 CONC f a bridge club for many years, and she and her husband, Len, liked to s
2 CONC quare dance and were involved in church activities.
2 CONT
2 CONT She enjoyed traveling, and even traveled around the world twice with he
2 CONC r nephew Ernie and others.
2 CONT
2 CONT And finally, she always worked hard, both around the house and helping o
2 CONC ut in her husband's business.  She was the bookkeeper for Walker Radio C
2 CONC ompany for many years, and was listed as the president of the company.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died from aspiration pneumonia -- stomach fluid in her lungs -- at t
2 CONC he age of 92.  This was the culmination of a problem she had for severa
2 CONC l years before she died -- she had problems with swallowing and with ch
2 CONC oking on phlegm that built up in her throat.  She was confined to a whe
2 CONC elchair for several years before her death, and eventually couldn't eve
2 CONC n get around by wheelchair any more and was bedridden.  She also develo
2 CONC ped diabetes late in life.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper article (don't know what paper, or the date, but the wedding w
2 CONC as 20 Oct 1926):
2 CONT
2 CONT TRINAISTICH-WILSON NUPTIALS.
2 CONT
2 CONT A pretty fall wedding will be solemnized this evening at five o'clock a
2 CONC t the Catholic Church when Miss Elizabeth Trinaistich will become the b
2 CONC ride of Mr. Leonard Wilson.  Mr. and Mrs. John McKelvey will act as mat
2 CONC ron of honor and groomsman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Trinaistich will wear a smart frock of jungle green flat crepe wit
2 CONC h accessories to match, and will carry a shower bouquet of Ophelia rose
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT At 6:30 a five course wedding supper will be served at the home of Mr. W
2 CONC ilson's mother, Mrs. E. W. Cookingham on Topeka Ave.  Covers will be la
2 CONC id for sixty.  A lovely color scheme of blue and white will be effectiv
2 CONC ely carried out, and an elaborately frosted brides cake will grace the c
2 CONC enter of the table.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Trinaistich is the youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Matt Trinaist
2 CONC ich of Trinidad.  She is a graduate of Trinidad High School and since h
2 CONC er graduation has been employed as bookkeeper at the Trinidad Creamery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilson is the son of Mrs. E. W. Cookingham of Topeka Ave.  He too r
2 CONC eceived his education here and graduated from T. H. S.  He holds a posi
2 CONC tion of bookkeeper at the Trinidad Creamery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The happy couple will leave immediately for a ten days honeymoon trip t
2 CONC hru the northern part of the state.  They will take up their residence i
2 CONC n Trinidad.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper article from the Albuquerque Journal on 31 Oct 1976:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anniversary Date Noted By Wilsons
2 CONT
2 CONT In celebration of their golden wedding anniversary, Mr. and Mrs. Leonar
2 CONC d E. Wilson, 221 Washington NE, were honored at a mass at Our Lady of F
2 CONC atima Church, followed by a reception in the Hilton Inn's Cabaret Loung
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple was married at Trinidad, Colo., on Oct. 20, 1926.  They have b
2 CONC een residents of Albuquerque since 1947.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hosts for the observance were their sons, Donald and his wife Audrey of A
2 CONC lbuquerque, James and his wife Susie of Phoenix, Ariz., and their daugh
2 CONC ter Dorothy of Albuquerque.  There are twograndchildren [sic].
2 CONT
2 CONT Attending the reception were approximately 115 guests from California, A
2 CONC rizona, Colorado and New Mexico.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on her death certificate is Elizabeth Marie Wilson.  Cause of d
2 CONC eath was aspiration pneumonia.  She died at St. Joseph Medical Center.
2 CONT The name on her birth certificate is just Elizabeth Trinaistich.  Does t
2 CONC hat mean that officially she had no middle name?
2 CONT Headstone of her grave says Elizabeth M. WILSON, 1904 - 1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT
