1 NOTE "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, p. 754:
2 CONT
2 CONT LEONARD WILSON, postmaster, P. O., Marshall.  Mr. Wilson is a native of t
2 CONC his state, also of this county, and was born in Marshall on the 13th of J
2 CONC anuary, 1846.  At the age of fifteen he commenced clerking in the store o
2 CONC f Q. O. Striker, in Marshall, and from 1862 to 1864 he was deputy postm
2 CONC aster of Marshall.  In 1865 he was deputy circuit clerk. In 1870 he was e
2 CONC ngaged in the grocery business in Marshall, which he continued to 1873, w
2 CONC hen he was appointed by President Grant, postmaster of Marshall, re-app
2 CONC ointed in 1877 by President Hayes, and in 1881 by President Garfield.  I
2 CONC n 1864 he served in Capt. Bingham's company E. M. M. [enrolled Missouri m
2 CONC ilitia -- Union], and was taken prisoner at Glasgow, which ended his mi
2 CONC litary service.  On the 6th of October, 1868, he was married to Miss Ma
2 CONC ry Sandidge, daughter of John Sandidge, of Saline county, and has had t
2 CONC hree children, only one, Leonie, living.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1870, Leonard ran to succeed his brother Ben as circuit clerk, but h
2 CONC e was defeated in the election ("History of Saline County", p. 365).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find A Grave (listing for Leonard W. [sic] Wilson):
2 CONT
2 CONT From a 1905 Nance County, Nebraska, newspaper clipping file, transcribe
2 CONC d by Linda Berney:
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Prominent Denver Jurist Dead -
2 CONT The following notice of the death of Leonard Wilson is taken from the D
2 CONC enver Post.  Deceased was well known to many of our readers, having bee
2 CONC n in business here with his brother H. E. Wilson a number of years:
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonard Wilson of 1641 Franklin street, brother of Adair Wilson, former
2 CONC ly of the court of appeals, died yesterday afternoon of a complication o
2 CONC f kidney and heart troubles.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to Denver from Marshall, Mo., in 1884, and for the past seven y
2 CONC ears had been a clerk in the Western Union Telegraph office.  He is sur
2 CONC vived by his widow,; a daughter, Mrs. J. E. Rinker of Cripple Creek, a s
2 CONC on, A. G. [sic - should be H. E.] Wilson, employed at the Denver Union d
2 CONC epot and two brothers besides Judge Wilson.
2 CONT
2 CONT [The middle initial of W in the Find A Grave listing is questionable.  N
2 CONC o record is known with this (or any other) middle initial.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that Leonard was named for his uncle, Abiel Leonard.  It i
2 CONC s even possible that his full name was Abiel Leonard Wilson, but I have n
2 CONC ever seen that anywhere.  Both of the children of Leonard and Mary Wils
2 CONC on were born in Marshall, Missouri; Leonie in 1873 and Horace in 1882.  A
2 CONC ccording to the McGrew letter, they moved to Colorado in 1884, Denver i
2 CONC n 1885.  From the short obituary above, he lived for a time in Nance Co
2 CONC ., NE; perhaps that was between Missouri and Colorado.  Brother Horace a
2 CONC pparently stayed there longer; he was married in Nance County in 1886.  A
2 CONC lternatively, perhaps Leonard did business in Nance County when he was i
2 CONC n the grocery business in the 1870s, but never lived there.
2 CONT
2 CONT The web site of the Missouri Secretary of State has old military servic
2 CONC e cards online at http://www.sos.mo.gov/archives/soldiers/.  The one fo
2 CONC r Leonard Wilson partially agrees with the information in the short bio
2 CONC graphy above (it mentions Capt. Bingham and E. M. M.), but it seems to i
2 CONC ndicate that he did not serve because of disability.  I don’t know what t
2 CONC hat’s about.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate is Leonard Wilson.  Can't read cause o
2 CONC f death.  Died at home (1641 Franklin).
2 CONT
2 CONT
