1 NOTE "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, pp. 415-416:
2 CONT
2 CONT JUDGE W. A. WILSON.
2 CONT
2 CONT The subject of this sketch--the youngest of four brothers, John, Robert
2 CONC , David, and Wm., sons of Wm. Wilson--was born, December 17, 1807, in A
2 CONC ugusta county, Virginia.  He, with his brothers, came to this state, lo
2 CONC cating in Howard county, in 1825.  He afterwards located in this county
2 CONC .  His brother David died many years ago, and Gen. John Wilson removed t
2 CONC o California, filled quite a number of high official positions, and die
2 CONC d in San Francisco, some two years since.  Robert located in St. Joseph
2 CONC , Missouri, represented his county and district for many years in the s
2 CONC tate legislature and senate, and other offices, and served out the unex
2 CONC pired term of one of our U. S. senators.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June, 1835, Judge Wilson married Miss Mary E. Reeves, daughter of th
2 CONC e late ex-Governor Reeves, of Missouri.  His wife survives him and resi
2 CONC des on the old homestead, in Marshall, Missouri.  The result of this ma
2 CONC rriage was five sons and two daughters, all living, except Martha C., w
2 CONC ho married Dr. Grove, of this county, and died in 1865, in Fayette, Ind
2 CONC iana, the present home of her husband; Annette married the Hon. John R. V
2 CONC ance, and now resides in Marshall, as does also Leonard Wilson, our pre
2 CONC sent efficient postmaster.  Col. Ben. H., Hon. W. A. and Bird L. Wilson n
2 CONC ow reside in Colorado; and Horace E. Wilson resides at St. Joseph, Miss
2 CONC ouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judges Wilson and Leonard, having located in Howard county about the sa
2 CONC me time, and having married sisters, there sprang up between them a ver
2 CONC y strong social, personal and political attachment, which lasted during t
2 CONC heir lives.  Both were staunch whigs, and both consistent, firm and unc
2 CONC ompromising Union men, and cordially supported the national government d
2 CONC uring the late conflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1835, he was appointed lieutenant-colonel of militia, by Gov. Dunkli
2 CONC n.  In August, 1836, he moved to Saline, and in 1838 was elected county j
2 CONC udge, serving with Judges Brown and Field.  On February, 6, 1850, appoi
2 CONC nted county and circuit clerk, and ex-officio recorder, vice John A. Tr
2 CONC igg, resigned, and in March following was elected to said offices for u
2 CONC nexpired term, extending to 1854, when he was re-elected and filled sai
2 CONC d offices for the term of six years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1862, Judge Wilson was appointed by Gov. Gamble, circuit and county c
2 CONC lerk of the county, and in the same year, provost marshal, with rank of m
2 CONC ajor.  When the 71st regiment, E. M. M. [enrolled Missouri militia -- U
2 CONC nion], was organized, he was made lieutenant-colonel of same.  It is no
2 CONC t saying too much to say, that no one in this county was better or more f
2 CONC avorably known at Union or Federal headquarters of this state, or at Wa
2 CONC shington City, than Judge W. A. Wilson.  He filled the clerkships of th
2 CONC e county up to the time of his death, August 18, 1865 [should be August 2
2 CONC 8]. Judge Wilson was a lawyer by profession, and, except while in offic
2 CONC e, practiced that profession.  He was, for a time, law-partner of Col. T
2 CONC . W. B. Crews and Hon. W. H. Letcher.  He, like his brothers, Robert an
2 CONC d John, was a man of more than ordinary ability, and possessed, in a re
2 CONC markable degree, that foresight and confidence, so well calculated to l
2 CONC ead and inspire any party with which he might become connected. In the d
2 CONC ischarge of all his official duties, he was honest and efficient, and n
2 CONC ever betrayed the trusts confided to him.  For more than twenty years p
2 CONC rior to his death, perhaps, no man was more generally or prominently kn
2 CONC own to the people here.  As a public spirited citizen, he was ever read
2 CONC y to contribute to every laudable enterprise.  A man of excellent judgm
2 CONC ent--having once made up his mind, was determined and invincible.  Duty a
2 CONC nd right outweighed all other considerations with him.  He was a Union m
2 CONC an in the broadest sense of that term.  It is not saying too much, when w
2 CONC e say, he formed the nucleus around which the Union element of this cou
2 CONC nty rallied, more, perhaps, than any one else.  Of a pure, positive and u
2 CONC nsullied character, his opinions were ever sought and respected by his f
2 CONC riends. He was faithful in all the relations of life; as a husband, par
2 CONC ent, friend, the same integrity of character and goodness of heart were m
2 CONC anifested, and gained for him that love and confidence he possessed to s
2 CONC uch an eminent degree.  He was unswervingly true to his principles and t
2 CONC o the instincts of his generous and manly nature.  In his friendship he w
2 CONC as faithful; in his susceptibilities, tender and emotional; in disposit
2 CONC ion and temperament, genial in a very high degree.  In all the relation
2 CONC s of life, he bore himself as a "man among men."  His memory to us is p
2 CONC recious, and his virtues remain a legacy to his surviving co-laborers i
2 CONC n the cause to which he gave the strength and power of his manhood.  Up
2 CONC on that brow, now cold in death, honors thick had clustered; and as he w
2 CONC ent forth from our ranks, many eyes were dim with tears, and many heart
2 CONC s were full of sighs.  In the cemetery, at Mount Olive, "may his ashes r
2 CONC equiescat in pace."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that he has a gravestone in Ridge Park Cemetery, Marshall, MO.  Pe
2 CONC rhaps the cemetery changed names or his remains were moved to Ridge Par
2 CONC k Cemetery?
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The above biography of William Adair Wilson says that he came with his f
2 CONC amily from Augusta County, VA, to Howard County, MO, in 1825; he then m
2 CONC oved to Saline County in August 1836.  When he moved to Saline County, h
2 CONC e initially moved to Old Jefferson, which no longer exists; it was on t
2 CONC he Missouri River in the northeastern part of the county near present-d
2 CONC ay Cambridge.  Later, he moved to Marshall; from letters in the Abiel L
2 CONC eonard Papers of the State Historical Society of Missouri Manuscript Co
2 CONC llection, it looks like he moved from Old Jefferson to Marshall in late 1
2 CONC 844.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From a biography of his son Adair in "Portrait and Biographical Record o
2 CONC f Denver and Vicinity, Colorado", Chapman Publishing Co., Chicago, 1898
2 CONC , pp. 321–325 (obtained from http://www.memoriallibrary.com/CO/1898Denv
2 CONC erPB/pages/pbrd0319.htm):
2 CONT
2 CONT The youngest of his large family was William A., a native of Augusta Co
2 CONC unty, Va., and in early life a merchant, but later a student of law wit
2 CONC h his brother, Gen. John Wilson, who had preceded the family to Missour
2 CONC i and had served in the war of 1812.  William was admitted to the bar i
2 CONC n Saline County and opened an office in Marshall, where he was a pionee
2 CONC r and prominent attorney. For years he was clerk of all the courts ther
2 CONC e. When the Civil war broke out he was somewhat advanced in years, but e
2 CONC nlisted in the state militia and was made lieutenant-colonel of a regim
2 CONC ent, serving until the close of the war, but the exposure of camp life c
2 CONC aused his death soon afterwards.  He was then about fifty-seven years o
2 CONC f age. Fraternally he was a Mason.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an interesting letter in the Abiel Leonard Papers from John Wi
2 CONC lson (William’s older brother) to Abiel Leonard, dated 13 Nov [?] 1852, i
2 CONC n which John says (some punctuation added):
2 CONT
2 CONT I have been thinking much of of [sic] my brother Wm & his family, & am e
2 CONC xceeding desirous to get them here [i.e., California] -- To remain at S
2 CONC aline, even with his clerkships, he can only live, & not gather any thi
2 CONC ng.  His family is large & growing & he ought in some way if he can pro
2 CONC vide better means for their support & education.  if he was here with o
2 CONC nly a couple of thousand Dollars to begin with he wd. on that day be ri
2 CONC cher than he can by any possibility be at the end of 20 yrs.  The quest
2 CONC ion is how is that to be effected?  I have written to my brother on the s
2 CONC ubject & I take the liberty to write to you and have made to my brother t
2 CONC his suggestion & hope I shall not offend you by laying it before you.  I s
2 CONC uppose, for I do not know the fact, that he could not do more than make a d
2 CONC ecent outfit to get here over the plains, & nothing left to begin on he
2 CONC re after he came, & without money is rather a bad fix here, but even th
2 CONC at will be better for him & his family than to stay where he is.  If my b
2 CONC rother & you will endorse for him for about 3000 [$ is probably cut off
2 CONC ] for two years or 18 months interest at 10 per cent, letting the lende
2 CONC r take out the first year’s percentage at the paying of the money & the o
2 CONC ther years interest & the debt to be paid at the end of the two years o
2 CONC r 18 months as it shall be agreed, & let him lay that money out in eith
2 CONC er cattle & sheep & bring it over the plains, he can pay the debt when d
2 CONC ue & int. & have a fund in cattle worth 20000 $ (indeed far more) to go o
2 CONC n -- I will stand his security to you & my brother that he pays the deb
2 CONC t when it is due.  the arrangement if made cannot fail to place him in e
2 CONC asy circumstances.  I shall be able by the time he gets here to give hi
2 CONC m a farm & I can say to you & my brother both that whatever you may say I s
2 CONC hall be good security unless all signs fail.
2 CONT
2 CONT [John’s proposal is very similar to what Rufus McDonald and James Wilso
2 CONC n (nephew-in-law and nephew to John and William) actually did in 1864 -
2 CONC - they took a lot of mules from Missouri to California and made a big p
2 CONC rofit on them (see the notes for Rufus LeeMcDonald).  William must not h
2 CONC ave liked the idea, though (or the deal fell through).]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to "History of Saline County", William lost the positions of c
2 CONC ounty and circuit clerk in the election of 1859.  Then, according to th
2 CONC e above, he was re-appointed to them in 1862. Presumably, the election w
2 CONC inners had run off and joined the Confederates.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the McGrew letter, during the Civil War, friends and neigh
2 CONC bors who sympathized and fought with the Confederacy made so many threa
2 CONC ts against his life (even coming to his home with ropes to hang him) an
2 CONC d also threatened the lives of his wife and children, that to protect h
2 CONC is family he moved them to Ohio for the duration of the Civil War.  In "
2 CONC History of Saline County, Missouri", it says that 90% of the citizens o
2 CONC f Saline County were from Virginia, Kentucky, or Tennessee, and most we
2 CONC re Confederate sympathizers (I have seen elsewhere that this part of Mi
2 CONC ssouri has sometimes been called "little Dixie").  William A. Wilson wa
2 CONC s an important leader of the Union sympathizers.  Marshall was occupied b
2 CONC y the Union during most of the Civil War; Lt. Col. William A. Wilson wa
2 CONC s in command of the post part of the time (in 1862 and 1863).  From let
2 CONC ters in the Abiel Leonard Papers, it was Oxford, OH, where the family w
2 CONC ent, probably sometime in 1864.  They may have chosen Oxford because it i
2 CONC s fairly near to where William’s sister, Nancy Wilson Loop, lived (in S
2 CONC omers Township, near Camden, OH).  There are more details on all of thi
2 CONC s in the WilsonCivilWar document.  In December 1864, William was the on
2 CONC ly one of the family in Marshall.  Adair was in Nevada, Ben was in the A
2 CONC rmy, and the rest of the family was in Oxford.  Leonard returned to Mar
2 CONC shall at the beginning of Jan 1865, and Ben returned a few months later a
2 CONC fter he was mustered out of the service.  It appears that the rest of t
2 CONC he family only returned at the time of William's death (perhaps in time t
2 CONC o see him before he died).  In a letter dated 15 Aug 1865 (from the Abi
2 CONC el Leonard Papers), Leonard wrote "I would have sent it [a deed] sooner b
2 CONC ut Father has been so unwell that I was was [sic] unable to attend to i
2 CONC t.  he is not improving any.  we have sent for Mother  She will be here i
2 CONC n a few days."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a little more about his passing in a death notice in the Howar
2 CONC d Union (Glasgow, MO), 7 Sep 1865, p. 2 (obtained from Newspapers.com):
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. William A. Wilson died at the Missouri Hotel, in Marshal [sic], on t
2 CONC he 28th ult., after a painful illness of more than three weeks of typho
2 CONC id fever.  The deceased was an old and valued citizen of Saline county.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William's will, from his probate case file (obtained from the Saline Co
2 CONC unty probate clerk; it is difficult to read in places, and poorly punct
2 CONC uated, so some may not be quite right):
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen, I William A. Wilson of the County of Saline in t
2 CONC he State of Missouri, do make and declare this my last will and testame
2 CONC nt hereby revoking all others or former wills by me made
2 CONT
2 CONT First   I will that all my just debts and funeral expenses be paid of w
2 CONC hatever kind
2 CONT
2 CONT Second   I give and bequeath to my beloved wife Mary E. Wilson all the r
2 CONC emainder of my estate, both real and personal, to hold and use during h
2 CONC er natural life for her support and the support and education of the yo
2 CONC unger children  All money arising from the sale of land or otherwise wh
2 CONC ich may not be necessary for the current expenses of the family, accord
2 CONC ing to the judgment of my executrix hereinafter named, I will, may be l
2 CONC oaned or invested for the benefit of the estate as my executrix may thi
2 CONC nk best.
2 CONT
2 CONT Third   I give and bequeath to my son Benjamin H. Wilson, one thousand d
2 CONC ollars, it being money loaned to and expend for me, at different times, a
2 CONC nd I will that no account be made of it at the final distribution of my e
2 CONC state
2 CONT
2 CONT Fourth   I give and bequeath to my daughter Kate Grove five dollars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fifth   I have given to my son Adair Wilson, one thousand dollars which I w
2 CONC ish to have charged to him and taken from his distributive share of the e
2 CONC state at the final distribution
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixth   I give and bequeath to my daughter Annett Wilson five dollars
2 CONT
2 CONT Seventh   I give and bequeath to my sons Leonard Wilson, Horace E. Wils
2 CONC on & Bird Wilson, each the sum of five dollars, as they arrive at the a
2 CONC ge of twenty one years respectively prior to distribution
2 CONT
2 CONT Eighth   I hereby appoint my beloved wife, Mary E. Wilson sole executri
2 CONC x of this my last will and testament with full power to execute the sam
2 CONC e, and to make such distribution to the heirs of my estate as she in he
2 CONC r judgment may think best at any time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ninth   At the final distribution of the residue of my estate among the h
2 CONC eirs thereof, after paying the legacies heretofore named I will that al
2 CONC l the surviving heirs as children share and share alike, and if any sai
2 CONC d heirs should die leaving issue they are to receive the distributive s
2 CONC hare of said deceased heir  In witness whereof I hereunto subscribe my n
2 CONC ame and affix my seal this 24th day of August A.D. 1865   W. A. Wilson
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate inventory is listed as $10,892.12 personal and $10,135 real.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 census
2 CONT William Adair might have been living with brother John Wilson in Howard C
2 CONC o., MO.  There were two 20-29-year-old males in the household; one of t
2 CONC hem could have been William Adair.
2 CONT
