1 NOTE Unknown newspaper (possibly the Fruita Times), 7 Jan 1931, pp. 1, 4 (ob
2 CONC tained from Richard & Lauretta Gillen):
2 CONT
2 CONT FUNERAL FOR B. L. WILSON, WIDELY KNOWN NEWSPAPER MAN, AT FRUITA TOMORRO
2 CONC W
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for the late B. L. Wilson, pioneer newspaper man of Co
2 CONC lorado, who for many years edited the Mesa County Mail at Fruita, and l
2 CONC ater was engaged in the grocery business in the lower valley town, will b
2 CONC e held tomorrow afternoon at 2 o'clock at the Starks-DeYarman funeral h
2 CONC ome in Fruita.  Interment will be in Elmwood cemetery near Fruita.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of the veteran newspaperman, who enjoyed a wide acquaintance t
2 CONC hroughout the entire western slope and also in Denver, and many other C
2 CONC olorado points, occurred suddenly Monday night at the Park hotel in Fru
2 CONC ita, where he had boarded for several years.  After eating his supper, h
2 CONC e had gone to a bathroom in the hotel, and it was there his lifeless bo
2 CONC dy was found.  Death was attributed to heart disease.  He had been in i
2 CONC ll health following an influenza attack suffered about three months ago
2 CONC .  He was 70 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coming to Colorado in the early 70's, when he was but a boy, Byrd Linco
2 CONC ln Wilson virtually grew up with the newspaper industry of the state.  A
2 CONC s a boy, he worked in the mines of the San Juan district, and later wor
2 CONC ked in farming districts around the present location of Durango, which h
2 CONC ad not then been established as a city.
2 CONT
2 CONT His entry into the printing business came to Mr. Wilson as a helper in n
2 CONC ewspaper offices of the mining camps of western Colorado.  Among the sh
2 CONC ops in which he was employed was the Ouray newspaper edited by David S. D
2 CONC ay, one of the most famous of the earlier Colorado editors.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1880, Mr. Wilson went to Leadville, working in mines there during th
2 CONC e "boom" days.  From there he went to Denver, where, according to frien
2 CONC ds at Fruita, he was employed for nearly 30 years in the composing depa
2 CONC rtment of the Rocky Mountain News, serving as foreman of the advertisin
2 CONC g composing room.  For about 10 years he was employed in the mechanical d
2 CONC epartment of the Denver Post.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilson came to Fruita about ten years ago, taking over the editorsh
2 CONC ip of the Mesa County Mail.  He continued in that post until about two y
2 CONC ears ago when that paper was sold to new owners.  He then entered into p
2 CONC artnership with L. E. Gredell in a grocery business in Fruita, the part
2 CONC nership continuing until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilson is survived by his widow, who was in the hotel at the time o
2 CONC f his death.  The only other survivor among his immediate relatives, so f
2 CONC ar as could be learned today, was a brother in Texas, whose address is n
2 CONC ot known.  An older brother who died a few years ago, was Judge Adair W
2 CONC ilson, who many years ago served as district judge in the southwestern p
2 CONC art of the state and later as judge of the state court of appeals.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his long residence in Denver, Mr. Wilson served at one time as p
2 CONC resident of the Denver local of the typographical union and was the lea
2 CONC der in organizing a benefit association in connection with the union, t
2 CONC he association still being in existence.  He was a member of the Masoni
2 CONC c and Knights of Pythias lodges.  He was prominent in political circles i
2 CONC n Mesa county for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilson was born in Saline county, Missouri, on July 12, 1860.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate (obtained from Richard & Lauretta Gil
2 CONC len) is Byrd Lincoln Wilson.  Cause of death listed as arteriosclerosis m
2 CONC yocarditis.  Place of death just listed as Fruita, Mesa Co., CO.  Occup
2 CONC ation listed as grocer.
2 CONT Headstone says Byrd L. Wilson, 1860 - 1931.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930 census, Byrd's age at first marriage is listed as 38, which w
2 CONC ould be about 1898.  In the 1910 census, he and Mamie are listed as hav
2 CONC ing been married for two years, which would make their marriage about 1
2 CONC 908.  It seems to indicate that it was Byrd's first marriage (and Mamie
2 CONC 's third), in which case the two censuses are inconsistent.  Since the A
2 CONC dair Wilson family record (Byrd's older brother) did not list a wife fo
2 CONC r Byrd, I'm inclined to think that Byrd was not yet married in 1902, wh
2 CONC en it was written.  For this reason, I am tentatively going with marria
2 CONC ge about 1908.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1870 census
2 CONT Listed as Bird L. Willson, age 10, born in Missouri, living with brothe
2 CONC r B.H. Willson and family.  Attended school within the year.
