1 NAME William /Wilson/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Wilson
1 SEX M
1 _UID 697E38605F05FF4F9A7422CCAB4E64F07A5C
1 CHAN
2 DATE 18 AUG 2018
1 NOTE From the Hotchkiss manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wilson family came to Missouri from Staunton, Augusta County, Virgi
2 CONC nia.  William was the youngest of eight children of Robert Wilson, who p
2 CONC robably came to Virginia from Pennsylvania.  Robert bought 461 acres of f
2 CONC arm land in Augusta County in 1755 from his brother, who had acquired i
2 CONC t ten years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT When you are the last of eight children, with five brothers ahead of yo
2 CONC u, the likelihood of inheriting land from your father is usually poor.  T
2 CONC hat society usually practiced a variation of primogeniture, leaving mos
2 CONC t of the land to the oldest son.  This was a necessary practice to keep t
2 CONC he land from being divided into small, uneconomical pieces.  There is n
2 CONC o concrete evidence, but one source says that William was a lawyer in V
2 CONC irginia before he came to Missouri.  It would be logical for him to try t
2 CONC o learn a profession rather than wait hopelessly for a farm of his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Wilson died in 1768 and left the property to two of William's ol
2 CONC der brothers.  Good things come to him who waits.  William waited 48 ye
2 CONC ars until 1816.  In the meantime brother Robert had one child who prede
2 CONC ceased him.  Brother David never married.  The entire property was will
2 CONC ed by David in 1816 to William.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time William was probably well past the age of 60.  His oldest b
2 CONC oy was 26, and the youngest was 9.  It was a little late to start a suc
2 CONC cessful career in farming.  We don't know what the condition of the pro
2 CONC perty was when William inherited it, but we do know from the county rec
2 CONC ords that he sold 200 acres in 1817.  The record of deeds of trust show t
2 CONC hat William and his sons placed nine separate deeds of trust on the pro
2 CONC perty between 1818 and 1821.  What was left of the equity interest was s
2 CONC old in 1821.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is quite reasonable that William wanted a new start in 1820.  Many s
2 CONC ettlers had already moved from Virginia to Saline County, Missouri.  Mr
2 CONC s. Dabney says that David did not come until 1830, but William and his w
2 CONC ife and the other three boys all left Virginia in 1820 or shortly there
2 CONC after.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that William moved to Howard County, Missouri, not Saline County (
2 CONC some of the family moved to Saline County later).  Also, he did not mov
2 CONC e to Missouri until 1824 or '25, though two of his sons did go earlier, a
2 CONC bout 1820.  The Adair Wilson family record says William moved from Virg
2 CONC inia to Missouri in 1824.  "History of Saline County" (in the biography o
2 CONC f William Adair Wilson) says 1825.  Daughter Nancy was married in Augus
2 CONC ta County in September 1824, and her marriage bond was signed by Willia
2 CONC m and son William A., showing that they were in Virginia at that time, s
2 CONC o I think 1825 is probably more likely.  The financial problems discuss
2 CONC ed below may explain why all of the Wilsons decided to move west to Mis
2 CONC souri, to get a fresh start.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The line in the Hotchkiss manuscript that William might have been a law
2 CONC yer in Augusta County is based on the following, which is from a summar
2 CONC y of a deed in Augusta County Deed Book 40, p. 362:
2 CONT
2 CONT 27 January 1816 - Whereas I, Edward Askins OF THE COUNTY OF PERRY IN TH
2 CONC E INDIANA TERRITORY (Note: became a State this same year) - have sold t
2 CONC o John Buchanan of Augusta County, State of Virginia, a tract of land c
2 CONC ontaining by survey 127 acres, he is to have it - which land is situate
2 CONC d in Augusta County, Virginia on the Waters of Christians Creek and was d
2 CONC evised to me by Will of John Askins, dec'd.  I authorize William Wilson S
2 CONC enior of said County to be my attorney in fact to convey said land...
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ("They Went Thataway" by Charles Hughes Hamlin, Genealogical Publishing C
2 CONC o., Baltimore, 1985, vol. 1, p. 7.)  This one mention is not much to go b
2 CONC y to say that William was a lawyer, but it might be significant that Wi
2 CONC lliam was typically listed as William Willson Sr. in the Augusta County t
2 CONC ax rolls.  On the other hand, perhaps this is the William referred to a
2 CONC s William Willson Esq. in the tax rolls.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Portrait and Biographical Record of Lafayette and Saline Counties, Mis
2 CONC souri", in the biography of Robert H. Wilson, says that his grandfather (
2 CONC who is never named) was a half-brother of James Wilson, the signer of t
2 CONC he Declaration of Independence.  I think they were confused about this; I d
2 CONC on't see any way it could be right -- but see the notes for William's f
2 CONC ather, Robert.  This biography plus a biography of Adair Wilson in "Por
2 CONC trait and Biographical Record of Denver and Vicinity, Colorado" also sa
2 CONC y that William served in the Revolutionary War.  In addition, one of th
2 CONC e entries for John Wilson in "California Pioneer and Immigrant Files, 1
2 CONC 790-1950" on Ancestry.com has a note "War of Revolution" next to Willia
2 CONC m Wilson (John's father).  A letter (obtained from William and Deborah V
2 CONC aughan) by Ben H. Wilson (another grandson of William) says that "Durin
2 CONC g the early wars Grandfather was in the Army and was ordered from Virgi
2 CONC nia to South Carolina and while there he married.  He was, so history s
2 CONC ays, about the only man of his company that succeeded in getting back t
2 CONC o Virginia, a majority of them having been killed or died from the hard
2 CONC ship of the campaign."  Unfortunately, there is very little documentati
2 CONC on available to confirm that William fought in the Revolution.  The bes
2 CONC t that has been found so far is in a book called "Historical Register o
2 CONC f Virginians in the Revolution" by John H. Gwathmey (Genealogical Publi
2 CONC shing Co., Baltimore, 1979; available on Ancestry.com).  There is an en
2 CONC try on p. 835 for a “Willson, William (son of Robert)” from Augusta Cou
2 CONC nty who served in Capt. Long’s Company.  Possibly there was another Wil
2 CONC liam Wilson/Willson in that time and place who also had a father named R
2 CONC obert, but there seems to be a good chance that this is our William.  T
2 CONC his also brings up that many of the old records from Augusta County for W
2 CONC illiam and his sons have Wilson spelled with a double-l.  It’s not very c
2 CONC onsistent, with sometimes single-l Wilson and double-l Willson in the s
2 CONC ame document.  It seems to have settled down to Wilson sometime in the 1
2 CONC 820s or 1830s, though I know of census records as late as 1870 with the W
2 CONC illson spelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was actually more than one Captain Long from Augusta County in th
2 CONC e Revolutionary War, but the one that seems most likely referred to her
2 CONC e is Francis Long.  The discussion of the Revolution in "Annals of Augu
2 CONC sta County, Virginia, from 1726 to 1871" by Joseph A. Waddell (Second E
2 CONC dition, C. Russell Caldwell, Staunton, VA, 1902; obtained from http://w
2 CONC ww.archive.org/details/annalsofaugustac00wadd) has two main mentions of S
2 CONC outh Carolina:  Several soldiers from Augusta County are noted to have b
2 CONC een taken prisoner at Charleston on 12 May 1780, and units from Augusta C
2 CONC ounty were involved in the battle of Cowpens in South Carolina on 17 Ja
2 CONC n 1781.  There is no indication that Capt. Long’s company was involved i
2 CONC n those actions, but then all the information is very fragmentary.  It i
2 CONC s also possible that William was in Capt. Long’s company part of the ti
2 CONC me and in another company part of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It is clear from his father's will that William was under 21 at the tim
2 CONC e the will was written (9 Sep 1768).  That means he was born after Sept
2 CONC ember 1747.  In information from Katherine Bushman that James Hotchkiss p
2 CONC assed on to me, in one place William is referred to as an infant, meani
2 CONC ng under 14, at the time of his father's death.  That would mean born a
2 CONC fter September 1754.  A different entry for John Wilson in "California P
2 CONC ioneer and Immigrant Files, 1790-1950" on Ancestry.com shows his father a
2 CONC s William Wilson (1750-1823).  I don't think that 1823 could be right f
2 CONC or his death because of the accounts of him moving to Missouri in 1824 o
2 CONC r 1825, and his signature on daughter Nancy's marriage bond in 1824; 17
2 CONC 50 is possible for his birth, but doesn’t have much credibility since t
2 CONC he death year is probably wrong.  An issue of the Fayette, MO, Intellig
2 CONC encer newspaper dated 9 Nov 1827 contained the following death notice (
2 CONC obtained from the State Historical Society of Missouri, Missouri Digita
2 CONC l Newspaper Project; http://shs.umsystem.edu/newspaper/mdnp/index.shtml
2 CONC ):  "In this county, on the 14th ult. after a short illness, Mr. Willia
2 CONC m Wilson, aged about 71 years."  I have no way of knowing if this is th
2 CONC e right William Wilson, but it fits with what I know.  It would make hi
2 CONC s birth in about 1756.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Following is my transcription of a deed in Augusta County Deed Book 41, p
2 CONC p. 294-295.  I have added some punctuation to try to make it more reada
2 CONC ble, but it's hard for me to make sense of the language in parts of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Indenture, made the 23rd day of June in this year of our Lord one t
2 CONC housand eight hundred and seventeen Between William Willson and Catha[r
2 CONC ]ine his wife of the County of Augusta and State of Virginia of the one p
2 CONC art and William Thompson of the County and State aforesaid of the other p
2 CONC art, Witnesseth that the said William Willson and Catharine his wife fo
2 CONC r and in consideration of the sum of three thousand dollars lawful mone
2 CONC y of the State aforesaid, to them in hand paid by the said William Thom
2 CONC pson, the receipt whereof is hereby acknowledged, traded [?] granted Ba
2 CONC rgained and sold and by these presents do grant bargain and sell unto t
2 CONC he said Wm. Thompson his heirs and assigns a certain tract or parcel of l
2 CONC and lying and being in the County of Augusta, on the head waters of the S
2 CONC outh river, containing by estimation two hundred acres be the same more o
2 CONC r less, being a part of the same tract of land conveyed by Samuel Wills
2 CONC on to Robert Willson by deed bearing date the 2nd day of June 1755 and d
2 CONC evised by the said Robert Willson to his sons Robert and David Willson b
2 CONC y his last will and testament bearing date the 9th day of Sept. 1768, a m
2 CONC oiety whereof fell to the sd. David by the death of the said Robert wit
2 CONC hout issue, he the said David being his heir at Law, whereby the said D
2 CONC avid became possessed of the legal title to the whole tract claimed as a
2 CONC foresaid, and which was afterwards devised by the said David Willson to t
2 CONC he above named William Willson by his last will and testament bearing d
2 CONC ate the 25th day of January 1804, which said deeds and wills are duly a
2 CONC dmitted to records in the County Court office of Augusta, and which sai
2 CONC d two hundred acres of land is bounded as follows, to wit:  Beginning a
2 CONC t a white oak corner to David H... [?] and running thence with his line S
2 CONC outh twenty one and a third degrees West two hundred and Eleven poles t
2 CONC o a Locust in Robert Tate’s line, thence with his line and a line of Jo
2 CONC hn Tate’s heirs South twenty one and two third degrees East one hundred a
2 CONC nd fifty two poles to a chesnut and hickory in the line of Tate’s heirs
2 CONC , thence a new line North twenty one and a third degrees East Two hundr
2 CONC ed and Eleven poles to a small hickory and sassafras, near a walnut poi
2 CONC nter in Doak’s line, thence with the said Doak’s and David Strain’s lin
2 CONC e North Twenty one and two third degrees west one hundred and fifty two p
2 CONC oles to the Beginning.  Together with all and singular the appurtenance
2 CONC s thereunto Belonging or in any wise appertaining.  To have and to hold .
2 CONC .. said tract or parcel of Land with its appurtenances unto the said Wi
2 CONC lliam Thompson, his heirs and assigns to the only proper use and behoof o
2 CONC f him, the said William Thompson, his heirs and assigns forever.  And t
2 CONC he said William Willson and wife for themselves, their heirs Execrs. an
2 CONC d Adms. do covenant with the said Wm. Thompson, his heirs and assigns t
2 CONC hat they, the said Wm. Willson and wife, their heirs Execrs. & Adms. th
2 CONC e said two hundred acres of land with its appurtenances unto the said W
2 CONC illiam Thompson, his heirs and assigns against the claim or claims of t
2 CONC hem, the said William Willson and wife and their heirs, and of all and a
2 CONC ny person or persons whatsoever, shall and will warrant and forever def
2 CONC end by these presents.  In testimony whereof the said Wm. Willson and w
2 CONC ife have hereunto set their hands and seals the day and year first abov
2 CONC e written.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wm. Willson
2 CONT Catharine Wilson
2 CONT
2 CONT Augusta County, to wit [?]
2 CONT
2 CONT We Robert Doak and Joseph Brown Justices of the peace in the County afo
2 CONC resaid and State of Virginia do hereby certify that Catharine Willson t
2 CONC he wife of William Willson parties to the foregoing deed for the convey
2 CONC ance of real Estate to William Thompson, bearing date the 23 day of Jun
2 CONC e 1817, personally appeared before us in our County aforesaid and being e
2 CONC xamined by us privily and apart from her husband, and having the deed a
2 CONC foresaid fully explained to her, she the said Catharine Willson acknowl
2 CONC edged the same to be her act and deed & declared that she had willingly s
