2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to information from Katherine Bushman that James Hotchkiss pa
2 CONC ssed on to me, the deed of 2 Jun 1755 referred to above was from Robert
2 CONC ’s brother Samuel to Robert.  It is in Deed Book 7, pp. 165-166 (3 Jun 1
2 CONC 755).  Samuel bought it from George and Robert Breckenridge on 8 Feb 17
2 CONC 45 (Deed Book 1, p. 12).  David Wilson's will, in which he left the lan
2 CONC d to William, is in Will Book 12, pp. 116-117 (25 Jan 1804, proved 22 J
2 CONC an 1816).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The angles in the description of the tract of land make no sense to me.  I t
2 CONC hought that "South twenty one and a third degrees West" would mean 21 1
2 CONC /3 degrees west of south, but if all the angles are interpreted that wa
2 CONC y, the tract is a diamond shape that will not fit within the George & R
2 CONC obert Breckenridge land in the 1954 map of the Beverley Patent by J.R. H
2 CONC ildebrand.  (The Beverley Patent, or Beverley Manor, was a large tract o
2 CONC f land in the heart of the Shenandoah Valley obtained by William Beverl
2 CONC ey in 1736.)  My second thought was to interpret it as 21 1/3 degrees s
2 CONC outh of west, but doing that consistently still results in an odd diamo
2 CONC nd shape, and if a pole is supposed to be the same as a rod (16.5 feet)
2 CONC , then the area would only be about 137 acres.  It appears to me that t
2 CONC he tract is supposed to be a rectangle (or nearly a rectangle) with sid
2 CONC es of 211 rods and 152 rods (which gives an area close to 200 acres), t
2 CONC ilted at an angle of 21 1/3 degrees toward the east.  Such a shape and o
2 CONC rientation would fit nicely within the George & Robert Breckenridge lan
2 CONC d in Hildebrand's map.  My initial thought was that the tract being cal
2 CONC led out is at the western end of the George & Robert Breckenridge land b
2 CONC ecause the description of the boundary ends with a mention of “David St
2 CONC rain’s line” and there is a tract labelled D STRAIN at the northwest co
2 CONC rner of the George & Robert Breckenridge land on the map.  However, thi
2 CONC s is difficult to reconcile with the description of the whole 461 acres i
2 CONC n the deed for Samuel Wilson’s purchase of the land from George & Rober
2 CONC t Breckenridge.  See the notes for Samuel Wilson.  That deed says that R
2 CONC obert Breckenridge had the northwest end of the original 761-acre tract b
2 CONC ut Samuel’s purchase was of George Breckenridge’s portion of the land.  
2 CONC Perhaps David Strain had moved or purchased additional land by 1817.  T
2 CONC here is a figure and additional discussion in the WilsonNotes document.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, William ran into financial difficulties.  There is refer
2 CONC ence to this in a biography of son Robert Wilson in "Reminiscences of t
2 CONC he Bench and Bar of Missouri" by W.V.N. Bay (F. H. Thomas and Company, S
2 CONC t. Louis, 1878; obtained from Google Books).  On p. 561, it says “Rober
2 CONC t received a fair English education, and it was the intention of his fa
2 CONC ther to give him a collegiate course, but, becoming financially embarra
2 CONC ssed, he was forced to take his son from school.”  The biography also s
2 CONC ays that Robert moved to Missouri in the spring of 1820, which was abou
2 CONC t the time these financial difficulties were coming to a head.  The fin
2 CONC ancial problems are documented in two Augusta County chancery-court cas
2 CONC es involving William, Haupe v. Wilson and others (http://www.lva.virgin
2 CONC ia.gov/chancery/case_detail.asp?CFN=015-1821-064) and Little & Telford v
2 CONC . Wilson and others plus Brown v. Little & Telford and others (http://w
2 CONC ww.lva.virginia.gov/chancery/case_detail.asp?CFN=015-1832-026).  These f
2 CONC inancial difficulties are probably why William sold part of his land in 1
2 CONC 817, but the problems continued to get worse after that.
2 CONT
2 CONT The information available in the court papers and various deeds of trus
2 CONC t seems to show the debts arising from William’s brother David and Will
2 CONC iam’s sons John and David rather than from William himself.  The compla
2 CONC int by Henry Haupe, filed on 9 Sep 1820, stated that William’s brother D
2 CONC avid, with William as security, had borrowed $676.70 from Haupe on 25 D
2 CONC ec 1813.  At the time of David’s death, he had not paid the money back, a
2 CONC nd Haupe wanted the remaining 260 acres sold, if necessary, to pay him b
2 CONC ack, with interest.  Sons John and David borrowed a considerable amount o
2 CONC f money from several people (see also the notes for John and David), an
2 CONC d some of the loans were secured by William’s land and personal propert
2 CONC y, all the way down to feather beds and kitchen furniture.  Haupe’s com
2 CONC plaint stated that there were already three liens on William’s land: a d
2 CONC eed of trust to Samuel Clarke dated 29 Jul 1819 to secure a debt to Jam
2 CONC es Moffett (the deed of trust shows that this was a debt of William’s s
2 CONC ons John & David along with William’s brother David), a deed of trust t
2 CONC o Michael Garber Jr. dated 10 Aug 1819 to secure a payment due from son
2 CONC s John & David to Jeremiah & James Kyle of Richmond, and a deed of trus
2 CONC t to William Clarke dated 1 Oct 1819 to secure a payment due from John & D
2 CONC avid to Adam & Samuel Grove (note that Samuel Grove was William’s son-i
2 CONC n-law, having married William’s daughter Mary on 16 Nov 1818).  What’s w
2 CONC orse is, this wasn’t even a complete list of liens on William’s land, a
2 CONC s a deed of trust to Preston Trotter dated 9 Oct 1819 to secure a debt b
2 CONC y John & David to Little & Telford also listed William’s 261 acres as s
2 CONC ecurity.  The 261 acres were apparently sold at auction by Michael Garb
2 CONC er Jr. to Henry J. Tapp and John Garber for $1,600 (Augusta County Deed B
2 CONC ook 45, pp. 385–387, dated 20 Nov 1820), who later sold it to John K. M
2 CONC oore (Augusta County Deed Book 47, pp. 72–73, dated 14 Nov 1822).  Ther
2 CONC e are more details on all of this in the WilsonAugustaCounty document.  A b
2 CONC ig factor in the travails of John, David, and William is that property v
2 CONC alues crashed.  This is probably reflected in the fact that William sol
2 CONC d 200 acres of his land in 1817 for $3,000, but then the remaining 261 a
2 CONC cres sold for only $1,600 in 1820.  This crash in property values was l
2 CONC ikely because of the Panic of 1819, which was “arguably this country’s f
2 CONC irst modern business cycle” and “the first truly national depression Am
2 CONC erica experienced” (p. 1 of Haulman [see below]).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a lot of information about the Panic, in particular as it affe
2 CONC cted Virginia, in "Virginia and the Panic of 1819: The First Great Depr
2 CONC ession and the Commonwealth" by Clyde A. Haulman (Pickering & Chatto, L
2 CONC ondon, 2008).  On p. 25 Haulman states:
2 CONT
2 CONT Contemporary observers indicate that the Panic of 1819 was a traumatic e
2 CONC xperience for the new republic.  For example, John C. Calhoun, discussi
2 CONC ng the situation with John Quincy Adams in 1820, said, “There has been w
2 CONC ithin these two years an immense revolution of fortunes in every part o
2 CONC f the Union: enormous numbers of persons utterly ruined; multitudes in d
2 CONC eep distress; and a general mass disaffection to the government.”
2 CONT …
2 CONT The young nation’s “traumatic awakening to the capitalist reality of bo
2 CONC om-and-bust” reported by these various sources was a complex combinatio
2 CONC n of financial market volatility, swings in international market demand
2 CONC , and federal government financial activity.  The actions of the Second B
2 CONC ank of the United States along with those of a number of state-chartere
2 CONC d banks have received much scholarly attention.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Of most relevance here is this from p. 63:
2 CONT  
2 CONT Table 4.3 shows the value of real property as assessed in the City of R
2 CONC ichmond for the years 1817–25.  Here the impact of the Panic of 1819 on p
2 CONC roperty values is clearly evident as assessments fell 49.1 per cent bet
2 CONC ween 1818 and 1819 and decreased again by 50.1 per cent between 1819 an
2 CONC d 1820 for a total decline of 74.6 percent in value of assessed propert
2 CONC y in the years between the peak in 1818 and the trough in 1820.  By 182
2 CONC 1 assessments had risen slightly, up 1.4 per cent, and in the following y
2 CONC ear the value of property continued to rise, up an additional 51.2 per c
2 CONC ent.  Thus, by 1822 assessed property values in Richmond had recovered s
2 CONC omewhat from the trough reached in 1820, but assessments, despite their u
2 CONC pward movement, still remained 61.1 per cent below their pre-Panic peak o
2 CONC f almost $16 million in 1818.  From this point until 1825, assessed rea
2 CONC l property values changed very little.
2 CONT
2 CONT  
2 CONT Note, however, that the timing of the real-estate crash could have been s
2 CONC omewhat later over in the Shenandoah Valley.  On p. 60, Haulman shows t
2 CONC hat a decline in commodity prices occurred somewhat later in western Vi
2 CONC rginia than in Richmond.  John and David Wilson made their ill-fated la
2 CONC nd purchases in October 1818 and March 1819, possibly before land price
2 CONC s started to drop in Augusta County.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It is perhaps worth noting that there were six William Wilsons in the A
2 CONC ugusta County land tax books during this time period.  With occasional e
2 CONC xceptions, the others were listed as Rev. William Wilson, Maj. William W
2 CONC illson, William Willson Esq., Capt. William Willson (noted once to be a s
2 CONC on of the major), and William Wilson who was a resident of Rockbridge C
2 CONC ounty.  (In addition to these designations, the different Williams can b
2 CONC e distinguished by their amount of land and its location.)  Starting in 1
2 CONC 818, our William was listed as William Willson Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There were three William Wilsons in the 1820 census for Augusta County, V
2 CONC A (the land tax records for Augusta County show six William Wilsons dur
2 CONC ing this time period).  Two of the census listings were in the area of W
2 CONC aynesboro (Maj. William Wilson and Rev. William Wilson); the third was i
2 CONC n the area of Greenville, with a listing of William Wilson (S. River). 
2 CONC The deeds dated 1 Oct 1819 and 20 Nov 1820 that were referred to previo
2 CONC usly both stated that William was living on the 260/261-acre tract of l
2 CONC and.  Deeds and tax records described the 260/261 acres as being on the h
2 CONC ead waters of the South River, which fits nicely with the census listin
2 CONC g near Greenville.  That listing is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 census
2 CONT Image 1 on Ancestry.com (Saunton [Staunton], Augusta Co., VA)
2 CONT Listed as William Wilson (S. River).  In the margin of this section, it s
2 CONC ays Greenville Augusta.  Household consisted of four males (ages 45+, 2
2 CONC 6-44, 10-15, under 10) and 6 females (two aged 45+, one 16-25, two aged 1
2 CONC 0-15, and one under 10).  Presumably the male aged 45+ was William and t
2 CONC he male aged 10-15 was William Adair.  John and David were already marr
2 CONC ied by this time; the male aged 26-44 might have been Robert, but if so h
2 CONC is age was marked wrong.  I don't know who the under-10 male was.  Pres
2 CONC umably one of the females aged 45+ was Catharine and the female aged 16
2 CONC -25 was Nancy.  Polly was already married by this time.  I don't know w
2 CONC ho the other four females would have been (ages 45+, 10-15, 10-15, unde
2 CONC r 10).  Also, one slave was listed, a male aged 14-25.
