1 NOTE I don't know much about her.  The Adair Wilson family record lists Cath
2 CONC arine Yancey as William Wilson's wife.  There are three Augusta County d
2 CONC eeds that show William's wife's name as Catharine or Catherine.  A coup
2 CONC le of listings for John Wilson in the "California Pioneer and Immigrant F
2 CONC iles, 1790-1950" on Ancestry.com show his mother's name as Yancey.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hotchkiss speculated that her father might have been Robert Yance
2 CONC y.  My impression is that the basis for this identification was the fac
2 CONC t that Robert Wilson (son of William and Catharine) probated a Robert Y
2 CONC ancey who died intestate in Howard Co., MO.  However, "Randolph County, M
2 CONC issouri Administration and Will Records 1836-1858" on Ancestry.com list
2 CONC s three other people who died intestate and were administered by Robert W
2 CONC ilson, so it may be a mistake to read too much into the connection with R
2 CONC obert Yancey.  A Yancey researcher, Dennis Yancey, thinks that this Rob
2 CONC ert is unlikely to be Catharine's father.  He knew of one Yancey family i
2 CONC n Augusta Co., VA (http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=G
2 CONC ET&db=dyancey&id=I13787; unfortunately, Dennis seems to have renumbered h
2 CONC is database, so this link no longer goes to the right person), but ther
2 CONC e is no information about them having a daughter named Catharine (also, n
2 CONC ote that Adair and Ben Wilson said she was from South Carolina).
2 CONT
2 CONT In his family record, Adair Wilson said that Catharine Yancey was born i
2 CONC n South Carolina and she and William were married in Charleston, South C
2 CONC arolina.  A couple of biographies of Robert Wilson (son of William and C
2 CONC atharine) have some additional clues about her.  First, on p. 561 of <i
2 CONC >Reminiscences of the Bench and Bar of Missouri</i>  by W.V.N. Bay (F. H
2 CONC . Thomas and Company, St. Louis, 1878; obtained from Google Books), it s
2 CONC ays “His father was a very respectable farmer of English descent, and h
2 CONC is mother was a Yancey, and belonged to the noted family of that name i
2 CONC n the Old Dominion.”  Old Dominion refers to Virginia, which seems to b
2 CONC e in contradiction to Adair's entry, though perhaps the family lived in V
2 CONC irginia before moving to South Carolina.  Second, in an obituary of Rob
2 CONC ert in the New York Times on 18 May 1870, it says “His mother was a rel
2 CONC ative of the late Hon. WILLIAM L. YANCEY, of Alabama.”  Unfortunately, “
2 CONC relative of” is too vague to clear things up very much.  However, Benja
2 CONC min Harrison Wilson (grandson of William and Catharine, Adair's older b
2 CONC rother) was a little more specific in a letter written around 1895 (obt
2 CONC ained from William and Deborah Vaughan).  He said, “Grandmother Wilson w
2 CONC as a Miss Yancey and an Aunt of Wm. L. Yancey of South Carolina – and s
2 CONC he was a native of that State.  During the early wars Grandfather was i
2 CONC n the Army and was ordered from Virginia to South Carolina and while th
2 CONC ere he married.”  This letter helps a lot by providing a reason why Wil
2 CONC liam happened to be married in South Carolina.  The ages aren't right f
2 CONC or Catharine Yancey to be William L. Yancey's aunt, but possibly a grea
2 CONC t aunt?  The big problem with Ben’s story, though, is the long gap betw
2 CONC een the end of the war and the birth of William’s oldest child John in 1
2 CONC 790.  It is possible that there were some older children who died young o
2 CONC r are not known for some reason.  Other possibilities are that he met C
2 CONC atharine during the war, but went back and married her several years la
2 CONC ter or that the trip or expedition to South Carolina wasn’t part of the R
2 CONC evolutionary War, but several years afterward.  I hope to follow up on t
2 CONC hese clues and find out more about her.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hotchkiss's contribution to the Ancestral File said that Catharin
2 CONC e was born before 1757.  I have no idea what basis there was for this, b
2 CONC ut it seems a bit early considering that the oldest child (John) was bo
2 CONC rn in 1790.  That would have made her 33 or older when John was born an
2 CONC d 50 or older when William was born.  Not impossible, but a bit on the h
2 CONC igh side.  It's hard to be sure of anything with the older censuses, bu
2 CONC t in Randolph Co., MO, in the 1830 census there is a listing for Robert W
2 CONC ilson with a female age 60-69 in the household.  It looks right for Cat
2 CONC harine living with her son after her husband had passed away.  This age r
2 CONC ange would make her born in 1761-1770.  In turn, that would make her 20
2 CONC -29 when John was born and 37-46 when William was born.  These are more r
2 CONC easonable ages.
