1 NOTE John Wilson was born in Augusta Co., VA, in 1790.  I know little about h
2 CONC is early life, but I think he was married to Priscilla McCoun in Mercer C
2 CONC o., KY, in 1812, served in the War of 1812, and then married Martha Woo
2 CONC ds in Augusta County in 1817.  Presumably his first wife had passed awa
2 CONC y.  And Martha seems not to have lived very long either.  John moved to H
2 CONC oward Co., MO, about 1820, and married his third wife, Ann Pulliam, in 1
2 CONC 825.  He was a lawyer and was active in the Whig party, which was a dis
2 CONC tinct minority in Missouri.  After Zachary Taylor (Whig) became preside
2 CONC nt in 1849, and with the California gold rush going on, John decided to m
2 CONC ove there.  (By the way, I have seen it suggested that John was related t
2 CONC o Zachary Taylor, but do not know if it's true, or what the relationshi
2 CONC p might have been.)  He obtained appointments as Indian agent for Salt L
2 CONC ake and navy agent at San Francisco.  The trip to California was quite e
2 CONC ventful, and included much difficulty getting through the mountains of n
2 CONC orthern California in late fall / early winter.  He and his family had t
2 CONC o abandon most of their belongings there.  He did not arrive in San Fra
2 CONC ncisco until 8 Dec 1849.  He became a prominent lawyer in San Francisco a
2 CONC nd engaged extensively in land speculation and to some extent in politi
2 CONC cs.  He died in 1877 at the age of 87.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was known as General John Wilson, though he wasn't really a general.  F
2 CONC red Rogers, in end note 171 of the third reference below, says that the
2 CONC re was a John Wilson who had short service as inspector general with a f
2 CONC ew companies of Missouri militia during the Black Hawk war, and possibl
2 CONC y that accounts for his being called "General" -- but he didn't know if t
2 CONC his was the same John Wilson.  However, John's War of 1812 pension pape
2 CONC rs from the National Archives contain a page with the following stateme
2 CONC nt: "John Wilson ... was the Divission Inspector General of the 1st Div
2 CONC ision of Missouri Militia a part of which were called into the service o
2 CONC f the United States in the summer of 1832 ... to defend against the exp
2 CONC ected attacks of the Indians under Black Hawk".  This confirms that our J
2 CONC ohn Wilson was an inspector general, though it's still speculation that t
2 CONC his is how he came to be known as General John Wilson.  (There is also a l
2 CONC etter to Abiel Leonard in the Abiel Leonard Papers, dated 20 Apr 1830 -
2 CONC - or possibly 1832 -- that is signed "John Wilson, Division Inspector o
2 CONC f 1st Div, Missouri Militia".)  The pension papers also contain a physi
2 CONC cal description:  He was 5 ft. 11 in. tall, and had blue eyes.  (The Ho
2 CONC tchkiss manuscript says he was 6' 3"; it doesn't say where that informa
2 CONC tion is from.)  At the time of his enlistment in 1814 he was a merchant
2 CONC .  His official dates of service in the War of 1812 were from 29 Aug 18
2 CONC 14 to 10 Dec 1814 (104 days).  He was in the Virginia Militia in the co
2 CONC mpany of Capt. Samuel Doak and the regiment of Col. James McDowell, and a
2 CONC ttained the rank of orderly sergeant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources say he was born in Tennessee, but I don't know why.  I don
2 CONC 't have any other information indicating that the family spent any time i
2 CONC n Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are several Augusta County deeds of trust involving debts of John a
2 CONC nd his brother David to various people in the years from 1815 to 1820.  T
2 CONC heir debts also figured in two Augusta County chancery-court cases, Hau
2 CONC pe v. Wilson and others (http://www.lva.virginia.gov/chancery/case_deta
2 CONC il.asp?CFN=015-1821-064) and Little & Telford v. Wilson and others plus B
2 CONC rown v. Little & Telford and others (http://www.lva.virginia.gov/chance
2 CONC ry/case_detail.asp?CFN=015-1832-026).  At least some of the borrowed mo
2 CONC ney was used to buy property, presumably for investment.  In particular
2 CONC , in 1819 John and David bought property in and around the town of Wayn
2 CONC esborough, including a tavern, from William Brown for $3,250, which the
2 CONC y were to pay in installments up through February 1827.  However, Brown
2 CONC ’s court complaint in 1826 said that shortly after he sold the land to t
2 CONC hem the Wilsons became insolvent and abandoned the property, and they h
2 CONC ad not made any of the payments.  A deed of trust dated 25 Apr 1820 fro
2 CONC m John and David to sheriff James McNutt mentions that they had been tr
2 CONC ading under the firm of John & David Wilson.  That deed of trust appear
2 CONC s to be them basically declaring bankruptcy and forfeiting loan collate
2 CONC ral.  There are affidavits along with Brown’s complaint stating that th
2 CONC e land Brown sold the Wilsons would probably not sell for more than $2,
2 CONC 000 in 1826, if even that much.  So John and David (and father William, b
2 CONC ecause he had secured some of their loans) were stuck and couldn’t make t
2 CONC he payments even by selling the property.  This drop in land value was l
2 CONC ikely caused by the Panic of 1819; see the notes for William Wilson.  T
2 CONC he court papers show that the court appointed a commissioner to oversee J
2 CONC ohn’s and David’s property starting in 1821.  Presumably, the property f
2 CONC inally reverted to Brown in 1827.  Note that John probably moved to Mis
2 CONC souri in 1820 or 1821, but David remained in Virginia until 1830.  Ther
2 CONC e are more details on all of this in the WilsonAugustaCounty document.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John continued with land speculation throughout his life.  There is qui
2 CONC te a bit of discussion of this in the Hotchkiss manuscript, including h
2 CONC ow he always seemed to be short of money.  He was involved in some very l
2 CONC arge deals, including the Valliere claim, which is discussed below.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are transcriptions of a few obituaries in the John Wilson Papers (
2 CONC BANC MSS C-B 420, The Bancroft Library, University of California, Berke
2 CONC ley, CA), but none with much additional information.  Indeed, a report o
2 CONC f an organization called Sons of Revolutionary Sires dated 7 Feb 1877 s
2 CONC ays (in part):
2 CONT
2 CONT For several months we have tried to get his record for publication in o
2 CONC ur first Historic volume, but the principle of putting off till to-morr
2 CONC ow what could be done to-day, has deprived us of the valuable incidents o
2 CONC f his long and eventful life, unless he has left some written informati
2 CONC on among his papers.  It is to be hoped this will be a warning to those w
2 CONC hose duty it is to furnish the material for labors of the council, as w
2 CONC e have undertaken to publish the biography of each member, with an appr
2 CONC oved genealogy of his family and interesting traditional incidents that s
2 CONC o generally mark the character of Revolutionary Patriots.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sure would have been nice to have that material!  One of the obituaries s
2 CONC ays that he was a protege of Thomas Jefferson when he was in Virginia.  I
2 CONC t would be nice to know if there's anything to that.  That obituary is a
2 CONC lso one of the places where it says that he was a relative of President T
2 CONC aylor.  Another obituary just says that he was "an intimate friend of P
2 CONC resident Taylor" and also that "He was on terms of great intimacy with P
2 CONC resident Tyler, though on the refusal of the latter to sign the Bank Bi
2 CONC ll a rupture took place, which was never healed."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A lengthy letter from John to family back in Missouri, written a few mo
2 CONC nths after he arrived in San Francisco, is reproduced in:
2 CONT
2 CONT "California Letter of John Wilson, 1850" edited by Frederic A. Culmer (
2 CONC Missouri Historical Review, Vol. 24, No. 2, pp. 200-213, January 1930).  A
2 CONC vailable from the web site of the State Historical Society of Missouri, h
2 CONC ttp://statehistoricalsocietyofmissouri.org/ .
2 CONT
2 CONT The letter is a very interesting portrayal of life in the early days of S
2 CONC an Francisco, plus a little bit about his family's difficulties getting t
2 CONC hrough the northern California mountains in late 1849.  More about John (
2 CONC and some about his family) is in three articles in the California Histo
2 CONC rical Society Quarterly:
2 CONT
2 CONT "'General' John Wilson, Signer of the Deseret Petition: Including Lette
2 CONC rs from the Leonard Collection" by Frederic A. Culmer (California Histo
2 CONC rical Society Quarterly, Vol. 26, No. 4, pp. 321-348, December 1947).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of Henry L. Ford (1822-1860), wit
2 CONC h Some Related and Contemporary Art of Alexander Edouart (Continued)" b
2 CONC y Fred B. Rogers (California Historical Society Quarterly, Vol. 30, No. 1
2 CONC , pp. 49-66, March 1951).
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of Henry L. Ford (1822-1860), wit
2 CONC h Some Related and Contemporary Art of Alexander Edouart (Concluded)" b
2 CONC y Fred B. Rogers (California Historical Society Quarterly, Vol. 30, No. 2
2 CONC , pp. 157-175, June 1951).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also quite a bit about John in the Hotchkiss manuscript.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John's will, obtained from the Howard County Genealogical Society (some p
2 CONC unctuation added):
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen: I, Genl. John Wilson of the City and County of S
2 CONC an Francisco, State of California, being weak in body but of sound and d
2 CONC isposing [?] mind do make publish and declare this my last will and tes
2 CONC tament.
2 CONT
2 CONT I will state first the following fact.  My wife Mrs. Ann R. Wilson owne
2 CONC s all my property in common with me, wheresoever the same may be, and o
2 CONC ut of which I desire that our common debts are first to be paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1st.  I give and bequeath to my beloved wife Ann R. Wilson during her n
2 CONC atural life the use of all the property or proceeds thereof we own in t
2 CONC he State of California or Oregon, and at her death what property I have i
2 CONC n California I leave one half to my Daughter Mary Eliza Wakeman and one h
2 CONC alf to my Son Robert Wilson as tenants in common.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd.  The real Estate or block of Lots in the City of Astoria State of O
2 CONC regon or the proceeds thereof I specially bequeath to my Daughter Mary E
2 CONC liza Wakeman after the death of my wife Ann R. Wilson.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3rd.  Of the property I own in the states of Missouri, Arkansas, Louisa
2 CONC na, Texis, Florada [sic] or Virginia -- especially the Valliare [sic] c
2 CONC laim now pending in the United States Courts in Missouri, I give and be
2 CONC queath one fourth to my Son David Wilson, one fourth to the three child
2 CONC ren of my son John Wilson, one fourth to my Daughter Mary Eliza Wakeman a
2 CONC nd one fourth to my Son Robert Wilson as tenants in common.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4th.  I leave my wife Ann R. Wilson and Daughter Mary Eliza Wakeman my E
2 CONC xecutrix is [??] and will add the name of my Son David Wilson as execut
2 CONC or -- to assist them as such in administering upon the property belongi
2 CONC ng to my Estate outside of the states of California and Oregon -- if th
2 CONC e law will permit such an appointment.  I desire that no bonds be requi
2 CONC red of my executrixes or executor or either of them, believing they wil
2 CONC l act fairly.  I want my executrixes to call upon Mr. McElrath Atty. at l
2 CONC aw and get him to sign an agreement which he has in writing, about the p
2 CONC roperty that my Wife Ann R. Wilson ownes in her own right in Contra Cos
2 CONC ta and Alameda Counties in the state of California.  I hereby revoke al
2 CONC l former wills, and hope my children will be satisfied with this arrang
2 CONC ement as I think it is under the circumstances a fair one.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Wilson
2 CONT signed sealed published and declared by the said John Wilson to be his l
2 CONC ast will and testament in the presence of us who in his presence and at h
2 CONC is requests and in the presence of each other have subscribed our names h
2 CONC ereto as witnesses this first day of February A.D. 1877
2 CONT Saml. F. Chapin, Residence Suisun City, Solano Co., Cal.
2 CONT Co. P. Reeves, Residence Suisun City, Solano Co., Cal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Filed in the office of the Clerk of the Probate Court in & for the City & C
2 CONC ounty of San Francisco, State of California this second day of March A.
2 CONC D. 1877
2 CONT Thos. H. Reynolds
2 CONT Clerk
2 CONT By Chas. E. Nougnes [?]
2 CONT Deputy Clerk
2 CONT
2 CONT [On a separate page, it is identified as a certified copy of the will, s
2 CONC igned by S.F. County Clerk Thomas H. Reynolds.  The copy was filed in H
2 CONC oward Co. MO:]
2 CONT State of Missouri
2 CONT County of Howard
2 CONT I, Jo. H. Finks, Clerk of the Circuit Court & ex-officio Recorder of De
2 CONC eds, within & for the County aforesaid, do certify that the within inst
2 CONC rument of unity [?] was filed in my office on the 11th Decr. 1878 at 10 o
2 CONC 'clock AM & is duly recorded in deed Book No. 21 at Pages 108 & 109.  [
2 CONC Another place mentions pages 107, 108, & 109.]
