2 CONT
2 CONT The Valliere claim, referred to in the third item above, was a huge Spa
2 CONC nish land grant covering millions of acres in Missouri and Arkansas.  T
2 CONC he story of John’s involvement is very interesting.  Following is an ex
2 CONC cerpt from a long newspaper article about the Valliere land grant in Th
2 CONC e Southern Guardian (Little Rock, AR), 22 Apr 1911, p. 2 (obtained from h
2 CONC ttp://arc.stparchive.com/Archive/ARC/ARC04221911p02.php):
2 CONT
2 CONT One account of this great land claim says that the records of this gran
2 CONC t were accidentally discovered by a gentleman in the surveyor-general’s o
2 CONC ffice, nearly forty years after the death of the grantee.  This gentlem
2 CONC an informed the Valliere heirs of the records of their deed in June, 18
2 CONC 41.  The surviving heirs gave a deed to John Wilson of Missouri for an u
2 CONC ndivided half interest in these lands.  Wilson was an enterprising lawy
2 CONC er, who moved to Missouri when there were no land titles there except S
2 CONC panish land titles.  He became involved in the elucidation of such gran
2 CONC ts and spent his whole life in pursuing clues to Spanish and Mexican la
2 CONC nd grants, and it was supposed that he would be able to straighten this g
2 CONC rant, as he had so many others.  But, as before stated, the land record
2 CONC s of Louisiana had been taken to Cuba and were not obtainable.  Wilson p
2 CONC urchased in New Orleans, for $7,000, from a Mrs. Pintard, copies of the g
2 CONC rants, some in full and some insubstance.  These formed evidence of the g
2 CONC rants made.  Then, he found, in the secretary of state’s office, the go
2 CONC vernor-general’s order book, containing the first official act of the V
2 CONC alliere grant, in the handwriting of Baron de Carondelet.  This, with t
2 CONC he archives purchased from Mrs. Pintard, gave him the first and second o
2 CONC fficial acts, and he went to Havana with a letter of introduction from D
2 CONC aniel Webster to try to get the third from the hidden archives.  He was p
2 CONC ut off for a long time, but finally the keeper of the archives took him s
2 CONC ecretly to the old tobacco warehouse, where they were secreted.  In a g
2 CONC loomy inner apartment, dumped on the floor, lay cart loads of records.  A
2 CONC fter great labor, Wilson extracted the Valliere grant, in due form and p
2 CONC roperly signed.  Of this he was permitted to take a copy.  This was all d
2 CONC one before the deed to him of half the claim was made.  After this deed w
2 CONC as made he went to New York, where he organized a company to secure con
2 CONC firmation of the grant.  Wilson sold his claim to the undivided half of t
2 CONC his grant to James Currie in 1842.  [Not sure how to reconcile this wit
2 CONC h the fact that John’s will in 1877 indicates that he still had an inte
2 CONC rest in the claim.  Maybe he sold part of his claim to Currie.]  In 184
2 CONC 3 Daniel Webster and Rufus Choate wrote a joint opinion saying that Val
2 CONC liere had obtained a good title to the land….
2 CONT  
2 CONT In 1860 an act was passed for the final adjustment of private land clai
2 CONC ms in Florida, Louisiana and Missouri. … Duryea, by information from Ha
2 CONC vana, was convinced of the genuineness of the claim, and filed a new pe
2 CONC tition for the land, just eighty-two years after the date of the origin
2 CONC al grant [this would be 1875]. The United States, through the attorney o
2 CONC f the United States Court of the Western District of Missouri, interpos
2 CONC ed a demurrer, which, after the case had dragged along till February, 1
2 CONC 878, the court sustained, declaring the grant invalid, and dismissed th
2 CONC e bill of the claimants.  The petitioners appealed to the United States S
2 CONC upreme Court, to which the case was submitted on printed argument.  The S
2 CONC upreme Court confirmed the decree of the District Court, dismissing the s
2 CONC uit from the courts, holding that for want of an adequate description b
2 CONC y which it can now be surveyed or identified, the claim did not come wi
2 CONC thin the provisions of the section of the act of 1860, which brought th
2 CONC e claims to the notice of the courts.  [This wasn’t quite the end of th
2 CONC e claims, but no more is needed here.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 census
2 CONT Image 4 on Ancestry.com (Waynesboro, Augusta Co., VA)
2 CONT Listed as John Wilson.  Household consisted of 1 male age 26–44 (John), 2 m
2 CONC ales age under 10 (could be David and John T.), 1 female age 16–25 (cou
2 CONC ld be John’s second wife, Martha/Patsy), and 1 female age under 10 (pos
2 CONC sibly a daughter who died young?).  Can't be sure this is the right Joh
2 CONC n Wilson, but he seems to fit well.  This is the only John Wilson liste
2 CONC d for Augusta Co., VA.  There is a John Wilson, age 26-44, living by hi
2 CONC mself in Mercer Co., KY, which doesn't seem likely.  There are no John W
2 CONC ilsons listed in Howard Co., MO.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 census
2 CONT Images 57 and 58 on Ancestry.com (Howard Co., MO)
2 CONT Listed as John Wilson.  Household consisted of three males (one aged 30
2 CONC -39, two aged 20-29) and two females (ages 20-29 and under 5).  The mal
2 CONC e aged 30-39 could have been John, though he probably was 40 at the tim
2 CONC e of the census (but maybe the age was supposed to be as of January 1?)
2 CONC .  One of the males aged 20-29 could have been his brother, William Ada
2 CONC ir.  Could one or both of sons David and John T. have been listed as ag
2 CONC e 20-29?  They were younger than that.  The female aged 20-29 could hav
2 CONC e been wife Ann, and the under-5-year-old could have been daughter Susa
2 CONC n Anne.  Also, two slaves are listed, females aged 24-35 and under 10.  T
2 CONC his doesn’t seem right because son William Henry is missing, but the ot
2 CONC her John Wilsons in Howard County fit even less well.
