1 NOTE Middle initial is from the biographies of Robert H. Wilson in "History o
2 CONC f Saline County, Missouri" and "Portrait and Biographical Record of Laf
2 CONC ayette and Saline Counties, Missouri", among other places.  The biograp
2 CONC hy in "Portrait and Biographical Record of Lafayette and Saline Countie
2 CONC s, Missouri" says that the family had eight children, three boys and fi
2 CONC ve girls.  If that is the case, then there is one other daughter not li
2 CONC sted here.  The Ancestral File listed a son named James Patton Willson, b
2 CONC orn before 1820.  That doesn't fit with the statement about three sons, a
2 CONC nd it also doesn't fit with David and Margaret being married in June 18
2 CONC 19.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children listed here are the same as listed on the Foster/Reid Fami
2 CONC ly Tree web site.  John and Robert are listed in biographies in "Histor
2 CONC y of Saline County, Missouri".  Robert, Julia, and A.G. are listed in "
2 CONC Portrait and Biographical Record of Lafayette and Saline Counties, Miss
2 CONC ouri".  Margaret, Paulina, Andrew, and Juliett are in census listings.  E
2 CONC liza is listed in Foster/Reid but not in any of those other sources.  I
2 CONC n legal papers associated with settlement of the estate of David's brot
2 CONC her-in-law, Givens Kinnear (available online at http://www.lva.virginia
2 CONC .gov/chancery/case_detail.asp?CFN=163-1851-020), a court hearing on 9 A
2 CONC ug 1848 is described, in which James Reid (husband of Eliza Jane Wilson R
2 CONC eid, who had already passed away) was chosen and appointed guardian of t
2 CONC he minor heirs of David S. Wilson, deceased.  Robert, Margaret, and Pau
2 CONC lina are listed as heirs over the age of 14, and Givens and Julia are l
2 CONC isted as heirs under 14 years of age.  The guardianship was for the pur
2 CONC poses of looking after their interests in the estate of Givens Kinnear.  J
2 CONC ames Reid was also appointed guardian of his own son, David William Rei
2 CONC d, as the heir of Eliza Jane Reid, deceased.  John W. Wilson was not li
2 CONC sted, as he was already over 21.  If there was another daughter in the f
2 CONC amily, as mentioned above, she must also have been already over 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of Saline County" says that David fought in the War of 1812, a
2 CONC nd moved to Saline Co., MO, in 1830 (see notes for sons John W. and Rob
2 CONC ert H.).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are several Augusta County deeds of trust involving debts of Davi
2 CONC d and his brother John to various people in the years from 1815 to 1820
2 CONC .  Their debts also figured in two Augusta County chancery-court cases, H
2 CONC aupe v. Wilson and others (http://www.lva.virginia.gov/chancery/case_de
2 CONC tail.asp?CFN=015-1821-064) and Little & Telford v. Wilson and others pl
2 CONC us Brown v. Little & Telford and others (http://www.lva.virginia.gov/ch
2 CONC ancery/case_detail.asp?CFN=015-1832-026).  At least some of the borrowe
2 CONC d money was used to buy property, presumably for investment.  In partic
2 CONC ular, in 1819 John and David bought property in and around the town of W
2 CONC aynesborough, including a tavern, from William Brown for $3,250, which t
2 CONC hey were to pay in installments up through February 1827.  However, Bro
2 CONC wn’s court complaint in 1826 said that shortly after he sold the land t
2 CONC o them the Wilsons became insolvent and abandoned the property, and the
2 CONC y had not made any of the payments.  A deed of trust dated 25 Apr 1820 f
2 CONC rom John and David to sheriff James McNutt mentions that they had been t
2 CONC rading under the firm of John & David Wilson.  That deed of trust appea
2 CONC rs to be them basically declaring bankruptcy and forfeiting loan collat
2 CONC eral.  There are affidavits along with Brown’s complaint stating that t
2 CONC he land Brown sold the Wilsons would probably not sell for more than $2
2 CONC ,000 in 1826, if even that much.  So John and David (and William, becau
2 CONC se he had secured some of their loans) were stuck and couldn’t make the p
2 CONC ayments even by selling the property.  This drop in land value was like
2 CONC ly caused by the Panic of 1819; see the notes for William Wilson.  The c
2 CONC ourt papers show that the court appointed a commissioner to oversee Joh
2 CONC n’s and David’s property starting in 1821.  Presumably, the property fi
2 CONC nally reverted to Brown in 1827.  Note that John probably moved to Miss
2 CONC ouri in 1820 or 1821, but David remained in Virginia until 1830.  There a
2 CONC re more details on all of this in the WilsonAugustaCounty document.
2 CONT
2 CONT David apparently continued to have financial problems.  The Library of V
2 CONC irginia has online copies of old chancery-court records, like the ones r
2 CONC eferred to above.  I will mention a couple of others here; in each case
2 CONC , the file can be obtained from http://www.lva.virginia.gov/chancery/ca
2 CONC se_detail.asp?CFN=xxx, where xxx is the file ID (for example, the case a
2 CONC bove is 163-1851-020, where 163 indicates Rockbridge County, 1851 is th
2 CONC e year of the final resolution of the case, and 020 indicates that it i
2 CONC s the 20th case for 1851).  In 1835 I have found two cases, Tompkins v. W
2 CONC illson (163-1835-004) and Stevens v. Willson (163-1835-005), which both h
2 CONC ad the purpose of subjecting "the Interest of David S Willson & Margare
2 CONC t his wife, in the Estate of Nancy Kinnear dec'd to a debt due the Plai
2 CONC ntiff".  Nancy Kinnear was a sister of David's wife, Margaret.  When Ma
2 CONC rgaret's brother, Givens Kinnear, died in 1838, he said in his will "th
2 CONC e interest of the sum of fifteen hundred dollars is to be given yearly t
2 CONC o my Sister Margaret Wilson & her offspring, for ten years[,] the princ
2 CONC iple to be given to her heirs".  The executor of Givens' estate, Willia
2 CONC m Davidson (husband of Givens' and Margaret's sister, Susan), in a stat
2 CONC ement dated 4 Oct 1844 at the beginning of the Kinnear v. Thompson case (
2 CONC 163-1851-020), said that "his [Givens'] object was to provide to the ex
2 CONC tent of his means for the support, and comfortable maintenance of his s
2 CONC ister and her children.  The Husband of Mrs Wilson had for years been d
2 CONC eeply embarrassed, and was in fact hopelessly insolvent, and [Givens] k
2 CONC new well that if the money went into his hands, his sister and her fami
2 CONC ly would receive no benefit therefrom."  There is no indication of the c
2 CONC ause of the insolvency.  It sounds like he incurred additional debts af
2 CONC ter the ones with his brother John.
