1 NOTE Glasgow, MO, Weekly Times, 15 Jun 1854 (obtained from Find A Grave, lis
2 CONC ting for Mary Polly Wilson Grove):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. MARY GROVE, wife of Sam'l Grove, Esq., was born in Augusta county, V
2 CONC a., in the year 1797; was married in 1818, joined the Presbyterian Chur
2 CONC ch the year following, and emigrated to Howard county in 1825;--three y
2 CONC ears after to Saline, where she died on the 6th inst., being one of the f
2 CONC irst settlers of that now flourishing county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Grove was universally known and esteemed.  Particularly characteri
2 CONC stic for her kindness and attention to the sick and afflicted, she most f
2 CONC aithfully exercised those noble and endearing virtues of the heart.  St
2 CONC rangers realised from her a mother's tender attentions in affliction; a
2 CONC nd the bereaved and disconsolate ever found in her unostentatious minis
2 CONC trations, that soothing sympathy which came warm and free from a genero
2 CONC us heart.  It was pertinently remarked by one of her neighbors that "th
2 CONC e <i>mother</i> of the neighborhood was gone."  How great indeed, then, m
2 CONC ust be the loss of her own immediate family.  Would that we could breat
2 CONC he words of consoling sympathy into their hearts.  We need not tell the
2 CONC m that their loss is her gain--of that they are well assured by her uni
2 CONC formly pious life, and her peacefully triumphant death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband, a few more brief days of toilsome sojourn, and thou shalt join t
2 CONC hy lost one, where there are no partings--no tears to wipe away.  Think t
2 CONC hat in mercy God took <i>her</i> first, for her gentle nature might not h
2 CONC ave brooked the storms of bereavement if thou hadst been the victim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons, daughters, think of your mother's virtues and graces, and make th
2 CONC em the rules of your lives, that death to you may be but the happy re-u
2 CONC nion with her whose undying love for you was all that bound her to eart
2 CONC h.  Deep and pure were the last breathings of prayer for you, her sons o
2 CONC n the Pacific shore.  And may the beautiful life of thy mother cheer th
2 CONC ee, thou <i>lone one</i>, into pious resignation to God's holy will.  A
2 CONC nd when life's toils and afflictions are over, may all meet her in Heav
2 CONC en.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth in 1797 does not agree with the information from James Hotchkiss a
2 CONC nd the Adair Wilson family record.  I don't know for sure which is corr
2 CONC ect.
