1 CHAN
2 DATE 10 SEP 2017
1 NOTE "A History of Northwest Missouri", ed. Walter Williams, Lewis Publishin
2 CONC g Co., 1915, pp. 1462-1463  (obtained from Google Books):
2 CONT
2 CONT GEN. ROBERT WILSON.  Among the stalwart men who helped to shape the des
2 CONC tinies of the state, few have played a more important part than Gen. Ro
2 CONC bert Wilson, who spent the latter years of his life on his farm in Andr
2 CONC ew County, a short distance north of St. Joseph.  Sympathetic with the n
2 CONC eeds and aspirations of the people, clear and fixed in his own ideas of e
2 CONC xpediency and right, and giving expression to his ideals both by precep
2 CONC t and example with force and dignity, his was a character of inestimabl
2 CONC e usefulness during the formative and tempestuous years of the state's f
2 CONC irst half century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born near Staunton, Virginia, November, 1800, Robert Wilson lived there u
2 CONC ntil he came to Missouri in 1820.  Settling in Howard County, he taught s
2 CONC chool for a time and later found employment in the office of the Circui
2 CONC t Clerk.  Subsequently he was appointed postmaster of Fayette and in 18
2 CONC 23 was elected Judge of the Probate Court.  In 1828 he was elected cler
2 CONC k of the Circuit and County Court, which office he continued to hold un
2 CONC til 1840.  Meantime he had studied law under his brother General John W
2 CONC ilson and had been admitted to the bar.  In 1837 on the outbreak of the s
2 CONC o-called Mormon war, he was appointed brigadier general of the state fo
2 CONC rces by Governor Boggs and was instrumental, by his firm and judicious c
2 CONC onduct of affairs, in ridding the state of a population so generally ob
2 CONC noxious to its citizens.  In 1844, Randolph County having been formed a
2 CONC nd he having removed to Huntsville, he was sent therefrom to the State L
2 CONC egislature.  Removing thence to Andrew County in 1852, he was elected i
2 CONC n 1854 to represent that district in the Senate and re-elected in 1858, a
2 CONC lthough he was a whig and the district strongly democratic.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early part of 1861, General Wilson was chosen as a union delegat
2 CONC e to the convention called by the state to determine its attitude regar
2 CONC ding secession and at its first session, February 28th, he was elected v
2 CONC ice-president with Sterling Price as president.  Subsequently Price hav
2 CONC ing fled to join the Confederacy, General Wilson succeeded to the presi
2 CONC dency and presided over the convention's deliberations until its close.  I
2 CONC n January, 1862, he was appointed by Acting Governor Hall to the United S
2 CONC tates Senate to fill the unexpired term of Waldo P. Johnson expelled, a
2 CONC nd discharged the duties of that office for two sessions until the elec
2 CONC tion of B. Gratz Brown.
2 CONT
2 CONT After retiring from the Senate, General Wilson though keeping in close t
2 CONC ouch with public affairs devoted himself to agriculture, in which his i
2 CONC nterest was intense.  While on a visit to his old surroundings in Centr
2 CONC al Missouri, he was stricken with pneumonia and passed away May 10, 187
2 CONC 0, at the home of his nephew, Capt. Ben Wilson, at Marshall, leaving be
2 CONC hind a record that was unblemished as to both public and private life a
2 CONC nd one that was unusually rich in evidences of the highest usefulness t
2 CONC o his fellow-men.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1825 General Wilson was married to Margaret Snoddy who died in 1836, l
2 CONC eaving him three children, John, who was graduated from Yale in 1847 an
2 CONC d who died in St. Joseph in 1858; James, who was educated at Centre Col
2 CONC lege, Danville, Kentucky, and who died in St. Joseph in 1906, and Mary A
2 CONC nn who in 1855 was married to Rufus L. McDonald of St. Joseph and who s
2 CONC till survives.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary from the New York Times, 18 May 1870 (obtained from https://ww
2 CONC w.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/24332216/person/1507256492/facts
2 CONC ):
2 CONT
2 CONT Gen. Robert Wilson, of Missouri.
2 CONT The St. Joseph (Mo.) Herald records the death in that City of pneumonia
2 CONC , on the evening of the 9th inst., of Gen. ROBERT WILSON, who was born i
2 CONC n Virginia in 1803.  His mother was a relative of the late Hon. WILLIAM L
2 CONC . YANCEY, of Alabama.  At an early age he removed to Franklin, Mo., and s
2 CONC ubsequently to Fayette, in the same State, and of that town he was the f
2 CONC irst Postmaster.  In 1838, being a Brigadier-General in the State Milit
2 CONC ia, he was directed by the Governor to hear and determine all the quest
2 CONC ions of dispute between the citizens and the Mormons, then residing in C
2 CONC aldwell County, and this difficult matter he accomplished to the entire s
2 CONC atisfaction of the people and the authorities of the State.  In 1840 he e
2 CONC ntered upon the practice of law, and although a Whig in politics, and l
2 CONC iving in a Democratic district, was elected to the Legislature in 1844, a
2 CONC nd again in 1845.  In 1854 he was elected to the State Senate by a hand
2 CONC some majority over his Democratic opponent, though the district was str
2 CONC ongly Democratic, and he was re-elected in 1858.  He was a good stump s
2 CONC peaker, and in 1860 he made many noble speeches in favor of BELL and EV
2 CONC ERETT.  While in the State Senate he was the acknowledged leader of the W
2 CONC higs, and to his activity and vigilance was due, in great measure, the d
2 CONC efeat of the bill for the sale of the free negroes in Missouri.  In the l
2 CONC ast Legislature of which he was a member, while CLAIB. JACKSON was Gove
2 CONC rnor, he opposed and fought to the end the Military bill and every othe
2 CONC r act of that body looking to the forcible secession of the State.  As t
2 CONC he contest waxed warm, he and JOHN SCOTT of St. Joseph, with Dr. MORRIS o
2 CONC f St. Louis and Mr. GORDON of Boone were the only open, outspoken, fear
2 CONC less Union men in the Senate.  In 1861 he was elected to the State Conv
2 CONC ention, called to consider the relations of Missouri with the Federal G
2 CONC overnment.  This Convention was called by the Secessionists, but the Un
2 CONC ionists elected a majority of the delegates.  In the organization of th
2 CONC at body--the most important one that was ever held in Missouri, for it d
2 CONC eposed the Governor, turned out the State officials and installed a Pro
2 CONC visional Government throughout, with HAMILTON GAMBLE for Governor, and W
2 CONC ILLARD P. HALL for Lieutenant-Governor--the Unionists voted for STERLIN
2 CONC G PRICE, then an avowed Union man, for President, and Gen. WILSON for V
2 CONC ice-President.  Both were elected.  STERLING PRICE went off with CALIB. [
2 CONC sic] JACKSON into the rebellion, and Gen. WILSON became President of th
2 CONC e Convention.  That body was composed of the ablest men in the State, i
2 CONC ncluding Hon. John B. Henderson, Willard P. and Wm. A. Hall, Uriel Wrig
2 CONC ht, James O. Broadhead, Hamilton Gamble, Samuel M. Breckinridge, and ma
2 CONC ny others.  Before adjourning, the country was plunged into civil war, a
2 CONC nd the two United States Senators, WALDO P. JOHNSON and TRUSTEN POLK, h
2 CONC aving wandered off into the Confederacy, were expelled, and the Governo
2 CONC r appointed Gen. WILSON to fill one of the vacancies.  He took his seat i
2 CONC n the Senate of the United States in 1861 and served till his successor
2 CONC , Hon. B. GRATZ BROWN, was elected by the Legislature in December, 1863
2 CONC .  Since that time Gen. WILSON has for the most part resided with his s
2 CONC on-in-law, RUFUS L. MCDONALD, of St. Joseph.  He owned a farm in Andrew C
2 CONC ounty, but he never resided upon it after the commencement of the war.  T
2 CONC he rebels stole from him nearly $25,000 worth of horses, cattle and oth
2 CONC er personal property, and having never married again, he let the old fa
2 CONC rm go, and made his home with his daughter.  The St. Joseph Herald, fro
2 CONC m which we glean the foregoing facts, continues:  "In 1860 his friends t
2 CONC hroughout this part of the State were enthusiastic in the determination t
2 CONC o run him for Governor against CLAIBORNE F. JACKSON, and many of the Be
2 CONC ll and Everett joarnals [sic] hoisted the name of their favorite and pr
2 CONC aised the virtues of 'The Farmer of Andrew.'  He has been President of t
2 CONC he Northwestern Agricultural and Mechanical Society ever since its orga
2 CONC nization.  As a presiding officer over a deliberative parliamentary bod
2 CONC y, he had very few equals--ever cool and calm, never thrown off his gua
2 CONC rd by excitement or passion, and prompt to decide every question which c
2 CONC ame before him.  He was proverbially upright, and of the strictest hone
2 CONC sty and integrity of character.  Kind, gentle and very affectionate in h
2 CONC is family, he bore the troubles and trials of life with a calm patience a
2 CONC nd resignation which gave him many warm friends, and not an enemy in th
2 CONC e world.  His early education and habits fitted him peculiarly for a ce
2 CONC rtain kind of legal labor, and we have often heard our best lawyers say t
2 CONC hat no man in the State of Missouri could draw up a difficult contract o
2 CONC r a complicated legal paper, with a correctness, conciseness and perfec
2 CONC tion which characterized every such act of his.  He was a man of plain e
2 CONC xterior, but gifted with most excellent judgment and the best practical s
2 CONC ense.  He was emphatically an honest man."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT As can be seen in the two bios above, some sources show 1800 for his bi
2 CONC rth and some show 1803.  The case for 1800 seems better to me, and this i
2 CONC s what is given by family sources (the Adair Wilson family record from 1
2 CONC 902 and Benjamin Harrison Wilson's letter from around 1895).  There als
2 CONC o is some uncertainty about his death date; I have seen May 8, 9, and 1
2 CONC 0 in different places.  The case seems best for May 10, and once again t
2 CONC his is what is given by the two family sources.  Ben's letter also conf
2 CONC irms what is stated in the first bio above -- that Robert died at Ben's h
2 CONC ome in Marshall.  Note also that the two biographies differ on where Ro
2 CONC bert resided after he left the Senate.  The first one says that he "spe
2 CONC nt the latter years of his life on his farm in Andrew County".  However
2 CONC , the NYT obituary says that he "never resided upon it [his farm] after t
2 CONC he commencement of the war", and instead lived with his daughter in St. J
2 CONC oseph.  Robert is not listed with the McDonald family in the 1870 censu
2 CONC s, but I have been unable to find him in Andrew County, either.  Perhap
2 CONC s the enumeration took place after Robert's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Another biography of Robert in "Reminiscences of the Bench and Bar of M
2 CONC issouri" by W.V.N. Bay (F. H. Thomas and Company, St. Louis, 1878; obta
2 CONC ined from Google Books), on p. 561 says that he was familiarly known as G
2 CONC eneral Bob Wilson.  It also says that he moved to Missouri in the sprin
2 CONC g of 1820.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1830 census
2 CONT Images 5 and 6 on Ancestry.com (Randolph Co., MO)
2 CONT Listed as Robert Wilson.  Household consisted of three males (one aged 2
2 CONC 0-29, two under 5) and two females (ages 60-69 and 20-29).  The male ag
2 CONC ed 20-29 was presumably Robert, and one of the males under 5 was presum
2 CONC ably son John.  I don't know who the other under-5 male was; perhaps a c
2 CONC hild who died young?  The female aged 20-29 was presumably wife Margare
2 CONC t, and the female aged 60-69 could have been Robert's mother, Catharine
2 CONC .  Also, five slaves were listed, males aged 10-23 and under10, two fem
2 CONC ales aged 10-23 and one female under 10.  Can't be sure this is the rig
2 CONC ht Robert, but it fits really well.
