1 _UID C30978C923E61940A078F0FA1008FBB53909
1 CHAN
2 DATE 27 MAY 2018
1 NOTE "Biographical History of Eminent and Self-Made Men of the State of Indi
2 CONC ana", Western Biographical Publishing Co., Cincinnati, OH, 1880, Vol. 1
2 CONC , 5th District, pp. 15-16 (obtained from http://www.archive.org/details
2 CONC /biographicalhist01cinc):
2 CONT
2 CONT GROVE, JOHN B., of Columbus, was born in Augusta County, Virginia, on t
2 CONC he 2d of August, 1829, and is the son of Adam and Anna S. (Rankin) Grov
2 CONC e, well-to-do farmers of that state.  He received an academic education a
2 CONC t Shemariah Academy, in Virginia, and at the age of seventeen began the s
2 CONC tudy of medicine under a private tutor.  In 1846 he entered the Univers
2 CONC ity of Pennsylvania, and graduated in 1849, receiving a premature exami
2 CONC nation, to enable him to remove to California.  He took his departure o
2 CONC n the third day of May, acting as surgeon of the vessel, and sailed aro
2 CONC und Cape Horn, visiting several of the South American states, and arriv
2 CONC ing at San Francisco November 6, 1849.  Here he immediately entered upo
2 CONC n the practice of his profession, which he continued until the next spr
2 CONC ing.  He then accepted the position of inspector in the custom-house at S
2 CONC an Francisco; but this proving less lucrative than he had anticipated, h
2 CONC e resigned the office in 1850.  Allured by the "gold fever" into the in
2 CONC terior, he removed to Long Bar, on the Yuba River, in Yuba County, wher
2 CONC e he engaged in the practice of medicine and in mining operations.  In 1
2 CONC 851 he was made the Whig candidate for the Lower House of the state Leg
2 CONC islature, and, although his party was in the minority by six hundred, h
2 CONC is personal popularity was such that he came within eighteen votes of r
2 CONC eceiving the election.  In 1854, the Whig party having attained the maj
2 CONC ority, its nomination became equivalent to an election, and his friends u
2 CONC rged his name for the office of county treasurer.  In the convention th
2 CONC ey were universally admitted to be largely in the majority, but so corr
2 CONC upt were political affairs in those primitive days that one of his oppo
2 CONC nents, in open market, shamelessly purchased enough delegates to secure t
2 CONC he nomination.  In the spring of 1856 he received the appointment as re
2 CONC sident physician of the Yuba County Hospital, a position of honor and t
2 CONC rust, which he held for a period of eighteen months.  At the end of tha
2 CONC t time he resumed the practice of medicine at Marysville, and continued i
2 CONC t until the spring of 1858, when he returned to the States, and in the f
2 CONC all of that year settled at Marshall, Saline County, Missouri, where he m
2 CONC arried his first wife, Miss Kate Wilson, daughter of Judge William A. W
2 CONC ilson.  In the spring of 1862 he received the important appointment of p
2 CONC ost surgeon for the Union army at Marshall, the duties of which positio
2 CONC n he discharged with skill, and to the entire satisfaction of all who c
2 CONC ame under his care.  That part of the state, becoming the field of oper
2 CONC ation for both the contending armies, was neither safe nor pleasant as a r
2 CONC esidence, and in 1864, when General Price overran the country with his d
2 CONC evastating army, Dr. Grove determined to seek a more peaceful locality.  H
2 CONC e accordingly removed to Columbus, Indiana, where he still resides, and w
2 CONC here he soon acquired a large and remunerative practice.  On the 25th o
2 CONC f January, 1866, he met a sad misfortune in the death of his estimable w
2 CONC ife, who left two children--Ada and Florence.  June 6, 1868, he married L
2 CONC ouise Westfall, by whom he has two sons--Albert Sidney and John Cliffor
2 CONC d.  In 1872 he was elected a member of the city council, a position whi
2 CONC ch he so ably filled that, in the spring of 1874, he was nominated as s
2 CONC tate Senator for the counties of Bartholomew and Brown, by the Democrat
2 CONC s of the district.  After a hot contest against a combination of Republ
2 CONC icans, Grangers, Temperance men, and Greenbackers, in which nearly the e
2 CONC ntire Democratic ticket was defeated by heavy majorities, he was triump
2 CONC hantly elected.  The three sessions of which he was a member were the m
2 CONC ost important and exciting that had been held for years.  At this time t
2 CONC he temperance question, which had been an exciting issue among the peop
2 CONC le, was pacifically adjusted by the repeal of the so-called "Baxter law
2 CONC ," and the enactment, in its stead, of the present well devised, closel
2 CONC y guarded, and satisfactory license law.  In bringing about this result
2 CONC , none were more active and influential than Senator Grove, whose effec
2 CONC tive speeches were largely commented upon by the press.  At this time, a
2 CONC lso, the gravel road question was attracting considerable attention, an
2 CONC d many important measures in regard to it were brought before the Legis
2 CONC lature.  So conflicting and intense were these interests that the clear
2 CONC est judgment was required to insure just and proper legislation.  The r
2 CONC esponsibilities resting upon Senator Grove were delicate and arduous, a
2 CONC nd yet, with tact of no ordinary kind, he discharged this duty to his c
2 CONC onstituents in so able a manner as to receive the warmest commendation o
2 CONC f all.  Through his instrumentality some meritorious laws were enacted u
2 CONC pon this subject, and some very bad measures were effectually defeated.  N
2 CONC o legislator can do all that he hopes.  The selfishness of mankind, the g
2 CONC reed of corporations, the sluggishness with which new impressions are r
2 CONC eceived, are so many barriers in the way of a really painstaking law-ma
2 CONC ker.  At the close of his term, his constituents in Bartholomew County w
2 CONC ere unanimous for his return; but party usages had alternated the offic
2 CONC e between the two counties composing the district, and, with a magnanim
2 CONC ity truly commendable, he refused to break in upon this custom, and per
2 CONC emptorily declined the nomination.  Doctor Grove sympathized with the o
2 CONC ld national Whig party in his earlier days, and up to its abandonment; a
2 CONC fterward he supported Fillmore, and, at the next election, Bell and Eve
2 CONC rett.  In 1863 he became identified with the Democratic organization, o
2 CONC f which he has since been an earnest and consistent member, taking a de
2 CONC ep interest in its welfare, and contributing to its successful manageme
2 CONC nt.  As a physician he stands at the head of the profession.  In religi
2 CONC ous faith he is an Episcopalian, and gives that denomination his aid an
2 CONC d support.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In this biography, it says that he became post surgeon for the Union Ar
2 CONC my at Marshall, MO, in the spring of 1862.  Note that his father-in-law
2 CONC , Lt. Col. William A. Wilson, became commander of the post in the fall o
2 CONC f 1862.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a great deal of Grove family history in "The History of a Grov
2 CONC e Family: The First Two Centuries in America" by Robert Neil Grove (rev
2 CONC ised edition self-published in 1990), who was a descendant of John Brig
2 CONC ht Grove by his second wife, Louise Westfall.
2 CONT
2 CONT
