2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN R. VANCE, a prominent member of the Saline County Bar, and a leadi
2 CONC ng and influential citizen of Marshall, Mo., received in 1892 the disti
2 CONC nguished and deserved honor of being appointed from the Seventh Distric
2 CONC t as delegate to the National Republican Convention held in Minneapolis
2 CONC .  For over a quarter of a century closely identified with all the best i
2 CONC nterests of his county and State, our subject ably represented his cons
2 CONC tituents in the great political gathering in Minnesota, and through his f
2 CONC idelity and energetic service in behalf of their instructions has won t
2 CONC he esteem and confidence of the general public.
2 CONT
2 CONT The paternal grandfather of John R. Vance was born in the North of Irel
2 CONC and, and was the purser of the first vessel which sailed after the clos
2 CONC e of the Revolutionary War.  James Vance, this worthy ancestor of our s
2 CONC ubject, built the first house erected in Martinsburgh, Va., and there e
2 CONC ngaged at his trade of cooper.  Later, he became one of the early settl
2 CONC ers of Ohio, and named Martinsburgh, Knox County, where he gave his tim
2 CONC e to agricultural duties until far advanced in life.  An energetic, ent
2 CONC erprising, and upright man and worthy citizen, he passed peacefully awa
2 CONC y in his eighty-seventh year.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Vance was a consistent member of the Scotch Presbyterian Church a
2 CONC ll his life, and gave to all religious duty and work the conscientious a
2 CONC nd earnest effort of a true Christian.  His son, Andrew Vance, was born i
2 CONC n Martinsburgh, Va., April 29, 1808, and removed to Martinsburgh, Ohio, w
2 CONC hen that country was little more than a wilderness.  He was trained in a
2 CONC gricultural duties upon his father's farm, and, arriving at manhood, im
2 CONC proved a homestead of his own.  He held official positions in the count
2 CONC y, discharging the duties with honor, and served as Captain in the mili
2 CONC tia in the boundary trouble between Ohio and Michigan.  Politically, he w
2 CONC as a Democrat, and in religious affiliation a Presbyterian.  He died Oc
2 CONC tober 8, 1887, of old age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary J. Agnue, his wife, was born in Pennsylvania April 27, 1809.  She w
2 CONC as the daughter of Capt. Agnue, who was born in Ireland, and who was a s
2 CONC ailor in his youth, and also a master of a vessel, and who settled in K
2 CONC nox County in a very early day, and was engaged in farming when he died
2 CONC .  This grandfather of our subject was of Scotch descent, and possessed a s
2 CONC trong and earnest character.  Mr. and Mrs. Andrew Vance were the parent
2 CONC s of six children:  Joseph M., the eldest son, lives in McLean County, I
2 CONC ll.; James S. is a resident of Ohio; William was killed by the Indians o
2 CONC n the plains in 1858; John R. is our subject; Rowland is a broker in th
2 CONC e grocery business in Columbus; and B. Eastburn is a resident of Columb
2 CONC us, in a general merchandise business.  The mother of these sons died S
2 CONC eptember 11, 1886, just thirteen months prior to the death of her husba
2 CONC nd.  The excellent couple both attained an advanced age, and commanded t
2 CONC he respect and esteem of all who knew them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our subject was born July 9, 1835, upon a farm in Knox County, Ohio, ne
2 CONC ar Mt. Vernon.  He attended the district school of the neighborhood whe
2 CONC n a little child, and afterward studied in Hayesville, and taught schoo
2 CONC l, meantime ambitiously preparing himself for college.  In 1862, after a f
2 CONC ull classical course in Kenyon College, at Gambier, Ohio, he graduated w
2 CONC ith the degree of A. B., and immediately began the study of law in the B
2 CONC uckeye State.  After a time he accepted the superintendence of the scho
2 CONC ols of Columbus, Ind., and was thus engaged one year, when he entered t
2 CONC he law department of the University of Ann Arbor, Mich., and graduated i
2 CONC n 1865 with the degree of LL. B.  Mr. Vance then began the study of law i
2 CONC n Columbus, but upon July 9, 1865, located in Marshall, and has practic
2 CONC ed his profession here with great success ever since.  A part of the ti
2 CONC me he has been in partnership with T.J. Yerby, associated in the genera
2 CONC l practice of law, his office being on North Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon April 9, 1868, Mr. Vance was united in marriage with Miss Annette W
2 CONC ilson, a native of Marshall, and a daughter of William A. Wilson, a Col
2 CONC onel in the Federal army, and attached to the Seventy-first Regiment, E
2 CONC . M. M.  Mrs. Vance was reared and educated at her home, and possesses a l
2 CONC arge circle of friends and acquaintances.  She is the happy mother of t
2 CONC hree bright, promising young people, all preparing to fill positions of h
2 CONC onor and usefulness.  Minnie is a Post-graduate of the High School; Ruf
2 CONC us A. is in his junior year in the Missouri Valley College; and Berenic
2 CONC e is at home.  Our subject is Trustee and Vestryman in the Episcopal Ch
2 CONC urch, and has been Superintendent of the Sunday-school.  He and his int
2 CONC eresting family are among the important factors in the social, benevole
2 CONC nt and religious enterprises of Marshall, and are universally respected a
2 CONC nd esteemed.  Mr. Vance has always taken an active part in the national a
2 CONC nd local conduct of political affairs.  He has represented his constitu
2 CONC ents at State and county conventions, as well as at the National Conven
2 CONC tion of 1892, and in all the duties of each position has clearly demons
2 CONC trated his ability, energy, intelligence and integrity of character.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: the dates given in this biography for John's birth and marriage d
2 CONC o not agree with other sources.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Middle name is from birth record of daughter Berenice.  Also from Ridge P
2 CONC ark Cemetery record at http://ridgeparkcemetery.com/V.html.
2 CONT
2 CONT In information from Ridge Park Cemetery in Marshall, MO, his cause of d
2 CONC eath is listed as "paralysis".
2 CONT
2 CONT
