1 _UID 279B52311650C844920C0DC565EE003DB7D0
1 CHAN
2 DATE 19 AUG 2018
1 NOTE James Hotchkiss spelled her last name Pullian.  The marriage record on A
2 CONC ncestry.com spells it Pulliam.  Full name of Anne Robertson Pulliam is g
2 CONC iven in a record on Ancestry.com in the California Pioneer and Immigran
2 CONC t Files, 1790-1950.  Some sources, like this one, spell her first name A
2 CONC nne, but more spell it Ann.  And she herself, in John's War of 1812 pen
2 CONC sion file, spells it Ann.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is included (spelled Ann Robertson Pulliam) in "Cockrill Genealogy" b
2 CONC y Granville Goodloe, in Vol. 3 of the American Historical Magazine (189
2 CONC 8), available from Google Books.  Her parents were Drury Pulliam and An
2 CONC n Cockrill.  Ann Robertson was her maternal grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper clipping (a handwritten note says SF Post Oct. 2, 1886) from K
2 CONC atherine Cooper's scrapbook in the John Wilson Papers (BANC MSS C-B 420
2 CONC , The Bancroft Library, University of California, Berkeley, CA):
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT A month ago San Francisco lost two of her mothers who were held in high r
2 CONC egard, and whose lives read like a romance--Mrs. General John Wilson an
2 CONC d her cousin, Mrs. Colonel Bryant.  Mrs. Ann Robinson [sic] Wilson was a n
2 CONC ative of Tennessee, and in her eighty-fifth year.  She had no illness, a
2 CONC nd her going was simply translation--laying aside the dear body that we l
2 CONC oved and that had served her so long as needed.  To the end none of her s
2 CONC enses were impaired--no infirmities attended her and she had her second s
2 CONC ight.  She read the most important literature and kept pace with the ti
2 CONC mes, taking especial interest in national affairs.  She was dignified a
2 CONC nd graceful, and while cordially hospitable and genial, proudly held to a
2 CONC ll polite forms.  She was like a grand old queen, enthroned in her rock
2 CONC ing-chair, holding court--always to a very happy audience.  She never l
2 CONC ost her important individuality in the household, although the children o
2 CONC f the first, second and third generation were of her home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even when she had ceased making conventional calls, separate invitation
2 CONC s and calling cards were sent her--and, in fact, the cream of Californi
2 CONC a people, old and young, made it their pleasure to visit her.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the recent G. A. R. [Grand Army of the Republic] Encampment my f
2 CONC irst greeting from General Sherman was: "How is ma, and how is my good o
2 CONC ld neighbor, Mrs. Wilson?  I must run up there and take her Mrs. Sherma
2 CONC n's messages."  Upon every return to California the latter lady always h
2 CONC ad a most enjoyable day with her.  Mrs. Wilson was a cousin of Mrs. Pre
2 CONC sident Polk, whom she was said to resemble in dignity of manner and exa
2 CONC lted character.  General Jackson was an intimate friend of the family, a
2 CONC nd in girlhood at balls he had more than once selected her as partner i
2 CONC n the Virginia reel.  When a little child, her uncle gave a dinner to A
2 CONC aron Burr, who had retired from the vice presidency when Jefferson was P
2 CONC resident, and who was then going through the Southwest.  It was suspect
2 CONC ed that his journey was for the purpose of organizing an expedition to i
2 CONC nvade Mexico and establish an empire which would embrace a number of th
2 CONC e Southwestern states of this Union.  Her parents rode over on horsebac
2 CONC k, the father carrying her before him.  She was perched upon a high sea
2 CONC t at table, near the distinguished guest.  She was afterward married to G
2 CONC eneral Wilson, who had for years been with Thomas Jefferson and an inma
2 CONC te of his home.  He was active in campaigns, and as lawyer and politici
2 CONC an traveled much about the country.  His wife often accompanied him dow
2 CONC n the Mississippi, sometimes in company with Henry Clay, Prentiss and B
2 CONC enton.  This couple danced the minuet in New Orleans more than sixty ye
2 CONC ars ago, and were prominent in the brilliant society of that day.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1849 President Taylor sent General Wilson to California on a governm
2 CONC ent mission.  A government train accompanied him over the plains, and b
2 CONC eside his family, Mr. J. M. Hutchings, now of Yosemite valley, and Dr. B
2 CONC irdsall and daughters--one of whom married Governor Latham--were also i
2 CONC ncluded in the company.  Mrs. Wilson was soon established in a house--n
2 CONC ew, large and convenient for the times--where she exercised an ennoblin
2 CONC g influence.  It became the center for the gay and brilliant spirits of t
2 CONC hat magnificent pioneer band--the flower of the best families from ever
2 CONC y section of the union.  This hospitable nature was hers to the end--ha
2 CONC ppily entertaining Mrs. Thos. Brown and Mrs. Tingley at dinner but a co
2 CONC uple of days before she died.  When eighty, after the General's death, s
2 CONC he revisited the East, where she was accorded ovations from the highest e
2 CONC ven to some of her aged slaves, who still continue about the old planta
2 CONC tions, and one of whom came out to California to her.  She made the tri
2 CONC p home entirely unattended.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a favorite among the young ladies and gentlemen, and receiving t
2 CONC hem was a great pleasure.  She was a benevolent Christian character, an
2 CONC d her long life, with all its vicissitudes, as near perfection as is of
2 CONC ten attained.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT POLLY IDLEWAY.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT To follow up on the comments about General Sherman, according to Wikipe
2 CONC dia he was a bank manager in San Francisco from 1853 to 1857 (before he w
2 CONC as a general, of course).  It must have been during that period that he g
2 CONC ot to know John and Ann Wilson.  Their granddaughter, Katherine Wakeman C
2 CONC ooper, wrote an article for The Grizzly Bear magazine, November 1912 is
2 CONC sue (available from Google Books), about the Rincon Hill neighborhood i
2 CONC n San Francisco where the Wilsons lived.  She mentions the Shermans and s
2 CONC ays that they were friends of the Wilsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An issue of the Jackson, MO, Independent Patriot newspaper dated 19 Feb 1
2 CONC 825 contained the following notice (obtained from the State Historical S
2 CONC ociety of Missouri):  "Passed at the present session of the Legislature o
2 CONC f Missouri, in addition to the ones previously published in the Patriot .
2 CONC .. 56. An act declaring the unlawful marriage of Ann R. Pulliam with W. M
2 CONC . Chick, null and void."  Wonder what that was about??
