1 NOTE "History of Saline County", p. 315:
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after this affair [a fight with guerillas], two young men, Grove, a n
2 CONC ephew of Colonel Wm. A. Wilson, and Gilliam, a son of A. W. Gilliam, Sr
2 CONC ., of the northeastern part of the county, were captured on the Miami r
2 CONC oad by a scouting party of Federal soldiers from Marshall.  Major Kelly
2 CONC , then in command of the post, says they were taken straggling in the r
2 CONC ear of a band of Quantrell’s [sic] guerillas, whom they had recently jo
2 CONC ined.  The relatives of these unfortunate men contend that they had not j
2 CONC oined any guerilla band, and did not intend to, but they were making th
2 CONC eir way to the Confederate army.  On the night of the 29th of July, abo
2 CONC ut midnight, a party of soldiers in Marshall, without the knowledge of t
2 CONC heir officers, took Grove and Gilliam from the guard, with or without t
2 CONC he connivance of the guard, dragged them into the woods north of town, a
2 CONC nd there hung them.  The bodies of these two ill-fated men were found t
2 CONC he next morning, quite dead, and buried by the citizens.
2 CONT
2 CONT  
2 CONT It’s too bad they didn’t give the first name of the accused Grove, but i
2 CONC t must have been Robert, who is known to have died on 30 Jul 1863.  Qua
2 CONC ntrill’s Raiders, of course, was a famous/infamous guerilla group.
