1 NOTE From the Hotchkiss manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Wilson ... probably came to Virginia from Pennsylvania.  Robert b
2 CONC ought 461 acres of farm land in Augusta County in 1755 from his brother
2 CONC , who had acquired it ten years earlier.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Robert Wilson died in 1768 and left the property to two of William's ol
2 CONC der brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest information I have about Robert is that he bought land in A
2 CONC ugusta Co., VA, on 3 Jun 1755.  He bought the land from his brother, Sa
2 CONC muel, who had bought it in 1745.  (I’m not completely sure that Samuel W
2 CONC ilson was Robert’s brother, but Katherine Bushman thought he was.)  Aug
2 CONC usta County was only organized in 1745, and the deed for Samuel's purch
2 CONC ase is near the beginning of the first county deed book.  (Documents be
2 CONC fore that time are in Orange County records.)  Augusta County was huge a
2 CONC t the time, stretching in theory from the Blue Ridge all the way to the M
2 CONC ississippi River.  However, Robert’s land was in the core part of the c
2 CONC ounty, south and a little west of Staunton, which is the county seat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augusta County was not a safe place at the time.  According to "Annals o
2 CONC f Augusta County, Virginia, from 1726 to 1871" by Joseph A. Waddell (Se
2 CONC cond Edition, C. Russell Caldwell, Staunton, VA, 1902; obtained from ht
2 CONC tp://www.archive.org/details/annalsofaugustac00wadd), the Shenandoah Va
2 CONC lley was not inhabited at the time European settlers started arriving t
2 CONC here in the 1720s.  Quoting an earlier author, he says "This delightful r
2 CONC egion of country was then only used as a hunting ground, and as a highw
2 CONC ay for belligerent parties of different nations, in their military expe
2 CONC ditions against each other.  In consequence of the almost continuous ho
2 CONC stilities between the northern and southern Indians, these expeditions w
2 CONC ere very frequent, and tended somewhat to retard the settlement of the V
2 CONC alley, and render a residence in it, for some time, insecure and unplea
2 CONC sant."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A major pathway for immigration to the Shenandoah Valley and beyond was c
2 CONC alled the Great Valley Road.  From "Map Guide to American Migration Rou
2 CONC tes, 1735-1815" by William Dollarhide (Heritage Quest, Bountiful, UT, 1
2 CONC 997):
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 1744, the “Great Warrior Path” was an Indian trading path from N
2 CONC ew York to the Carolinas.  This trail marked the western frontier of th
2 CONC e colonies, and no white man ventured across that line without fear of a
2 CONC ttacks from hostile Indians.  In 1744, when a treaty with the Indians g
2 CONC ave whites total control of the area east of the Great Warrior Path in V
2 CONC irginia, the way was clear for the path to evolve into the most heavily t
2 CONC raveled road in colonial America.  By the end of the 1740s, the Great V
2 CONC alley Road was the scene of large migrations into the wilderness of wes
2 CONC tern Virginia, beginning at the Shenandoah Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the period 1745-1775, thousands of immigrants used this road.  M
2 CONC any of them were Scotch-Irish families who had sailed from Ireland to P
2 CONC hiladelphia or Alexandria….
2 CONT
2 CONT The general route of the Great Valley Road today is called U.S. Highway 1
2 CONC 1 (or I-81) and is very easy to locate on a modern map….
2 CONT
2 CONT  
2 CONT Beginning in the 1730s, the government of the Colony of Virginia was en
2 CONC couraging settlement in the area that would become Augusta County to fo
2 CONC rm a buffer between the Native Americans (and the French) and the more s
2 CONC ettled parts of Virginia east of the Blue Ridge Mountains.  The danger i
2 CONC ncreased quite a bit after the French and Indian War started, and espec
2 CONC ially after the defeat of British General Edward Braddock at what is no
2 CONC w Pittsburgh, PA, on 9 Jul1755 -- just a month after Robert bought his l
2 CONC and.  Most of the action in the French and Indian War was farther north
2 CONC , but there were a number of raids and even massacres in Augusta County
2 CONC .  "Annals of Augusta County" has a long discussion of events in the "I
2 CONC ndian Wars" from 1753 to 1764.  Included are some incidents involving W
2 CONC ilsons, but they are not known to be related to Robert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the early settlers of Augusta County were from Scots-Irish fami
2 CONC lies who had initially settled in Pennsylvania (as noted above), so spe
2 CONC culation is that Robert and Samuel might have been born in Pennsylvania o
2 CONC r in Northern Ireland.  (The Scots-Irish, or Scotch-Irish, were people w
2 CONC ho had settled in Northern Ireland from Scotland and northern England, p
2 CONC rimarily of Presbyterian faith; see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotc
2 CONC h-Irish_Americans.)  There is some backing for this theory in the biogr
2 CONC aphy of Adair Wilson in "Portrait and Biographical Record of Denver and V
2 CONC icinity, Colorado", which says “His paternal great-grandfather emigrate
2 CONC d from Ireland to the United States and after a short sojourn in Pennsy
2 CONC lvania went to the Shenandoah Valley, of Virginia, where he was engaged a
2 CONC s a planter until his death.”  Note, however, that this biography also s
2 CONC ays that Adair’s paternal great-grandfather was a brother of James Wils
2 CONC on, the signer of the Declaration of Independence—but James Wilson was f
2 CONC rom Scotland.  This suggests another possibility, that Robert could hav
2 CONC e been born in Scotland, but it also undercuts the credibility of the A
2 CONC dair Wilson biography unless Robert moved from Scotland to Northern Ire
2 CONC land to Pennsylvania to Virginia.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Stories that our Wilson family was related to James Wilson, signer of t
2 CONC he Declaration of Independence and one of the authors of the Constituti
2 CONC on, are fairly widespread.  It was just mentioned above that an Adair W
2 CONC ilson biography said his paternal great-grandfather (Robert Wilson) was a b
2 CONC rother of James Wilson.  Leonie Rinker, in a letter to me, said that Ja
2 CONC mes Wilson “was a brother of a many times great grandfather of yours.”  M
2 CONC ollie Turner Reid, in the Reid manuscript, said that James Wilson was a h
2 CONC alf-brother of a grand or great-grandfather of D.W. Reid (this would be W
2 CONC illiam or Robert Wilson).  "Portrait and Biographical Record of Lafayet
2 CONC te and Saline Counties, Missouri", in the biography of Robert H. Wilson
2 CONC , said that his grandfather (this would be William Wilson) was a half-b
2 CONC rother of James Wilson.  Benjamin Harrison Wilson, in a letter written a
2 CONC round 1895, also said that his grandfather, who would be William, was a h
2 CONC alf-brother of James Wilson.  The Foster/Reid Family Tree has Robert Wi
2 CONC lson as a half-brother of James Wilson.  This version is the most plaus
2 CONC ible, but Page Smith’s biography of James Wilson says nothing about Jam
2 CONC es having a brother named Robert or having any half-brothers.  This doe
2 CONC sn’t necessarily mean he didn’t, but I don't know of any evidence for i
2 CONC t.  Another possibility is that James was some sort of cousin to Robert
2 CONC , rather than a brother or half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is my attempt to transcribe Robert's will (which is in Augusta Cou
2 CONC nty Will Book 4, pp. 161-163; originally obtained from James Hotchkiss)
2 CONC .  I have added punctuation to improve readability; the original has al
2 CONC most no punctuation marks.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God Amen, I, Robert Wilson, of the County of Augusta in t
2 CONC he Colony of Virginia, Farmer, being sick and weak of body but of Perfe
2 CONC ct mind & memory thanks to Almighty God, & Calling to mind the Mortalli
2 CONC ty of my body, do make this my last Will in manner & form following tha
2 CONC t is to Say Principally.  I recommend my soul to god & my Body I Commit
2 CONC t to the dust to be buried in a Christian like & decent Manner, & as to
2 CONC uching such worldly estate where with it hath pleased God to bless me I g
2 CONC ive, devise, & bequeath as follows.  Imprimis: I give & bequeath to my s
2 CONC on David two hundred & Sixty acres of the land I live on, with the hous
2 CONC es and other Improvements, & order him to give a Sufficient Maintenance t
2 CONC o my Wife out of it during her life and to take care of my son William a
2 CONC nd daughter Littice till they see reason to remove from this or till th
2 CONC eir Marriage, and also to pay to my Daughter Ann Twenty Pounds at her M
2 CONC arriage.  Item: I give & bequeath to my son Robert the Remainder of my L
2 CONC and, Viz. two hundred acres & Order him to Pay my son William Forty pou
2 CONC nds when he Arrives to the age of Twenty-one Years.  Lastly, I order al
2 CONC l my House hold Goods & Stock of Horses, Cows, hogs, and sheep to be eq
2 CONC ually divided Betwixt my Wife and my sons Robert, David, Ann, & Lettice & m
2 CONC y Sons Thomas, Samuel, and James having already had their Shares from m
2 CONC e I order them five Shillings each [without punctuation, this is ambigu
2 CONC ous, but I'm pretty sure the first group is sharing the livestock, etc.
2 CONC , and Thomas, Samuel, and James are getting five shillings each (having a
2 CONC lready had their shares)] & I appoint my Wife Mary my Executrix & I do h
2 CONC ereby revoak & disanul all other former Wills made by me heretofore, ra
2 CONC tifying and Confirming this to be my last Will.  In witness whereof I h
2 CONC ave hereunto set my hand & Seal this 9th September 1768.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Wilson, his mark
2 CONT
2 CONT Sealed & delivered in the presence of Robert Mitchell, John Tate Senr., J
2 CONC ohn Tate Junr.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a Court contd. & held for Augusta County Novr. the 16th 1768, This L
2 CONC ast Will and Testament of Robert Wilson dec'ed was proved by the Oaths o
2 CONC f John Tate and John Tate Junr., two of the witnesses thereto, and Orde
2 CONC red to be Recorded and on the motion of Mary Wilson the Executrix -- na
2 CONC me in the said Will, who made Oath According to Law, Certificate is Gra
2 CONC nted her for Obtaining a probat thereof in due form, she having with Se
2 CONC curities entered into & Acknowledged their bond According to Law.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is additional text about the administration of the will, but it's n
2 CONC ot particularly interesting.  Thanks to William J. Wilson for help deci
2 CONC phering the will.  Note that Robert’s will was dated 9 Sep 1768 and was p
2 CONC roved on 16 Nov 1768, so he died sometime between those dates.
