1 NOTE He was born Noah Eastman Ford, but after deserting from the U.S. Army w
2 CONC hen he was young, he assumed the identity of his brother Henry, who had d
2 CONC ied.  He played an important part in the California Bear Flag Revolt of 1
2 CONC 846, and there is a great deal about him in a series of articles by Fre
2 CONC d B. Rogers titled "Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of Henry L. Fo
2 CONC rd (1822-1860), with Some Related and Contemporary Art of Alexander Edo
2 CONC uart" in the California Historical Society Quarterly, Vol. 29 Nos. 2-4 a
2 CONC nd Vol. 30 Nos. 1-2 (1950-51).  He first met the Wilson family when he w
2 CONC as part of a rescue party helping them and other groups through the mou
2 CONC ntains of northern California in late fall and early winter of 1849.  H
2 CONC e had a ranch in Colusi Co., CA, and was elected to the state legislatu
2 CONC re from there.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the concluding section of "Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of H
2 CONC enry L. Ford (1822-1860), with Some Related and Contemporary Art of Ale
2 CONC xander Edouart (Concluded)" by Fred B. Rogers (California Historical So
2 CONC ciety Quarterly, Vol. 30, No. 2, pp. 157-175, June 1951):
2 CONT
2 CONT Ford entered the valley on July first, intending to drive some stock ba
2 CONC ck to his reservation.  The next morning at about nine o'clock, while t
2 CONC he saddle cover was being placed on his horse, a pistol attached to the h
2 CONC orn of the saddle was accidentally discharged, and the ball which struc
2 CONC k Captain Ford in the neck killed him almost instantly.
2 CONT
2 CONT There, in a small frontier burying-ground in the valley of Nome Cult, b
2 CONC etween the Sacramento and the Mendocino coast, was interred the body of H
2 CONC enry L. (Noah Eastman) Ford.
