1 NOTE Newspaper clipping (newspaper and date not identified) from Katherine C
2 CONC ooper's scrapbook in the John Wilson Papers (BANC MSS C-B 420, The Banc
2 CONC roft Library, University of California, Berkeley, CA):
2 CONT
2 CONT Peaceful Death of Mrs. Wakeman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Mary E. Wakeman, the mother of Mrs. Harry C. Cooper, died suddenly a
2 CONC bout three o'clock this morning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Wakeman has been living alone in her cottage at Seabright, surroun
2 CONC ded by the flowers she loved so well, and exchanging visits almost dail
2 CONC y with her daughter and family.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Monday evening she dined with the Coopers and was in her usual healt
2 CONC h, though she has for many years been aware that there was some trouble w
2 CONC ith her heart, and had often said that she expected and hoped to die su
2 CONC ddenly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Wakeman's death was discovered about 11 o'clock this forenoon and w
2 CONC hen a physician was called, it was his judgment that death had occurred a
2 CONC t 3 o'clock or soon after and was caused by heart failure.  As the door o
2 CONC f the cottage was found unlocked, it is believed that the heavy shock o
2 CONC f the powder mill explosion, which occurred about 2:45, aroused and fri
2 CONC ghtened her, that she went to the door to look out and, possibly, that t
2 CONC he shock and fright brought the end suddenly.  She was lying in bed, wi
2 CONC th no evidence of struggle or suffering in her attitude or expression.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grief and the suddenness of her loss have quite prostrated Mrs. Coo
2 CONC per, who was Mrs. Wakeman's only daughter, and whose three children wer
2 CONC e her only grandchildren
2 CONT
2 CONT The deceased was the daughter of Gen. John Wilson, who was appointed by P
2 CONC resident Zachary Taylor the first naval officer of San Francisco.  The f
2 CONC amily came to California in the early part of '49, when Mrs. Wakeman wa
2 CONC s a young girl, and in that city she married and became a widow.  She w
2 CONC as acquainted with nearly all the well-known Californians of early days a
2 CONC nd could relate many interesting reminiscences.  She and her daughter, t
2 CONC hen Mrs. Kate Dennis, came to Santa Cruz to reside over ten years ago a
2 CONC nd a few years later Mrs. Dennis married Harry C. Cooper.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Wakeman was a lady of genial disposition, exceedingly fond of flow
2 CONC ers and successful in their culture, and enjoyed to the utmost her quie
2 CONC t life and the daily intercourse with those she loved and by whom she w
2 CONC as beloved.  Her many friends and those of the Coopers will extend symp
2 CONC athy to them in their bereavement.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are questions about her birth year.  In the John Wilson Papers (B
2 CONC ANC MSS C-B 420, The Bancroft Library, University of California, Berkel
2 CONC ey, CA) there is a page of notes by Fred B. Rogers of dates taken from t
2 CONC he bible of Mary Eliza (Wilson) Wakeman.  It gives her birth date as 19 A
2 CONC pr 1836.  This is consistent with ages given in the 1852 California sta
2 CONC te census and the 1880 federal census.  However, in two places I have s
2 CONC een it said that she was 65 years old at her death (including a death n
2 CONC otice in the San Francisco Chronicle dated 29 Sep 1897).  This would ma
2 CONC ke her birth in about 1832.  I suppose the weight of evidence is for 18
2 CONC 36 and the age 65 at death is wrong.
