2 CONT Some harsh words in an 1871 letter written by John Wilson (Ferdinand's f
2 CONC ather-in-law), as quoted in the Hotchkiss manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT He had the reputation of being a moral businessman and had been well ra
2 CONC ised in Philadelphia.  He came here with two brothers who then were als
2 CONC o clever moral businessmen.  Both afterwards became drunkards.  Of cour
2 CONC se, the family remained with us.  Mary Eliza never left us.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Gradually, all three brothers took to drink to excess.  One of them is a
2 CONC lready dead as a drunken vagabond.  The third is on the verge of the gr
2 CONC ave from the same cause.  F. O. continued to get worse and worse till a
2 CONC bout three years ago I told him he could not stay here at my charge any l
2 CONC onger.  He left then and has been some times in the police stations, so
2 CONC metimes in the Home of Inebriate, and is now in prison on a charge of f
2 CONC orgery.  I have just been to see some influential friends to see if the
2 CONC y can not aid in getting him out that he may leave the country.  His cr
2 CONC ime, if he really committed it, was his drunkenness.
