2 CONC ted that way in the bible record in Fred B. Rogers' notes in the John W
2 CONC ilson Papers (BANC MSS C-B 420, The Bancroft Library, University of Cal
2 CONC ifornia, Berkeley, CA).  "Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of Henry L
2 CONC . Ford (1822-1860), with Some Related and Contemporary Art of Alexander E
2 CONC douart (Concluded)" by Fred B. Rogers (California Historical Society Qu
2 CONC arterly, Vol. 30, No. 2, pp. 157-175, June 1951) gives her name as Kath
2 CONC erine Wakeman Cooper.  This is also her byline on an article in The Gri
2 CONC zzly Bear magazine, Nov 1912, about the Rincon Hill neighborhood in San F
2 CONC rancisco where she grew up, and an article in The Overland Monthly, Jan
2 CONC -Jun 1917, about Patty Reed of the Donner Party (both available from Go
2 CONC ogle Books).  Her first name is spelled Katharine in the 1920 census.  S
2 CONC he is listed as Kate W. Cooper in the 1900 & 1910 censuses and Kate W. D
2 CONC ennis in the 1880 census.  She is listed in the 1860 census as Kate J. W
2 CONC akeman.  In the 1870 census, her listing also looks like Kate J. Wakema
2 CONC n, but that J. could possibly be an I.  I don't think there's any doubt t
2 CONC hat the middle initial in 1860 is J.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Santa Cruz Evening News, 20 Feb 1929, p. 10 (obtained from Newspapers.c
2 CONC om):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Kate Cooper, wife of Harry C. Cooper, and sister-in-law of Frank P
2 CONC . Cooper, and a former well known resident of Santa Cruz, passed away i
2 CONC n Oakland yesterday, according to a message received here yesterday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Cooper was born in San Francisco of a prominent old California fam
2 CONC ily.  She came to Santa Cruz about 45 years ago, and was very active in s
2 CONC ocial life here.  She was a past president of the Native Daughters of t
2 CONC he Golden West, past worthy matron of the Order of the Eastern Star, an
2 CONC d a member of the Daughters of the American Revolution.  Mrs. Cooper al
2 CONC so was a charter member and past president of the Saturday Afternoon cl
2 CONC ub.
2 CONT
2 CONT For many years Mrs. Cooper was active in the work of the Calvary church
2 CONC , in both the Ivy guild and the St. Agnes guild.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Coopers moved to Oakland some eyars [sic] ago and have resided in t
2 CONC hat city ever since.  The deceased leaves her husband Harry C. Cooper, t
2 CONC wo sons Collis Dennis of Vancouver, B. C., and George Dennis, and a dau
2 CONC ghter Mrs. Helen Dingwall of Portland, Oregon.
2 CONT
2 CONT [This obituary is incorrect that she died "yesterday".  Newspapers.com a
2 CONC lso has the 19 Feb 1929 issue of the Oakland Tribune, which has a short o
2 CONC bituary on p. 46 stating that she died on February 17.]
