1 NOTE From a letter by John Wilson, quoted in the Hotchkiss manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT We that remain of our family are all in good health, but the prettiest a
2 CONC nd smartest child of her age (5 years 4 months) ever produced in any of o
2 CONC ur families, Maude Campbell Wakeman, died about two months ago.  Playin
2 CONC g in our yard with her little associates, how or why we know not, her c
2 CONC lothes got on fire from matches at about 1 P.M., and before any of the f
2 CONC amily knew it, her clothes were literally burned off her.  Several doct
2 CONC ors were on the ground in a few minutes, who did all they could, but at h
2 CONC alf past 7 P.M. she fell as it were on sleep, seemingly without pain, a
2 CONC nd was dead and her spirit in heaven before the sun's rays had fully wi
2 CONC thdrawn themselves from the tops of the Sierras.  This of all others ha
2 CONC s been our most terrible misfortune of our lives, which has certainly f
2 CONC urnished us many, too many misfortunes to name.  This will be with us a
2 CONC lways.  She possessed the most remarkable matured mind.  She was the pr
2 CONC eferred leader of all her associates of her own age and was a welcome a
2 CONC ssociate of all who knew her of the girls of 15 or 16 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Had death in his ordinary rounds taken her off, our grief would have be
2 CONC en almost inconsolable.  As it is, nothing will assuage our grief till w
2 CONC e follow after.
