1 NOTE James Hotchkiss listed John Wilson's first wife as McCoun [first name n
2 CONC ot known], with no date or place for the marriage.  He listed her death a
2 CONC s 1831, which does not seem likely.  John's second marriage was in 1817
2 CONC , and chances are that his first wife died before then.
2 CONT
2 CONT A record on Ancestry.com in the California Pioneer and Immigrant Files, 1
2 CONC 790-1950 lists McCoun [no first name] as the first wife of General John W
2 CONC ilson and says that the date of marriage is "Uncertain.  About 1812 or b
2 CONC efore."  For place of marriage, it says "Supposedly Virginia.  Maybe Au
2 CONC gusta Co.", with "Staunton" written above "Augusta Co."
2 CONT
2 CONT A very short biography of John Wilson at http://www.josephsmithpapers.o
2 CONC rg/person/john-wilson?highlight=John%20Wilson says that his first wife w
2 CONC as Percilla McGowen (McCoun).  Their source is John Wilson's War of 181
2 CONC 2 pension papers from the National Archives (note that this is on a for
2 CONC m filled out by John's widow, Ann, after he was dead, so it wouldn't be s
2 CONC urprising if it's not quite right).
2 CONT
2 CONT McCoun genealogies list a Priscilla McCoun/Maccoun who married a John W
2 CONC ilson on 1 Dec 1812 in Mercer Co., KY (see "James McCoun & Margaret Wal
2 CONC ker & Their Descendants" by Maurie McCoun Klimcheck, self-published, 19
2 CONC 93; selected pages copied from microfilm obtained from FamilySearch.org
2 CONC ; in particular, this marriage is listed on p. 218 of Volume II).  This w
2 CONC as unexpected, as I have no other information that John lived in Kentuc
2 CONC ky for a time.  However, it seems to fit, so I am provisionally taking t
2 CONC his Priscilla McCoun to be John's first wife.  Priscilla was the daught
2 CONC er of John McCoun and Elizabeth Tilford.  Another bit of information in
2 CONC dicating that this might be a good fit is that Priscilla is known to ha
2 CONC ve had sons David and John T. Wilson.  We know from John Wilson's will t
2 CONC hat he had sons David and John, and James Hotchkiss indicated that they w
2 CONC ere from his first marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Klimcheck book, on pp. 172-178 of Vol. II, has quite a bit about le
2 CONC gal challenges over the estate of Priscilla's father, John McCoun, who d
2 CONC ied intestate in Dec 1826.  On p. 173 is a transcription of a document d
2 CONC ated 14 Apr 1828, which was signed by (among others) "Lanty Holman, gua
2 CONC rdian to the heirs of Priscilla Wilson and John Maccoun, Jr."  The "hei
2 CONC rs of Priscilla Wilson" having a guardian seems to imply that their fat
2 CONC her had also died, which would mean that this was the wrong John Wilson
2 CONC , since our John Wilson lived until 1877.  However, additional informat
2 CONC ion on this can be found on Mercer County guardian bonds dated 7 May 18
2 CONC 27 for David Wilson and John Tilford Wilson, appointing Lanty Holman th
2 CONC eir guardian (copies obtained from the Harrodsburg Historical Society).  T
2 CONC he wording is a little different on the two bonds (one for David and on
2 CONC e for John), but with the same meaning.  The one for David says that La
2 CONC nty Holman was being appointed a Special Guardian "for the purpose of t
2 CONC aking care of his estate descended to him from his grandfather John McC
2 CONC oun, Dec'd".  Each bond describes the child as an "orphan son of John W
2 CONC ilson, Dec'd", but with the "orphan" and the "Dec'd" crossed out.  I th
2 CONC ink it is reasonable to conclude from this that their father, John Wils
2 CONC on, was not dead.  At this time, John Wilson Sr. was long gone from Mer
2 CONC cer County, living in Howard Co., MO.  My guess is that someone local w
2 CONC as needed in Mercer County to represent the interests of the boys in se
2 CONC ttling their grandfather's estate, so Lanty Holman was not a guardian i
2 CONC n the sense of the person who raised them, but rather was a legal repre
2 CONC sentative.  I do not know whether David and John T. were living with Jo
2 CONC hn Sr. at this time, or perhaps with some other family member(s).  Howe
2 CONC ver, it is known that David was in Howard Co., MO, as of 1835.  See the n
2 CONC otes on David for more about this.  (By the way, we see this type of gu
2 CONC ardian elsewhere in the family as well.  After David S. Wilson, the bro
2 CONC ther of John Wilson Sr., was dead, a legal challenge arose over the est
2 CONC ate of a brother of David's wife, Margaret Kinnear Wilson.  James Reid, w
2 CONC ho was the husband of David's daughter Eliza Jane, was named guardian o
2 CONC f the minor children of David and Margaret for purposes of representati
2 CONC on in the legal challenge.  Margaret was still alive and raising the ch
2 CONC ildren, as can be seen from census records.)
