2 DATE 19 JUL 2017
1 NOTE "One Hundred Twenty-Three Biographies of Deceased Faculty Members", com
2 CONC piled by P. O. Shelby, Northeast Missouri State Teachers College, Kirks
2 CONC ville, MO, 1963, pp. 77-78 (obtained from Karen Levin):
2 CONT
2 CONT WILSON, J. D.  Professor of Psychology, 1903-1912
2 CONT
2 CONT J. D. Wilson was born in Howard County, Mo., August 22, 1862, son of Da
2 CONC vid and Sarah Wakeman Wilson.  He attended a log school two or three we
2 CONC eks per year for eight years or more.  He entered the Kirksville State N
2 CONC ormal School, attending perhaps thirty months, graduating in 1886.  For e
2 CONC ach of four years he taught in the fall and attended the Normal School f
2 CONC or the remainder of the year.  He belonged to the Zetosophian Literary S
2 CONC ociety and served as secretary, treasurer, and president.  He attended t
2 CONC he University of Michigan for a year and later took special work for tw
2 CONC o years at the University of Missouri.  The Normal School gave him the d
2 CONC egree of bachelor of scientific didactics.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was superintendent of schools at Cameron for two years and principal o
2 CONC f the high school at Sedalia for ten years.  He joined the faculty at K
2 CONC irksville in 1903 and remained till 1912.  He was a teacher of Educatio
2 CONC n, but his subject matter was chiefly psychology.  He mastered the scie
2 CONC nce of hypnotism and often gave amateur shows around the campus.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left Kirksville to become superintendent of the Missouri Training Sc
2 CONC hool for Boys at Boonville, but remained there only a year.  He then wa
2 CONC s county agricultural agent for Cooper County for two years and then he
2 CONC ld a similar position at Bentonville, Arkansas, for one year.  During W
2 CONC orld I he was a lecturer at Camp Pike, Arkansas.  He moved to Memphis, T
2 CONC ennessee, where he was principal of Pope School for six years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Professor Wilson married Miss Clara Carroll on June 18, 1890.  They had s
2 CONC ix children: Edna (Mrs. Charles A. Walz), Mabel (Mrs. Virgil D. Wall), C
2 CONC lara (Mrs. R. K. Brommer), Carroll (Mrs. Wier), John and Sam.  He belon
2 CONC ged to the Christian Church and the Republican Party.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at Memphis, Tenn., February 6, 1934, and was buried in Forest H
2 CONC ill Cemetery, Memphis.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of the bio isn't quite right.  First, it should say he attended a l
2 CONC og school two or three months per year (not weeks).  Karen Levin also s
2 CONC ent me a questionnaire that he apparently filled out himself by hand, w
2 CONC hich much of this bio is based on, and his answer to "How long did you a
2 CONC ttend each [school]?" was "Two or three months in the year for 8 or 10 y
2 CONC rs."  Second, as far as I know, his mother's name was not Wakeman.
