2 DATE 3 JUL 2018
1 NOTE "History of Buchanan County, Missouri", Steam Printing Company, St. Jos
2 CONC eph, Missouri, 1881 (obtained from http://genforum.genealogy.com/mo/buc
2 CONC hanan/messages/715.html):
2 CONT
2 CONT R. L. McDonald was born near Harrisburg, Kentucky, May 19, 1832. His fa
2 CONC ther was a farmer of that neighborhood, and here the subject of our ske
2 CONC tch resided till he was nineteen years of age, when he left his home fo
2 CONC r the West, and settled in St. Joseph, Missouri, where he engaged as a c
2 CONC lerk in the store of Donnell & Saxton. In 1853, he was received as a pa
2 CONC rtner in the business. A short time previous to the breaking out of the c
2 CONC ivil war, he purchased the interest of his partners, and became sole pr
2 CONC oprietor of the concern, a general retail dry goods house, then located o
2 CONC n the corner of Fourth and Felix Streets. In 1865, he associated with h
2 CONC imself C. H. Buckman and F. L. McLain, and under the name and style of R
2 CONC . L. McDonald & Co., engaged in the wholesale trade. In 1867, F. L. McL
2 CONC ain retired from the partnership for the purpose of engaging in the ret
2 CONC ail business alone. He was succeeded by George Henderson. In 1872, Colo
2 CONC nel J. H. Dayton became a member of the firm. In January, 1877, Dayton a
2 CONC nd Buckman both retired from the partnership, and W. A. P. McDonald bec
2 CONC ame a partner. Since that period, this gentleman and George Henderson, J
2 CONC r. have composed the Co. of the concern. In December, 1880, the elegant s
2 CONC tructure of the present business house of this firm, a brick faced with c
2 CONC ut stone, on the northwest corner of Fourth and Francis streets, was co
2 CONC mpleted and occupied. This is the largest structure of the kind in the c
2 CONC ity, having a frontage of one hundred feet on Fourth Street, running ba
2 CONC ck one hundred and forty feet on Francis Street, and is four stories hi
2 CONC gh, with an elegantly lighted basement, opening on the grade of Francis S
2 CONC treet. The floor space of the entire building includes 70,000 square fe
2 CONC et. The stories are of unusual height, the basement being twelve feet, t
2 CONC he first floor twenty feet, the second seventeen feet, the third sixtee
2 CONC n, and the fourth story twelve feet. This house, the largest west of St
2 CONC . Louis, distributes its sales over twelve states and territories, empl
2 CONC oying constantly about twenty traveling salesmen, besides the sixty of s
2 CONC eventy engaged in the house. R. L. McDonald was married May 16, 1857, t
2 CONC o Miss Mary A. Wilson, daughter of General Robert Wilson, formerly Unit
2 CONC ed States Senator from Missouri, and niece of Armstrong Beattie, deceas
2 CONC ed. They have had seven children. Of these, John, Maggie, Mattie, Rober
2 CONC t, Annie and Marie survive.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some interesting history in the National Register of Historic P
2 CONC laces Registration Form for Walnut Park Farm Historic District (1999; o
2 CONC btained July 2017 from Missouri Department of Natural Resources web sit
2 CONC e, http://dnr.mo.gov/shpo/nps-nr/99001597.pdf), including this bit:
2 CONT
2 CONT As a pioneer St.Joseph businessman in 1864, McDonald assembled and disp
2 CONC atched to California a wagon train of supplies and around a hundred you
2 CONC ng mules bought on-the-cheap.  McDonald's brother-in-law James Wilson w
2 CONC as in charge of the wagon train, which McDonald joined in Salt Lake Cit
2 CONC y after traveling there by stagecoach.  McDonald accompanied the proces
2 CONC sion to San Francisco where the mules, now fully grown, were sold to th
2 CONC e U.S. government at a large profit.  With the proceeds, McDonald trave
2 CONC led via the Panama Canal [sic -- the Panama Canal did not yet exist at t
2 CONC hat time (comment by MLW)] to New York City where he purchased vast qua
2 CONC ntities of new merchandise for his store.  The entire episode was "high
2 CONC ly successful...though one fraught [mainly the trek to California (comm
2 CONC ent in original)] with much danger."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some information about Walnut Park Farm in the notes for Marvi
2 CONC n Middleton Davis (husband of Rufus’s daughter Annette).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate is Rufus Lee McDonald.  Can't read ca
2 CONC use of death.  Place of death was at home (811 North 9th).
