1 NOTE Excerpt from a writeup by Anna Larens Wilson (obtained from Todd Adair W
2 CONC ilson; a few obvious typographical errors have been corrected):
2 CONT  
2 CONT <u>REMEMBRANCES OF MY SISTER DELLA RUTH LARENS WILSON
2 CONT </u> 
2 CONT Della Ruth Larens Wilson was born to Alma Louise Smith Larens, and Harv
2 CONC ey Nicholas Larens on April 16, 1911.  She was a beautiful baby, and gr
2 CONC ew into a lovely young girl and woman.  She was blessed with a very mil
2 CONC d disposition, and caring nature, and loved by everyone.  Her mother al
2 CONC ways said she was such an easy child to raise.  Ruth was a little mothe
2 CONC r to her sister Anna and brother Martin, who were two and four years he
2 CONC r junior.  The life in the Larens household went very smoothly the firs
2 CONC t seven years of Ruth’s life.  Then at seven, her father was called int
2 CONC o the Congregational Church as a minister.  So this new job entailed a m
2 CONC ove to another town.  The family rode in the caboose of a train on movi
2 CONC ng day.  The furniture in a freight car.  Ruth was so delighted over th
2 CONC e big two story manse we moved into.
2 CONT  
2 CONT No matter how large the house or small, Ruth and Anna always slept toge
2 CONC ther.  So there was one room we didn’t use upstairs.  We found many hat
2 CONC s in the closet, so we called it the hat room, and Ruth and Anna spent m
2 CONC any happy hours playing dressup in that room, and trying on all the hat
2 CONC s, left by former minister’s wives.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Then on September 12, 1918 Ruth’s dear papa, as we called him, and who h
2 CONC ad been ill for several days with a nurse in a white uniform attending h
2 CONC im, slipped downstairs while Ruth’s mother, Sister and brother and hers
2 CONC elf slept on the floor in the large entrance hall.  The stairway went u
2 CONC p from that.  Papa slipped by us to a coat closet and got his rifle, an
2 CONC d quietly returned upstairs without waking us.  Then we were all awaken
2 CONC ed by a terrible blast that we never forgot.  Ruth’s mother ran upstair
2 CONC s and found papa dead in the bathroom.  The nurse had gone home that ni
2 CONC ght, and we were on the floor downstairs so we wouldn’t disturb him as w
2 CONC e got ready for bed.  Ruth’s mother could never talk about this, so she g
2 CONC rew up never knowing what illness her papa had.
2 CONT  
2 CONT This tragic happening naturally caused chaos in the life of Ruth and he
2 CONC r family.  Her Uncle George O. Smith, who was Superintendent of the Pri
2 CONC nceton Schools of Bureau County, and brother to her mother, came immedi
2 CONC ately.  He took Ruth, her sister and brother to Grandma Smith’s home in D
2 CONC e Pue, Ill.  Grandma Smith was Ruth’s mother’s mother.  Ruth watched ov
2 CONC er her younger sister and brother, and helped them in their confusion.  G
2 CONC randma Smith had a big two story house, and at this time she took in bo
2 CONC arders and roomers.  Grandpa Smith had been dead for many years.  Aunt N
2 CONC ora O’Byrne, who was Ruth’s mother’s sister and had a husband and 6 chi
2 CONC ldren, and lived in a little house up on DePue Hill.  To help Grandma S
2 CONC mith out, Ruth and her siblings were sent to Aunt Nora’s.  Ruth’s mothe
2 CONC r came in a few days, and so did the body of papa, which was laid in st
2 CONC ate in the living room.  These were awe filled days for Ruth and her si
2 CONC blings.
2 CONT  
2 CONT So many people we didn’t know came, and Ruth was the little mother watc
2 CONC hing over her 5 yr. old sister, and 2 yr. old brother.  The Congregatio
2 CONC nal Church, that Ruth’s mother grew up in was just two doors from her G
2 CONC randma’s home.  Then came the day when the funeral took place.  There w
2 CONC ere so many people.  At the graveside, again Ruth was the little mother
2 CONC , taking care of her siblings.  In a few days Ruth and the other two ch
2 CONC ildren had the comfort of their mother, as we all shared one big bedroo
2 CONC m.  Ruth and Anna in one bed, and little Martin in bed with his mother.
2 CONC   The family drew very close together as life began without papa.  Ruth
2 CONC ’s mother had to go to work.  She was a teacher, but the school year ha
2 CONC d already started.  She worked on the night shift at the Zinc Plant, an
2 CONC d had to wear kaki knickers, which was very brazen for women in 1918.  R
2 CONC uth started school, which was just around the corner from her Grandma’s
2 CONC .  Ruth had a close friend, Grace Charles, who lived right across the s
2 CONC treet, and they spent many happy hours together.  Her parents were divo
2 CONC rced, and she lived with her grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christmas came, and everyone was ill with the great flu of 1918.  Grand
2 CONC ma and mother escaped it, but Grandma helped other people who needed ca
2 CONC re.  Ruth and Anna had it, and were just recovering from it on Christma
2 CONC s Eve.  They were allowed to eat dinner with the family for the first t
2 CONC ime in a week or more.  As we sat at the table, Ruth said she heard jin
2 CONC gle bells.  There was someone at the door shouting “Merry Christmas, Me
2 CONC rry Christmas”, and who should come in but Santa himself laden with a b
2 CONC ig bag of toys over his shoulder.  He said, “HO, Ho, HO,” and opened th
2 CONC e bag and left many gifts of toys and food.  As he left Ruth and all th
2 CONC e family ran to the door, and there in the snow was a sleigh with jingl
2 CONC e bells, and Santa driving away laughing.
2 CONT  
2 CONT World War One was over Nov. 11, 1918, and Ruth’s Uncle Jesse was coming h
2 CONC ome soon after Christmas, and the house was a buzz with excitement.  Th
2 CONC ere was a blackboard hanging on one Wall of the dining room where Ruth a
2 CONC nd Anna used to draw pictures of the Kaiser, and our soldier boys at wa
2 CONC r.  Ruth had us all excited about Uncle Jesse coming home and maybe bri
2 CONC nging a gas mask.  The day arrived, and he did have a gas mask, a horri
2 CONC ble looking object.  He stayed with us a couple of weeks, then left for C
2 CONC alifornia to find work.  He worked on the roads at Yellowstone before g
2 CONC oing to California where he did the same line of work.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Ruth started piano lessons, and progressed rapidly.  She had lessons fo
2 CONC r about two and a half years.
2 CONT  
2 CONT When Ruth was 8 yrs. old Grandma Smith died.  Consequently Uncle Con an
2 CONC d Aunt Nora, and their six children moved in Grandma’s house with us, s
2 CONC o Aunt Nora could care for Ruth, Anna and Martin, while Ruth’s mother w
2 CONC orked.  This was a lively household with 9 children, and the oldest 16 y
2 CONC rs. of age.  When Ruth was 9 yrs. old, her mother was called to teach i
2 CONC n the Bureau Elementary School, 6 miles from De Pue.  Ruth, her mother a
2 CONC nd siblings first lived in a big duplex near the railroad tracks.  Bure
2 CONC au was a town of 300 population.  The next year a little house next to t
2 CONC he school was for rent, so Ruth’s family moved there.  In the fall blac
2 CONC k walnuts were gathered in the woods, and we would have a picnic the fo
2 CONC ur of us, and it was an enjoyable time.  Sunday, all through the years, w
2 CONC as a time to go to Sunday School, Church then back to church in the eve
2 CONC ning for that service.  Martin had a little red wagon, and Ruth and Ann
2 CONC a took turns pulling the wagon with Martin asleep in it after the eveni
2 CONC ng service.  We were happy to do this, as great love existed among the f
2 CONC our of us.  In the winter the entire town on a Friday or Saturday night w
2 CONC ent to the Town Pond that was frozen over and ice skated.  We would tak
2 CONC e hot dogs and roast them in the huge fire on the shore the men in the t
2 CONC own had built.  These were happy times, and Ruth’s mother had the entir
2 CONC e town loving her and her family.  Ruth made friends with a girl on a f
2 CONC arm, and they delivered milk to the homes of Bureau.  Ruth was thrilled e
2 CONC very time she was asked to accompany her friend Mavis on the milk wagon
2 CONC , and deliver milk to the homes.  Of course the milk wagon was pulled b
2 CONC y a horse.  Ruth and her siblings spent many happy hours at the Spute f
2 CONC arm playing in the barn, the fields, and riding ponies.
2 CONT  
2 CONT One day in summer, Ruth’s mother had to go to a Teacher’s Institute in P
2 CONC rinceton.  She had to leave early in the morning on the inter urban, or s
2 CONC treet car.  No one had cars then except the wealthy.  Ruth had to get b
2 CONC reakfast for her sister and brother, and she cooked hot cereal on our k
2 CONC erosene stove.  When she turned the burner off it didn’t go out, not kn
2 CONC owing it burned until all the kerosene burned, so she poured water on t
2 CONC he stove, and it did go out.  When her mother returned she told Ruth ho
2 CONC w to operate the stove.  I guess she thought she knew from observation.
2 CONT  
2 CONT In October 1922, Ruth’s mother was diagnosed as having tuberculosis.  H
2 CONC er only symptom was the loss of her voice.  Nevertheless, after being c
2 CONC hecked and rechecked, she was sent to a T. B. Sanitarium in Springfield
2 CONC , Ill. where they kept her for 1 yr.  Uncle George and Aunt Ella took c
2 CONC are of Martin, who was 7 yrs old.  Anna lived with a Catholic family, w
2 CONC ho had the biggest farm in the Bureau area.  Ruth lived with a farmer a
2 CONC nd his wife who had no children, so she had many lonely days, although t
2 CONC he couple were very good to her.  Ruth would see her sister Anna at sch
2 CONC ool, and that was her only contact with family.  In October 1923 when R
2 CONC uth’s mother was declared free of T. B. Uncle Jesse contacted her, and i
2 CONC nvited her to come to California and he would make a home for her and h
2 CONC er children.  So October 1923 found Ruth, her mother, sister and brothe
2 CONC r on a train headed for California.  We arrived in Roseville, Ca on Oct
2 CONC . 23, 1923.  Uncle Jesse met the family, and drove them to Red Bluff, C
2 CONC a.  Where he had a duplex he rented.  He was working for the State High
2 CONC way Dept. on the road to Mineral.  In November he changed jobs, and mov
2 CONC ed the family to Redding, Ca. where he became a car salesman for Star A
2 CONC uto Sales.  Ruth was 12 years old, Anna 10 and Martin 8.
2 CONT  
2 CONT The family lived in an apartment in the Del Monte.  The next summer the
2 CONC y moved into a duplex on Butte St. east of Market St.  Ruth and her sib
2 CONC lings attended Pine St. School in the fall of 1924.  In the other duple
2 CONC x lived the Kite family.  Ruth became fast friends withTeddy and Virgin
2 CONC ia Kite, and their friendship lasted through the years.  The next summe
2 CONC r of 1925 the family moved across the street into a 6 room house.  The f
2 CONC ormer landlord found Ruth, her siblings and neighbor friends playing mu
2 CONC mblety-peg on the lawn.  The next morning when Uncle Jesse went out to p
2 CONC ick up the paper he found carpet tacks strewn all over the yard.  It ma
2 CONC de him so angry, that he immediately moved the family in the house for r
2 CONC ent across the street.
2 CONT  
2 CONT The depression was beginning to be felt, and car sales went down to not
2 CONC hing.  Ruth’s mother was able to get an afternoon job at the Shasta Sun
2 CONC , a newspaper in Redding.  Her small salary kept us eating, and helped p
2 CONC ay the rent.  Uncle Jesse about this time fell in love with Margaret Mu
2 CONC rphy, a nurse.  He was courting her when Ruth’s mother felt she needed t
2 CONC o free him of his commitment to her.  She took the Teacher’s Exam, and a
2 CONC sked us all to be in prayer for her that she would pass it.  Well, need
2 CONC less to say many heartfelt prayers went up to God, and He heard them, a
2 CONC nd Ruth’s mother passed the exam, and was hired by the Round Mountain S
2 CONC chool District to teach the Round Mountain School.  It was a summer sch
2 CONC ool beginning in March.  So once again as a family, we were divided as R
2 CONC uth is now a freshman in high school.  She lived with the Shipley famil
2 CONC y, and worked for her room and board.  Ruth’s mother and siblings moved t
2 CONC o Round Mountain Mar. 1, 1926.  Ruth continued to stay in Redding, and v
2 CONC isited her mother and siblings during holidays.  She worked in the summ
2 CONC er at Wilson’s Confectionary, and Mc Call’s Soda Fountain.  In the summ
2 CONC er of 1928 Ruth’s mother took a teaching job in French Gulch so all the f
2 CONC amily could be together.  It was a joy to all be together again.  There w
2 CONC ere many young people in French Gulch, and we all rode the school bus t
2 CONC o Shasta Union High School.  Martin is still in grade school having his m
2 CONC other as his teacher.  All the young people congregated at our house, a
2 CONC nd I always thought my pretty sister Ruth was the attraction.  Great ti
2 CONC mes were had, and lots of fun.  Dances were held on many Saturday night
2 CONC s to benefit the school, so everyone went.  Ruth’s boy friends Henry Ro
2 CONC nlake and Adair Wilson would attend, and if there wasn’t a dance they’d c
2 CONC ome out Sunday to take her riding or take her into Redding.  Then in 19
2 CONC 29 due to a school board member wanting her relative to be the French G
2 CONC ulch Teacher, Ruth’s mother lost her job.  Luckily Kosk Creek School in B
2 CONC ig Bend was available, and it was a summer school, so in June the famil
2 CONC y moved to Big Bend, all but Ruth.  She lived with Aunt Margaret and Un
2 CONC cle Jesse that summer, and worked at Mc Call’s Soda Fountain.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Then in the fall Ruth lived at Gilzeans working for her board and room.
2 CONC   Ruth is now a senior in high school.  Anna is also working for her bo
2 CONC ard and room.  Ruth graduated from Shasta Union High School June 1929.  S
2 CONC he is now going steady with Adair Wilson, and she is working at Firth’s D
2 CONC ry Goods, and lived this summer with Aunt Margaret and Uncle Jesse.  In t
2 CONC he fall Ruth’s mother rented an apartment at the Del Monte, so Martin c
2 CONC ould go to high school.  Anna was working for her board and room with a f
2 CONC amily named Forrest.  After Christmas, however, Mother had all of us wi
2 CONC th her in the apartment until March when she resumed teaching at Kosk C
2 CONC reek, so Ruth took care of her 2 siblings in the apartment, and worked a
2 CONC t Firth’s.  The summer of 1930 Ruth’s mother rented another apartment i
2 CONC n the Brickwood Apts. near the high school, and Ruth was still working a
2 CONC t Firth’s, and lived there alone until her sister and brother joined he
2 CONC r in September to go to high school.  They had spent the summer with th
2 CONC eir mother in Big Bend.  The family lived there through the summer of 1
2 CONC 931, as Ruth’s mother was called to teach the school at Montgomery Cree
2 CONC k, and that school ran from September until June.  Adair and Ruth are s
2 CONC till going together, and as she is working in Redding, she didn’t come h
2 CONC ome very often due to no means of transportation.  On Sept.12, 1933 Rut
2 CONC h and Adair were married in Reno, Nevada.  In California you had to wai
2 CONC t 3 days to get a marriage license, in Reno you could get married immed
2 CONC iately.  No one had enough money for a church wedding, anyhow.  However
2 CONC , they were married by a minister in a church.  Adair was working in Ar
2 CONC buckle for the Union Oil Co., so that is where he took his bride….
