1 NOTE According to information from Katherine Bushman that James Hotchkiss pa
2 CONC ssed on to me, Samuel sold 461 acres of land to his brother Robert in J
2 CONC un 1755.  Samuel had bought it from George and Robert Breckenridge in F
2 CONC eb 1745.  The land was in the so-called Beverley Patent, which was part o
2 CONC f Augusta Co., VA, after it was split off from Orange County.
2 CONT
2 CONT I'm not sure how certain it is that Samuel and Robert were brothers.  T
2 CONC his identification apparently comes from a March 1760 will of Samuel Wi
2 CONC lson (Augusta County Will Book 2, pp. 355-356), in which he names his b
2 CONC rother Robert his executor.  However, how sure are we that this is the r
2 CONC ight Robert?  This Samuel bought 400 acres of land in the Borden Grant i
2 CONC n 1751 (Augusta County Deed Book 4, p. 190), which were then left to "c
2 CONC uzens" Thomas and Samuel Wilson in the 1760 will.  (The Borden Grant wa
2 CONC s south of the Beverley Patent; Samuel's land was in what is now southe
2 CONC rn Rockbridge County.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's death occurred a few months after the Kerr's Creek Massacre of O
2 CONC ctober 1759, which took place in his area [http://www.werelate.org/wiki
2 CONC /The_Kerr%27s_Creek_Massacres_(1759-1763)].  It has been suggested that h
2 CONC e might have died of wounds suffered in that incident.  The website jus
2 CONC t cited lists a Samuel Wilson as killed in the raid of 10 Oct 1759; it s
2 CONC eems unlikely that one Samuel Wilson died in October and then another o
2 CONC ne died the next March.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is a portion of the deed from George and Robert Breckenridge to Sa
2 CONC muel Wilson dated 8 Feb 1745 (Augusta County Deed Book 1, pp. 12-14):
2 CONT
2 CONT This Indenture made the Eight day of February in the Year of our Lord o
2 CONC ne thousand Seven hundred and forty five Between George & Robert Bracke
2 CONC nridge of the County of Augusta of the One part & Samuel Willson of the s
2 CONC ame County and Colony of Virginia of the Other part.  Witnesseth that f
2 CONC or and in Consideration of the sum of fifty pounds Current Money of Vir
2 CONC ginia to the said George & Robert Brackenridge … paid Samuel Willson at o
2 CONC r before the sealing and Delivery of these forever the Receipt whereof h
2 CONC e doth hereby Acknowledge and thereof [?] doth release Acquit and disch
2 CONC arge the said Samuel Willson his Executors and Administrators by these p
2 CONC resents, he the said George & Robert Brackenridge Have [?] granted barg
2 CONC ain’d sold … Released and Conferred and by these presents Doth grant ba
2 CONC rgain sell … Release and Confirm unto the said Samuel Willson (in his A
2 CONC ctual possession now being by virtue of a bargain and sale to him there
2 CONC of made by the said Robert Brackenridge for one whole year, by Indentur
2 CONC e bearing date the day next before the day of the date [?] of these pre
2 CONC sents and by force of the Statute for Transferring … and his heirs four h
2 CONC undred and Sixty One Acres of Land on Augusta [?] and Mannor [?] of Bev
2 CONC erley part of a tract of Land containing seven hundred and Sixty One Ac
2 CONC res granted by Deed of Lease and … to George and Robert Brackenridge by W
2 CONC illiam Beverley Esq. of Essex County bearing date the Twenty fifth and T
2 CONC wenty sixth days of November in the year of our Lord one thousand seven h
2 CONC undred and forty two Declaring that the said George should have three h
2 CONC undred Acres of the north west end of the tract and the remainder there
2 CONC of to Robert Brackenridge … by the said Deeds remaining of Record in Or
2 CONC ange, which remainder [?] … of [four] hundred and Sixty One Acres as bo
2 CONC unded as followeth … beginning at a red Oak [?] Corner to said land the
2 CONC nce North Sixty five Deg’rs West three hundred and twenty Nine poles to a w
2 CONC hite Oak & two hickorys [?] Corner to the afores’d George’s land and wi
2 CONC th his line South twenty Five [?] degrees east two hundred and Sixteen [
2 CONC ?] poles to a Locust [?]  in a line of said tract thence with the lines o
2 CONC f the same South Sixty five degrees East three hundred & thirty poles t
2 CONC o two small Chestnuts [?] and a hickory North forty three degrees East t
2 CONC wo hundred and Eight poles to … White Oaks by the South River North Six
2 CONC ty One degrees West seventy [?] poles to [?] the beginning and all hous
2 CONC es Buildings, orchards, Ways Waters, Watercourses [?], profits commodit
2 CONC ies, hereditaments [?] and appurtenances Whatsoever to the said premise
2 CONC s hereby granted, or any part thereof, belonging or in any wise apperta
2 CONC ining [?] and the Revertion and Revertions [?] remainder and remainders [
2 CONC ?] … Issues and profits thereof and also, all the Estate, Right, Title … u
2 CONC se, trust, property, claim, and Demand, whatsoever of … George & Robert B
2 CONC rackenridge of in and to the s’d premises and all Deed Evidences and Wr
2 CONC itings touching [?] or in any wise concerning the … To have and to hold t
2 CONC he said tract of land and all and … Other the premises hereby granted a
2 CONC nd Released, and every part & parcel thereof, with their and every of t
2 CONC heir appurtenances … said Samuel Willson his heirs and assignes forever t
2 CONC o the only … use and behoof of him the said Samuel Willson and of his h
2 CONC eirs and assigns forever and the said George and Robert Brackenridge fo
2 CONC r himself his heirs Executors, and administrators ...
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT As with the 200-acre tract described in the notes for William Wilson, t
2 CONC he angles in the description above do not make sense to me.  It seems l
2 CONC ikely that this is a not-quite-rectangular tract tilted at an angle of 2
2 CONC 5 degrees toward the east.  I think the 200-acre tract is probably a re
2 CONC ctangle at the western end of this tract, and since the surveying was p
2 CONC robably more accurate in 1817 than in 1745, the tilt angle is probably m
2 CONC ore like 21 1/3 degrees rather than 25 degrees.  There is a figure and a
2 CONC dditional discussion in the WilsonNotes document.
