1 NOTE "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, p. 609:
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. ROBERT H. WILSON, P. O., Cambridge.  Was born on Timber Ridge, Rock
2 CONC bridge county, Virginia, March 4, 1829, in the same house in which Gen. S
2 CONC am Houston was born, and on the same day on which Gen. Jackson was inau
2 CONC gurated president the second time.  He was the second son of David S. W
2 CONC ilson, of Augusta county, Virginia, who was raised to manhood, and was m
2 CONC arried to Miss Margaret Skinner, in Rockbridge county, and moved to thi
2 CONC s county when the subject of this sketch was but fifteen months old.  D
2 CONC r. Wilson has spent the principal part of his life in Saline county, ex
2 CONC cept six years, from 1849 to 1857, which he spent in California.  Dr. W
2 CONC ilson attended the National Medical College of Washington City, and gra
2 CONC duated there in 1861.  He is of Scotch-Irish descent.  His grandfather, W
2 CONC illiam Wilson, had four sons who came to the west, viz.: Gen. John Wils
2 CONC on, now of San Francisco, California; Robert Wilson, formerly of St. Jo
2 CONC seph, Missouri; Col. William A. Wilson, deceased, of this county, and D
2 CONC avid S. Wilson, the doctor's father, who, with Gen. John Wilson, were s
2 CONC oldiers of the war of 1812, and were known in the old times as old Virg
2 CONC inia gentlemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Portrait and Biographical Record of Lafayette and Saline Counties, Mis
2 CONC souri", Chapman Bros., Chicago, 1893, pp. 326-327 (obtained from Google B
2 CONC ooks):
2 CONT
2 CONT [First part skipped]  Our subject was one of a family of eight children
2 CONC , five sisters and three brothers.  A. G. Wilson was born in Saline Cou
2 CONC nty in 1837, and continues his residence here.  Julia, born in Saline C
2 CONC ounty in 1842, married G. A. Cannon, and with her husband resides in he
2 CONC r native locality.  One of our subject's brothers served in the Confede
2 CONC rate army for three years under Gov. Shelby.  Dr. Wilson attended the c
2 CONC ommon schools of Missouri, and at ten years of age began farming upon h
2 CONC is father's land, and until nineteen years old gave his time industriou
2 CONC sly and successfully to the duties of agriculture.  His first departure f
2 CONC rom home was made to the distant State of California, where he remained s
2 CONC ix years, when on account of failing health he returned to the farm in M
2 CONC issouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a time our subject began the study of medicine under Dr. Alexande
2 CONC r, and afterward completed a course of study in the Nathan Medical Coll
2 CONC ege at Washington, where he remained two years, graduating with the deg
2 CONC ree of Doctor of Medicine in 1861.  Immediately after receiving his dip
2 CONC loma he began the duties of his profession, and soon enjoyed an excelle
2 CONC nt practice in Cambridge, Mo., where he remained for a full score of ye
2 CONC ars.  In 1880 he came to his present home in Gilliam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Attention to his medical duties and other business of his active life h
2 CONC ave absorbed the time of Dr. Wilson, but he is nevertheless willing to a
2 CONC id all public measures of known value.  He is a valued member of the In
2 CONC dependent Order of Odd Fellows, and is ever ready to assist in social, b
2 CONC enevolent or religious enterprises of his neighborhood or vicinity.  Dr
2 CONC . Wilson is an ardent advocate of educational progress and reform.  He h
2 CONC as never aspired to official positions, nor been a politician in the wi
2 CONC der significance of the term, but is a sturdy Democrat, his father havi
2 CONC ng been a Whig.  He takes a lively interest in the conduct of local and n
2 CONC ational affairs.  An excellent and skillful physician, ministering to t
2 CONC he wants of suffering humanity, he has long since won and worthily reta
2 CONC ins the confidence of the general public.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the above biographies, it doesn't sound like he ever married.  He w
2 CONC as living with his mother and other family members in the 1850 and 1860 c
2 CONC ensuses.  It appears that he was living as a boarder with a Grove famil
2 CONC y in the 1870 census and living by himself in the 1900 census.
