1 NOTE He is listed as Andrew G. Wilson in the 1850 and 1900 censuses, Givins i
2 CONC n the 1860 and 1870 censuses, and Andrew in the 1880 census.  His middl
2 CONC e name is listed as Givens in the Foster/Reid Family Tree.  His mother (
2 CONC Margaret Kinnear Wilson) had brothers named Andrew and Givens; it seems l
2 CONC ikely that he was named for them.  The name Givens could be an indicati
2 CONC on that they were close to the Givens family, which also had marriage c
2 CONC onnections with the Donley and Reeves families.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the biography of Robert H. Wilson in "Portrait and Biographical Reco
2 CONC rd of Lafayette and Saline Counties, Missouri", it says that one of his b
2 CONC rothers "served in the Confederate army for three years under Gov. Shel
2 CONC by."  "History of Saline County, Missouri" has lists of Confederate and U
2 CONC nion soldiers in the Civil War, but there is no Wilson listed under Col
2 CONC . Joseph Shelby.  There is, however, an A. G. Wilson listed in the Sali
2 CONC ne Jackson Guards of the Missouri State Guards (Confederate), which it s
2 CONC ays later became part of the 1st Cavalry of the Missouri State Guards.  A
2 CONC dditional support that this was Andrew Givens Wilson is from a passage i
2 CONC n a letter from James W. Reid to his son, David W. Reid, dated 21 Nov 1
2 CONC 864 (obtained from the Foster/Reid Family Tree web site), which says "I r
2 CONC eceived a letter from your Aunt Pine [Paulina] this morning stating tha
2 CONC t her and Givens would start on the first Boat from Glasgow for St Loui
2 CONC s, and wished me to remain here untill she came down.  I suppose I will
2 CONC . ... I cant tell you whether Givens will be released or not when he ge
2 CONC ts to Saint Louis.  Ed is here a prisoner tho' I have not seen him yet, h
2 CONC e & Bob Harvey.  Mr. Wm H Porter is here to see what he can do for them
2 CONC .  I suppose they will all be Exchanged."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It doesn't look like Andrew ever married (or if he did, it wasn't for v
2 CONC ery long).  He was living on the farm with his mother and other family m
2 CONC embers in the 1850, 1860, and 1870 censuses.  In the 1880 and 1900 cens
2 CONC uses, he was living with his sister Juliette's family.
