2 DATE 16 FEB 2017
1 NOTE The 1900 census said he was born in Apr 1832, but the biography below s
2 CONC ays that he was born in 1831.  The web site for Mount Mora Cemetery als
2 CONC o says 1831, including a picture of his gravestone, which says 1831 - 1
2 CONC 906, so I will go with that.  The Mount Mora web site also gives his ag
2 CONC e at death as 74, which might mean that he was born after June 7 in 183
2 CONC 1.  In addition, it gives his cause of death as cancer of face.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of Buchanan County and the City of St Joseph and Representativ
2 CONC e Citizens", downloaded from http://members.aol.com/buchanancomo/wilson
2 CONC ja.html, 28 Jul 2004:
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES MADISON WILSON, who is practically retired from business life, is a d
2 CONC irector of the Merchants' Bank of St. Joseph, and has been engaged in t
2 CONC he banking business here almost continuously since 1853.  Of the banker
2 CONC s now in business in this city, he was the first to engage in the banki
2 CONC ng business here.  A man of superior ability and attainments, he ranks a
2 CONC mong the foremost business men.
2 CONT
2 CONT James M. Wilson was born in Howard County, Missouri, in 1831, and is a s
2 CONC on of Robert Wilson, who was a distinguished member of the Missouri bar i
2 CONC n the pioneer days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Wilson removed to Howard County, Missouri, about 1820, and later l
2 CONC ocated in Randolph County, the lines of which he laid off.  He was a me
2 CONC mber of the Union Convention of 1860, was appointed United States Senat
2 CONC or during the War by Governor Gamble, and served one term as a member o
2 CONC f that honorable body.  He came to St. Joseph and lived in retirement u
2 CONC ntil his death in 1870.  He was born in Abingdon, Virginia, and was a b
2 CONC oy when he came to this State, where he was reared and educated for his p
2 CONC rofession.  He married a daughter of Col. John W. Snoddy, who was a pro
2 CONC minent citizen and extensive farmer of Howard County, Missouri, where h
2 CONC e died.  She was born in Madison County, Kentucky, and was young in yea
2 CONC rs when she removed with her parents to Howard County, in 1819.  This u
2 CONC nion resulted in the birth of five children, three sons and two daughte
2 CONC rs, of which number our subject and one sister, Mrs. R. L. McDonald, ar
2 CONC e now living.  The eldest son, John Wilson, was a lawyer and was associ
2 CONC ated until his death, in 1857, with J. M. Bassett, of St. Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT James M. Wilson was but a boy when he came to St. Joseph in 1852 and en
2 CONC gaged as a clerk in the general store of Donald, Saxton & McDonald.  In 1
2 CONC 853 Armstrong Beattie came to St. Joseph and started a private bank, th
2 CONC e first bank in the city, and employed our subject to keep his books.  T
2 CONC he latter continued in this position until the outbreak of the Civil Wa
2 CONC r in 1861, then went West to Montana where he remained until the close o
2 CONC f the war.  He then returned and has since made his home in St. Joseph, h
2 CONC aving been identified with many banks as stockholder and director and h
2 CONC aving engaged in the loan business.  It devolved upon him to close up M
2 CONC r. Beattie's estate in the early "seventies", but 20 years past he has b
2 CONC een treasurer of Mount Mora Cemetery, probably the most beautiful cemet
2 CONC ery in the State.  Although it is in the nature of a charity, as it dep
2 CONC ends largely on gifts, it has been self-sustaining for a number of year
2 CONC s, all money received being expended toward beautifying the grounds and m
2 CONC aking substantial improvements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilson is independent in politics, and although frequently importun
2 CONC ed has steadfastly refused to accept of city or State offices.  He is a m
2 CONC an without family, never having married, and belongs to no secret order
2 CONC s.  He is liberal in religious views, although greatly interested in ch
2 CONC urch work.
