1 NAME John Irving /McDonald/
2 GIVN John Irving
2 SURN McDonald
2 NSFX Jr.
1 SEX M
1 _UID DD97E61A01224C9BAB905D9B5D708F6D7A48
1 CHAN
2 DATE 24 JUL 2017
1 NOTE Full name is from http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:14
2 CONC 49016&id=I79509368.  There is a cemetery record for Irving M. McDonald a
2 CONC t http://www.mountmora.com/findagrave/main.asp?page=search_details&id=9
2 CONC 281, which might be him (in which case the "Jr." is not correct).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of the Class of 1903: Yale College", edited by Ziegler Sargent
2 CONC , Yale University, 1906, pp. 184-185 (obtained from Google Books):
2 CONT
2 CONT *Irving McDonald (B.A. 1903) died November 15, 1903. It is a duty assoc
2 CONC iated with much grief to write of the death of a classmate, but when th
2 CONC at classmate has been a lifelong friend and companion the duty becomes a s
2 CONC orrowful one indeed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irving McDonald was born in St. Joseph, Mo., November 5, 1881, prepared a
2 CONC t the St. Joseph High School and at the Taft School, Watertown, Conn., a
2 CONC nd entered college with the Class.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, John Irving McDonald (Yale 1878), of the R. L. McDonald & C
2 CONC ompany, wholesale dry goods and manufacturers of men's furnishings of S
2 CONC t. Joseph, was born in St. Joseph June 11, 1858, the son of Rufus Lee M
2 CONC cDonald and Mary Ann (Wilson) McDonald. Mrs. R. L. McDonald was the dau
2 CONC ghter of General Robert Wilson, United States Senator from Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother, whose maiden name was Frank Haskell Fanning, was the daught
2 CONC er of Frank Fanning and Sarah (Scott) Fanning.
2 CONT
2 CONT A great uncle, John Wilson, graduated from Yale in 1847.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irving McDonald's life in college is more or less familiar to us all. W
2 CONC hile not a student in the strict sense of the word, he always maintaine
2 CONC d a high standing in his class and did so with the ease and apparent ab
2 CONC sence of effort which marks the quick, ready and absorbing mind. When h
2 CONC is interest was aroused he showed a wonderful capacity for work and ori
2 CONC ginality of thought. These mental qualities had already begun to assert t
2 CONC hemselves in his short business career after college and a life full of e
2 CONC very promise of success was ended by his sudden death five months after g
2 CONC raduation. He died the victim of an unfortunate accident, which left so
2 CONC rrowing his family, friends and the entire community in which he lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his home he was the idol of his father and mother and loved and admi
2 CONC red by his brother and sister. To know him was to love him, is the stor
2 CONC y of his social life. There never was a truer friend or a more genial a
2 CONC nd pleasant companion. It has been nearly three years since his death, y
2 CONC et among his intimate circle of friends his name is still fresh and his a
2 CONC bsence a lasting regret. His companionship was always sought, for he wa
2 CONC s truly a radiating focus of life, good will and happiness. He possesse
2 CONC d a kind, generous and singularly unselfish disposition, ever ready to s
2 CONC acrifice his own pleasure for the enjoyment of those he loved. He was b
2 CONC rave and honorable in every act; courteous and gentlemanly in all his a
2 CONC ssociations with men. These noble traits of character endeared him to a
2 CONC ll who knew him and the Class of 1903 together with his many other frie
2 CONC nds will ever honor and cherish the memory of Irving McDonald.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Barrow Motter.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Triennial Meeting of the Class, June 26, 1906, the following res
2 CONC olutions were adopted:
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereas, It has pleased God in His infinite wisdom to remove from among u
2 CONC s our beloved friend and classmate, Irving McDonald, be it
2 CONT
2 CONT <i>Resolved</i>, That his untimely death came as a shock and surpassing g
2 CONC rief to his friends, and that in behalf of the Class we hereby desire t
2 CONC o convey to his family our keen sorrow and great sense of loss; and be i
2 CONC t further
2 CONT
2 CONT <i>Resolved</i>, That a copy of these resolutions be sent to his family a
2 CONC nd entered in the records of the Class.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfred H. Bill,
2 CONT Willard A. Lyon,
2 CONT J. R. Wait,
2 CONT for the Class of 1903.
