1 NOTE The National Register of Historic Places Registration Form for Walnut P
2 CONC ark Farm Historic District (1999; obtained July 2017 from Missouri Depa
2 CONC rtment of Natural Resources web site, http://dnr.mo.gov/shpo/nps-nr/990
2 CONC 01597.pdf) has some information about him.  Walnut Park Farm belonged t
2 CONC o Rufus Lee McDonald.  It was a combination of land he bought and land t
2 CONC hat his wife Mary Ann inherited from her father, Robert Wilson.  Appare
2 CONC ntly the main business of the farm was raising race horses, and Marvin w
2 CONC as the farm manager.  Marvin and Annette moved into the farm house afte
2 CONC r they were married in 1896.  They moved to St. Joseph a few years late
2 CONC r, but returned to the farm periodically.  The write-up notes that daug
2 CONC hter Annette Davis Dabney was born on the farm.  Also, it says that Mar
2 CONC vin "became ill on a business trip in Kansas and died shortly afterward
2 CONC ."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dearborn Democrat, 5 Jul 1907 (from Find A Grave, listing for Marvin M D
2 CONC avis):
2 CONT
2 CONT M. M. Davis Dies After Operation For Appendicitis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marvin Middleton Davis, 36 years old, a prominent St. Joseph real estat
2 CONC e dealer, died at 8 o'clock yesterday morning at St. Joseph's hospital.  D
2 CONC eath followed an operation for appendicitis, performed the night before
2 CONC .  It is not thought to have been the real cause of death, however.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in New Market, Platte county, March 20, 1870, Mr. Davis while stil
2 CONC l an infant, was brought to St. Joseph by his parents, Rev. and Mrs. Jo
2 CONC hn C. C. Davis.  He resided in St. Joseph continuously until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Davis attended the public schools of St. Joseph until he was about 1
2 CONC 5 years old, then entered the employ of the Chicago, Burlington & Quinc
2 CONC y railroad.  He was later, for a number of years, connected with the Tu
2 CONC rner-Frazer Grocer company.  In 1891, with his brothers, F. C. Davis an
2 CONC d R. L. Davis, he established the real estate firm of Davis Brothers, w
2 CONC ith which he was actively interested at the time of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May, 1896, Mr. Davis married Miss Annie U. [sic] McDonald, daughter o
2 CONC f R. L. McDonald.  His widow, three children, Marvin M. Davis Jr., Anne
2 CONC tte and Dorothy Davis, his mother, Mrs. Mary C. Davis, a sister, Mrs. W
2 CONC . F. McMurray, of Louisville, Ky., and three brothers, Dr. W. B. Davis, F
2 CONC . C. Davis and R. L. Davis, survive him.  He was a nephew of W. F. Davi
2 CONC s, of St. Joseph, and the late R. T. Davis.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT His death date is somewhat uncertain.  The Mount Mora Cemetery web site s
2 CONC hows that he died on 28 Jun 1907.  But Find A Grave (listing for Marvin M D
2 CONC avis) shows 30 Jun 1907, and there is a photo of his gravestone with th
2 CONC at date on it.
