1 NOTE She seems to be listed as Hellen in the 1900 census and Annette in the 1
2 CONC 910 census.  Perhaps Hellen (or Helen) is her middle name?
2 CONT
2 CONT The Boyle Genealogy Addendum mentions a source, "McDonald Genealogy: Na
2 CONC mes of Rufus Lee McDonald Family to February 1974", by Annette Davis Da
2 CONC bney.  (I would like to find a copy of that.)  The National Register of H
2 CONC istoric Places Registration Form for Walnut Park Farm Historic District (
2 CONC 1999; obtained July 2017 from Missouri Department of Natural Resources w
2 CONC eb site, http://dnr.mo.gov/shpo/nps-nr/99001597.pdf) mentions a 1974 ma
2 CONC nuscript by Annette Davis Dabney called "The Life of Rufus Lee McDonald
2 CONC " (her grandfather).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1940 census, she was still single.  An entry at Ancestry.com in "
2 CONC U.S. Social Security Applications and Claims Index 1936-2007" for Annet
2 CONC te Davis Dabney says that she was listed as Annette D Dabney in 1965, s
2 CONC o she married sometime between 1940 and 1965.  Marrying late in life li
2 CONC ke that, she probably did not have children.  In both 1930 and 1940 cen
2 CONC suses, she was listed as a secretary for the YWCA, first in St. Joseph, M
2 CONC O, and then in Richmond, VA.  The 1940 census says she was still living i
2 CONC n Missouri in 1935, so she moved to Virginia sometime between 1935 and 1
2 CONC 940.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Katherine Gentry Bushman Papers, 1961-1997 (Accession 35743, Person
2 CONC al Papers Collection, The Library of Virginia, Richmond, VA) contain a l
2 CONC etter from Annette Davis Dabney to Katherine Bushman, a small portion o
2 CONC f which is quoted in the Hotchkiss manuscript.  The letter follows, wit
2 CONC h some adjustments to punctuation (also note that a few statements in t
2 CONC he letter are questionable, such as that all of William Wilson's sons s
2 CONC tudied law and several became judges):
2 CONT
2 CONT January 21, 1979
2 CONT
2 CONT My dear Mrs Bushman:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Rudolph Shaver of the Circuit Court of Augusta County, Virginia has k
2 CONC indly suggested that I write you concerning an ancestor of mine.  The p
2 CONC roblem is that I do not know the maiden name of my great-great grandmot
2 CONC her.  My great great-grandfather was <u>William Wilson</u>, a farmer wh
2 CONC o lived near Staunton in Augusta County.  According to one report he ma
2 CONC rried into the <u>Yancey family</u> of Virginia.  I do not know how man
2 CONC y children William Wilson and his wife had but I do know that they had f
2 CONC our fine, ambitious sons who came to Missouri around 1820.  Their names w
2 CONC ere Robert, John, William and David Wilson.
2 CONT
2 CONT These young men first settled in a place named Old Franklin Missouri th
2 CONC en moved nearby to Fayette, Missouri in Howard County.  All studied law a
2 CONC fter coming to Missouri and several became judges.  William moved to Ma
2 CONC rshall Mo.  John later moved to California.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was my great grandfather  He was born in Augusta County near Sta
2 CONC unton in 1800.  He was buried in St Joseph Missouri in 1870.  He became o
2 CONC ne of the most important men in Missouri.  He served eight years in the M
2 CONC o. state legislature and became United States Senator from Missouri in 1
2 CONC 862.
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of my family inherited the portrait of Senator Robert Wilson w
2 CONC hich was painted by the famous George C Bingham.  Not long ago I found a
2 CONC n excellent book on Bingham, and I was so interested to know that he ca
2 CONC me to Missouri from Virginia when he was a young boy (with his parents)
2 CONC .  The Binghams came approximately the same time the Wilson young men c
2 CONC ame.  All went to Old Franklin and then to Fayette, Missouri where the f
2 CONC amous Bingham’s father opened a lodge and died soon.  In Virginia the B
2 CONC inghams lived near Staunton in Augusta Co near Weyers Cave.  The Bingha
2 CONC ms and the William Wilsons <u>must</u> have known each other <u>in Virg
2 CONC inia</u>.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bingham paintings are becoming more valuable every day.  I suppose you r
2 CONC ead where one of his paintings, “The Jolly Boatman” (I believe it was) w
2 CONC as sold at a Park Bernet Auction for nine hundred thousand dollars last y
2 CONC ear.  For a painter who had never had a lesson this is amazing indeed.  I h
2 CONC ave always enjoyed his painting at the Metropolitan Museum in New York o
2 CONC f the fur trader coming down the Missouri River.  Enough about Bingham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now back to the Wilson brothers.  David was the last to come to Missour
2 CONC i (about 1830).  David married Miss Margaret Skinner [sic] in Rockbridg
2 CONC e County, VA.  The other brothers who came to Missouri married Missouri g
2 CONC irls.  David eventually lived in Saline County Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT My great grand-father Robert Wilson moved to Andrew Co. very near St Jo
2 CONC seph, Missouri as his daughter Mary Ann Wilson had married R. L. McDona
2 CONC ld of St Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was born and raised in St Joseph, Missouri.  All of my married life I l
2 CONC ived in Richmond, Virginia but have returned to St Joseph as my husband d
2 CONC ied in 1975.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do not know whether the William Wilsons came to Missouri with their s
2 CONC ons.  Perhaps they remained in Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT I would be so appreciative if you could find any information about this W
2 CONC ilson Family and particularly about Mrs William Wilson’s maiden name wh
2 CONC ich is probably Yancey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Shaver wrote that you charge six dollars for the first two hours of r
2 CONC esearch.  I am enclosing a check for this amount.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely,
2 CONT Annette D. Dabney
2 CONT (Mrs James Kennon Dabney)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Century Apartments
2 CONT Apt 307
2 CONT 627 North 25th St.
2 CONT St. Joseph, Missouri
2 CONT (64506)
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 AUG 1899
2 PLAC Andrew Co., MO
