1 _UID 2B50B4F9F5CF4F2BB34B87617207AB2898E6
1 CHAN
2 DATE 3 JUL 2018
1 NOTE James Hotchkiss did not have this child listed.
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter from John Wilson to Abiel Leonard, dated 11 Apr 1852, quoted i
2 CONC n "'General' John Wilson, Signer of the Deseret Petition: Including Let
2 CONC ters from the Leonard Collection" by Frederic A. Culmer (California His
2 CONC torical Society Quarterly, Vol. 26, No. 4, pp. 321-348, December 1947), i
2 CONC ncludes the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT I lost my son Wm. Henry last fall with Disentary, caught in the mines w
2 CONC here the necessary mode of living leads to the encouragement of that di
2 CONC sease.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT End note 172 of “Bear Flag Lieutenant: The Life Story of Henry L. Ford (
2 CONC 1822–1860), with Some Related and Contemporary Art of Alexander Edouart (
2 CONC Continued)” by Fred B. Rogers says that William attended Princeton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The John Wilson Papers (BANC MSS C-B 420, The Bancroft Library, Univers
2 CONC ity of California, Berkeley, CA) include a letter from W. H. Wilson to h
2 CONC is father that looks like it is dated 5 May 1857.  Based on the above, t
2 CONC his can't be right.  My thought is that the 7 must really be a 1, so th
2 CONC at the date is 5 May 1851.  At the time of the letter, William had give
2 CONC n up on gold mining and was trying to set up a salmon-fishing business.  I g
2 CONC uess that must have fallen through.  Here is my attempted transcription o
2 CONC f the letter (punctuation added; the original has almost no punctuation
2 CONC ):
2 CONT
2 CONT Klamathport  May 5th 1851
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Father
2 CONT
2 CONT Some few days ago Mr Thompsons men all went down and took possession of t
2 CONC here [sic] old Claim at the Bluff and for this Reason that the company h
2 CONC ad not settled with them according to contract.  now our hope is blaste
2 CONC d again by paying some mean [?] men to manage the Business of the compa
2 CONC ny.  if it had been carried on by good honest men [several words uncert
2 CONC ain] out of it but as it has been managed so badly we are again Blown s
2 CONC ky high and as this has happened I have concluded to have nothing more t
2 CONC o do with it.  I have taken up a very valuable Claim near the mouth of t
2 CONC he River for fishing which I think will pay some 30 or 40 thousand doll
2 CONC ars yearly if propperly [sic] managed by cachching [?] Salmon and salti
2 CONC ng them up in Barrels and sending them to San Francisco.  I wish you to a
2 CONC id me in this by enquiring what they will be worth by the Barrel in you
2 CONC r city.  all that we will want will be a scin [? skein?] about 400 or 5
2 CONC 00 hundred [?] yards long and a few hundred Barrels and salt.  it is an I
2 CONC sland within 1/4 mile of the mouth.  Salmon any quantity may be caught i
2 CONC n a days work and of the finest stock.  any Class of vessels can come i
2 CONC n at this time.  This is a good Business here and I hope you will give m
2 CONC e all of the assistance you can.  Business is very dull here as there i
2 CONC s nothing to sell.  applications made every day for goods.  write me by t
2 CONC he first opportunity.  tell Mr [?name?] to send the [?word?] that I hav
2 CONC e written for you.  make the contract with him for me what you think is [
2 CONC ?word?] and I will be satisfied.  [?I could?] have sold a great many go
2 CONC ods If I had of had them.  do all you can for me in this fishing busine
2 CONC ss as it will pay a large fortune for all of us and [?word?] what you c
2 CONC an do.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am well.  I am doing a littl [sic] in my office.  your Son
2 CONT
2 CONT W. H. Wilson
2 CONT
2 CONT P.S. I will Refer you to Capt [?name?] of the Schooner Fayanary [?] and h
2 CONC e will explain to you the chance I have and he will have an interest in i
2 CONC t.  write me as he owns two Schooners and will Run them and as all of o
2 CONC ur shipping.  yours
2 CONT
2 CONT W H Wilson
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1851
2 PLAC CA
