4 FIELD
5 NAME Page
1 SEX M
1 _UID 241EA4187C344AA088430F0911FD8D546A07
1 CHAN
2 DATE 19 JUL 2017
1 NOTE D.W. and Molly Reid were boarding with the Vance family in the 1880 cen
2 CONC sus in Marshall, MO (Annette Wilson Vance was David's first cousin, onc
2 CONC e removed).  See the notes for Mollie Turner Reid, which have more abou
2 CONC t this.
2 CONT
2 CONT His name is given as David Wilson Reid in the Foster/Reid Family Tree, b
2 CONC ut David William Reid in the Reid manuscript.  Since Mollie Turner Reid w
2 CONC as his wife, she should know his middle name!  This is confirmed in an e
2 CONC ntry in "1845-1890. Catalogue of Phi Alpha Society ... Illinois College
2 CONC ", Jacksonville, IL, 1890, p. 85 (available on Google Books), which say
2 CONC s:
2 CONT
2 CONT 361. David William Reid, b. 13 April, 1845, near Cambridge, Mo.  He ent
2 CONC ered the freshman class of Illinois College in 1863 remaining one year.  H
2 CONC e attended Michigan University one year, and then studied medicine at t
2 CONC he St. Louis medical college where he was graduated in 1868.  In 1868-7
2 CONC 0 he practiced at Oregon, Mo., in 1870-80 at Cambridge, and since then a
2 CONC t Slater, Mo., where he now lives with his wife and two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Reid manuscript, written by Mollie Turner Reid in 1931 (obtain
2 CONC ed from the Foster/Reid Family Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. David W. Reid
2 CONT
2 CONT Was born near Cambridge, Mo. April 13, 1845, the son of Dr. Jas. and El
2 CONC iza Reid.  His mother died when David, or "Bub" as he was generally cal
2 CONC led, was 2 1/2 years old, leaving a daughter, Ada, only a week or two o
2 CONC ld.  (Some other troubles, perhaps flux, developed while she was still i
2 CONC n bed, I think.)  This baby only lived to be 4 months old and the only r
2 CONC ecollection Dr. Reid had of his mother was when he climbed over her to "
2 CONC see the baby", hurting her probably, & she gave him a little spank to d
2 CONC rive him back, & he crawled back, howling, to be petted by his aunt.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his mother's death, his grandmother & aunts seem to have had char
2 CONC ge of him.  Whether his father stayed in their home or in his own farm h
2 CONC ome I do not know, but I know that at some time he was in his own home a
2 CONC nd Polly Patrick, a colored servant, kept house for him -- & it was a c
2 CONC ustom on Sunday morning before his starting to church, for a plate of c
2 CONC ookies & a pitcher of butter milk to be placed on the table for his dis
2 CONC posal.  The fondness for these lasted to his old age when he lived with u
2 CONC s (until death 1883) & his son, D.W. seemed to have inherited the same.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do not think that Dr. Reid had a happy childhood.  His father was dev
2 CONC oted & kind, doing everything a man could do for him, studying his inte
2 CONC rest always, but he was with old people altogether, & he said Polly was m
2 CONC ore like a mother than anyone else.
2 CONT
2 CONT At an early age he was boarded at Judge Gilliam's (a widower whose grow
2 CONC n daughters kept house for him) & he roomed with several men.  This was i
2 CONC n order to go to school I presume.  Later, he boarded at the teacher's, M
2 CONC r. Price, grandfather of "Bob" -- who guarded him conscienciously [sic] o
2 CONC n the way to school &c &c.  I don't suppose anyone was ever intentional
2 CONC ly unkind to him in his life, but circumstances gave him no youth.  In t
2 CONC own, he & his father lived in their own home & ate at an aunt's "Aunt M
2 CONC ary Grove's" & she & "Aunt Sarah" looked after his clothes.  [These wer
2 CONC e sisters of David's father, James W. Reid.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, when the war was on, his father tho't best to send him over into I
2 CONC llinois -- Jacksonville -- to school.  Still later, he went to Ann Arbo
2 CONC r, Michigan, for one year I think -- & here, I think he had some of the h
2 CONC ome boys with him -- Clay Grove, Steve Claycomb, & Taylor Goode -- a co
2 CONC mpany of four.  I know they were together somewhere & I think it was he
2 CONC re -- or Taylor Goode may have been at the medical school in St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT He studied medicine & was graduated from a school in St. Louis, later i
2 CONC ncorporated into Washington University.  He had previously studied with D
2 CONC rs. Wilson (his uncle) & Alexander in Cambridge, & afterwards became th
2 CONC eir partner & practiced there a number of years.  However, he had previ
2 CONC ously moved to Oregon, Holt Co. where he had a drug store & practiced m
2 CONC edicine for about a year or two.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Annie Beeding, daughter of E. L. Beeding, merchant & banker o
2 CONC f Cambridge -- & who was a very dear friend of my sister Julia, & one o
2 CONC f the bridesmaids at her wedding.  I, as a young girl, knew her too, as s
2 CONC he had often visited in our home, & she had once taken me home with her f
2 CONC or a few days -- & I was very fond of her.
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage lasted only about 3 yrs. when she died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a widower about 7 yrs. & lived, whenever in Cam., with Mr. & Mrs
2 CONC . Beeding at their urgent request -- until some time after the death of M
2 CONC r. Beeding -- & when he afterwards married me, Mrs. Beeding seemed plea
2 CONC sed & with her daughter-in-law came to Boonville to the wedding, bringi
2 CONC ng me a present.
2 CONT
2 CONT We were married April 21, 1880, & lived together nearly 49 yrs. -- in M
2 CONC arshall, Slater, Wellsville, Kas, & Wichita Kas, until he died in Wichi
2 CONC ta Jan. 16, 1929.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Reid was a man of rare character I doubt if one of cleaner, purer m
2 CONC ind ever lived.  He had no bad habits, rarely used even a simple by-wor
2 CONC d, and a profane or foul one, never! -- When he was a youth, or young m
2 CONC an, his father said to him, "David, there is a barrel of whiskey in the c
2 CONC ellar & if you ever want it you can go to it, but I'll advise you not t
2 CONC o."  He never touched it!  -- tho' he really liked the taste.  At one t
2 CONC ime he decided he would learn to smoke and took a cigar, when the tho't c
2 CONC ame, "how silly it is to acquire a useless habit" -- he threw the cigar a
2 CONC way & never took another!  He was modest & unostentatious, not parading
2 CONC , but living his virtues -- & I cannot refrain from quoting a few encom
2 CONC iums uttered by those who from close association knew him most intimate
2 CONC ly.  Mr. Oscar Crane, living on his farm for years, said years before h
2 CONC is (Dr. Reid's) death, & repeated after it, "he is the best man I ever k
2 CONC new -- I'll except no one, not even my own father!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Claude Field who once lived in our home, was associated with him in b
2 CONC usiness & knew him well -- when he met us at the hotel as we took him t
2 CONC o Slater for burial, exclaimed, with emotion in his voice, "he was the s
2 CONC traightest man I ever saw!"
2 CONT
2 CONT And the eulogies of Judge Jno. A. Rich & Mr. Com P. Storts (lawyer) at t
2 CONC he funeral testified to his influence, his service as a physician & cit
2 CONC izen, & the high esteem in which he was held in the town.  In the home o
2 CONC f his daughter-in-law where he lived some years & received nothing but t
2 CONC heir love & consideration, there comes this testimony.  Erma, at his be
2 CONC dside as he passed away, exclaimed "he was the best man that ever lived
2 CONC !" and Mildred, in addition to many words of praise for his kindness, a
2 CONC nd consideration for others -- sometimes when we discuss the ways of pe
2 CONC ople & I refer to him -- always says "yes but he was an exception!"  An
2 CONC d even little Jane, then 6 yrs old, said to me one day, "Aren't you sor
2 CONC ry Grandpa died?  he was so good to me, he'd play with me -- & let me c
2 CONC omb his hair -- " -- -- (Jane was a great comfort to him).
2 CONT
2 CONT As a wife, I can add my tribute -- He was a kind, considerate, true hus
2 CONC band, never failing in his loyalty to me -- & when times of weakness ca
2 CONC me to me, he was the sympathizing physician & helper -- the prop upon w
2 CONC hich I leaned & which has been taken away -- to my loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Could his sons, for whom he would have sacrificed anything, ask a bette
2 CONC r legacy from a father than such a character? -- He was 83 yrs, & 9 mon
2 CONC s. old when he died very suddenly, Jany 16, 1929.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Slater we were met by some 25 or 30 people at 10 or 11 o'clock at ni
2 CONC ght with urgent invitations to go to their houses but there being eight o
2 CONC f us, we waited until next morning when the home of Judge Rich (& other
2 CONC s) was opened to us, & where many friends came to see Dr. Reid & to bri
2 CONC ng us their sympathy.  We will never forget their kindness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not by any means do I mean to imply, by quoting the above testimonials, t
2 CONC hat Dr. Reid was literally "the best man that ever lived" for there are m
2 CONC any, many good men, but these were the spontaneous utterances of those w
2 CONC ho knew him intimately & are an index to his true, everyday life.
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 APR 1845
2 PLAC near Cambridge, MO
