1 CHAN
2 DATE 22 FEB 2017
1 NOTE She is the author of the Reid manuscript.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Reid manuscript, written by Mollie Turner Reid in 1931 (obtain
2 CONC ed from the Foster/Reid Family Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mollie Turner Reid,
2 CONT single & married a fuller account.
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of John Van Pelt & Sarah O. Turner, I was born in Keytesvi
2 CONC lle, Mo. April 16, 1855 & was christened Mary, but always called Mollie
2 CONC , & so I have always signed it.  There I lived until about 3 1/2 yrs. o
2 CONC ld when my father moved, with his family, to Glasgow, Mo., there to pra
2 CONC ctice Law as partner of Thos. H. Shackelford until the civil war broke o
2 CONC ut & Mr. S. moved to St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of that first year in Glasgow, when from 3 1/2 to 4 1/2 yrs. old, I hav
2 CONC e a good many memories of the house itself, the children I played with, & g
2 CONC ames -- the big open lot we roamed in, the hole in the fence I crept th
2 CONC ru to go to the neighbor's -- & the day we moved to our new home where a b
2 CONC ig roaring fire in the fireplace awaited us.  I know I remember this be
2 CONC cause we were in the first house only one year -- until I was 4 1/2 yrs
2 CONC . old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tho' rather young, I have some vivid recollections of the civil war.  (
2 CONC War is apt to impress one!)  I recall the many, many soldiers, who, som
2 CONC etimes ragged & torn stopped at our "alley door" to ask, or sometimes t
2 CONC o demand, food; -- of a special one who asked my little sister Annie to g
2 CONC ive him a "bus" saying she reminded him of the little one he left behin
2 CONC d him; -- of another special one half drunken, or pretending to be, who t
2 CONC alked & brandished his pistol rather freely as he ate until my father a
2 CONC sked him to put it up; -- of the day when they were searching for a man w
2 CONC ho they tho't had jumped into our back yard, & then searched our house t
2 CONC o find & when they came to the attic, with a narrow stair, no one was b
2 CONC rave enough to go up & look for him! the search stopped.  The Union sol
2 CONC diers held the town.  The night before the battle of Glasgow, it happen
2 CONC ed that my mother had pulled a small tooth for me, & when next morning w
2 CONC e were awakened by the booming cannon that split a neighbor's bed post a h
2 CONC alf-block away, it seemed to me that somehow there was a rather close c
2 CONC onnection between the two events!
2 CONT
2 CONT As the battle raged, we with some neighbors who had gathered in --(one o
2 CONC f whom bro't a pan of cooked rice & her dress bonnet! --) were in compa
2 CONC rative safety in our basement until the warning came to get out in hast
2 CONC e as the city hall with its great stock of ammunition was on fire.  (th
2 CONC ey found afterwards the ammunition had been moved).  Of course we speed
2 CONC ily vacated & marched thru the streets, the "minnie balls" whizzing ove
2 CONC r our heads -- from the sharp shooters (confederates) across the river -
2 CONC - to a higher & more exposed location, passing on the way, for an encou
2 CONC ragement, a dead man lying in the street.
2 CONT
2 CONT After some hours came surrender & then the holocaust of fire which dest
2 CONC royed fourteen houses I am told, but strange to say, I do not remember t
2 CONC he fire at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT Next day (?) I was permitted to go to a home -- Steinmetz -- & see thre
2 CONC e young men, two of them the sons, the other a boarder, laid out on thr
2 CONC ee cots, dead!
2 CONT
2 CONT So far as I know, these were the only casualties among the townspeople, e
2 CONC xcepting the accidental killing, or mortally wounding, at his own home o
2 CONC f our Methodist minister by a bursting bomb.  The young men were fighti
2 CONC ng in the breastworks that had been thrown up in the town.
2 CONT
2 CONT But war had an end (1865) and the years of my childhood passed smoothly o
2 CONC n.  I was for a short while in Mr. Strother's Seminary, where my sister J
2 CONC ulia graduated but my main education was in Pritchett College, or Pritc
2 CONC hett Institute as it was then called, under the presidency of Rev. Carr W
2 CONC aller Pritchett, & the leadership of such instructors as Prof. Thomson B
2 CONC ond, -- later of Central College, Fayette, Mo., -- S. H. Trowbridge & o
2 CONC thers, and there has ever been in my heart a feeling of deepest gratitu
2 CONC de for their painstaking care & patience.
2 CONT
2 CONT At eighteen yrs. of age -- 1873 -- June 12 -- I was graduated from this i
2 CONC nstitution, in a class of six, -- taking the degree, "Mistress in Arts"
2 CONC , the equivalent of of [sic] "Bachelor in Arts" which the boys rec'd, -
2 CONC - but our Pres. was entirely too "old fogy" to call girls bachelors, so h
2 CONC e gave us the more feminine title, tho we sat in class with the boys.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following winter 1874-5 I taught the four months' term in the Glasg
2 CONC ow Public School being associated with the father of Judge Jno. A. Rich a
2 CONC s Principal, & a classmate, Mary Grove, as the other assistant teacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875, my older sister, Mrs. Julia Dunnica, became very seriously ill
2 CONC , & my father having died the previous year, & there being nothing spec
2 CONC ial to hold my mother in Glasgow, she with my sister Annie & myself mov
2 CONC ed to Boonville & into the Dunnica home to care for her & her two littl
2 CONC e children, Leon, & the baby Julia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three months later, in Jan'y 1876 -- Julia was taken to Philadelphia fo
2 CONC r operation for ovarian tumor, and a few days later died from an emboli
2 CONC sm (blood clot in the heart).  This established my mother in Boonville u
2 CONC ntil my brother-in-law died, July 1880, when she moved to Independence M
2 CONC o. where my brother lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the mean time I had taught in Cooper Institute, Boonville, a private S
2 CONC eminary for Young Ladies owned & operated by Prof. A. Haines, for 3 yea
2 CONC rs -- 76 - 79.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 21st, 1880, I was married to Dr. D. W. Reid, & we located in M
2 CONC arshall Mo. for our first home, boarding with Cousin Annie Vance & Aunt M
2 CONC ary Wilson, who indeed gave me a kindly welcome & rec'd me as real kin.  A
2 CONC bout 6 months with them, and three months of house keeping -- & then we m
2 CONC oved to the new town of Slater, Dr. Reid being drawn, I think, by the m
2 CONC any old associates who had come there from Cambridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Any romances? the girls may ask.  Why certainly, don't most girls have t
2 CONC hem? -- but as "petting parties" were not in vogue then, I have no memo
2 CONC ry to cause a blush now -- but when the "right one came along" we excha
2 CONC nged vows "until death us do part" and they held until death us did par
2 CONC t.
2 CONT
2 CONT And would the girls like to know what "Grandma's wedding dress" was?  '
2 CONC twas quite "chic" I assure you -- a travelling dress, strictly tailored
2 CONC , having a navy blue satin skirt, & a close fitting cloth coat, or redi
2 CONC ngote, trimmed in the blue, the rather long skirt of it opening in fron
2 CONC t to show the skirt.  The coat a light gray with tiny blue & pink speck
2 CONC s woven in it, a blue satin bow with long ends hung from the neck.  The b
2 CONC onnet was something like a "poke" but not so poky, of light straw mixed w
2 CONC ith pink (or red) and blue & trimmed very simply with blue satin ribbon t
2 CONC o match dress.  Gray kid gloves & black shoes (always then) finished th
2 CONC e costume -- & if I didn't look nice it wasn't the fault of the dress, w
2 CONC hich was the "very latest" & made by a St. Louis dress maker.  We were m
2 CONC arried at 8 o'clock in the morning, a few intimate friends being presen
2 CONC t, & my mother gave us a lovely wedding breakfast, after which we start
2 CONC ed on our little journey to St. Louis & Chicago.
2 CONT
2 CONT As these are incidents of my own simple life, of which I naturally know m
2 CONC ore than any other, you may pardon me for recalling some of the things t
2 CONC hat I undertook -- perhaps simple in themselves & unimportant to others
2 CONC , but breaking the monotony of household cares & keeping me from "rusti
2 CONC ng out."  My children were never a burden & loving care for them went a
2 CONC long with other things.
2 CONT
2 CONT As I had liked teaching in school, so I enjoyed teaching in Sunday scho
2 CONC ol & my work with classes of young men, mixed classes of young men & wo
2 CONC men, & some of older women, has seemed to me one of the more worth whil
2 CONC e things that I have attempted & it gives me pleasurable memories.  (Up t
2 CONC o two yrs. ago, 1929, I taught for several years a class of women in We
2 CONC llsville.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Another is my work in the Women's Foreign Mis. Soc. in the home church & a
2 CONC s District Sec'y & Treas. for about 16 yrs., which required a good deal o
2 CONC f time & attendance at two meetings a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT For my own profit & pleasure I undertook & finished the four year Chaut
2 CONC auqua Course, first in company with quite a number of friends, notably R
2 CONC ev. & Mrs. Willingham, Presbyterian minister & wife -- but as the club b
2 CONC roke up, by removal & otherwise, I finished alone.  Upon examination by c
2 CONC orrespondence, which was rather rigid, I was lucky in missing only one q
2 CONC uestion & that was in American history! where I allowed my southern "sp
2 CONC unk" to dictate the answer to "What caused the civil War?"  I knew it w
2 CONC as not according to the book, & was not surprised that it didn't pass w
2 CONC ith that northern bunch!  If I had dreamed that I was to get 100 in eve
2 CONC ry thing else I might have tempered my enthusiasm & wiped out that 99. -
2 CONC - but 'twas funny & I still have my diploma & 5 seals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mind you, I don't claim to have known everything in those 20 or 28 book
2 CONC s -- not by any means! -- We were permitted to consult the books as muc
2 CONC h as we pleased -- the difficulty was to find the correct answers, but i
2 CONC t was evidence that we had studied them & that was what they wanted.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Slater Travel Club, organized 1903 (?), was a source of pleasure & p
2 CONC rofit, and if I could only remember all we went over, I would have a fu
2 CONC nd of information, but alas, alas! -- -- When we left Slater, this went w
2 CONC ith other pleasant associations, but others are still enjoying it, 1931
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Our married life passed smoothly & happily on.  Our children were our f
2 CONC irst interest & we tried to fit them for life as far as we could, -- se
2 CONC nding them, after graduation in High School, to Columbia & Kansas City f
2 CONC or further instruction & for their professions.
2 CONT
2 CONT We were "home bodies" -- not being financially able to take long & very e
2 CONC xpensive tours, we did indulge in some pleasurable ones.  Our boys with u
2 CONC s, we visited both the Chicago & St. Louis Expositions -- We visited my m
2 CONC other in Las Vegas [I think this was Las Vegas, NM, because it is menti
2 CONC oned in another part of the manuscript] one winter & went on to Califor
2 CONC nia for a number of weeks.  Since the boys have grown older & left us, w
2 CONC e have visited Colorado several times, and Okoboji Iowa, a resort & Was
2 CONC hington, D.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT This past summer, -- Dr. Reid having passed away, I alone, went with al
2 CONC l my children & grands. to Fair Hills, Michigan. -- Thus, mingled with m
2 CONC y sorrows, -- especially in the loss of family & some dear friends, -- m
2 CONC y life had its pleasures.  I am the only one left of my father's family & s
2 CONC ometimes I feel very much alone, but far, far more so since my companio
2 CONC n of nearly forty nine years passed on two years ago, thus breaking our f
2 CONC amily circle.  But I am thankful that I still have my boys & their fami
2 CONC lies & so am not utterly alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Inmates)
2 CONT Other inmates of our home were Dr. Reid's father who was with us for se
2 CONC veral years & died there.
2 CONT
2 CONT His aunt, Miss Sarah, who was also there for several yrs., but died at "
2 CONC Cousin Pick" Porter's in Warrensburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Beeding spent a great deal of time there & was devoted to the chil
2 CONC dren who called her "Grandma" & knew no difference, -- & to Dr. Reid as t
2 CONC o a son -- & I believe I can claim to have had a place in her affection
2 CONC s too.
2 CONT
2 CONT My mother & Julia were at home there tho frequently away, for a good ma
2 CONC ny years & my mother died there -- Julia staid on, off & on, until her m
2 CONC arriage, & would still consider it home, I think, if I had one.  I can n
2 CONC ever forget Julia's devotion to, & care of my mother, & her considerati
2 CONC on for me.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the past ten years, the doors of our children's homes have been ope
2 CONC n to us & it is a source of great comfort to know that Dr. Reid's last d
2 CONC ays were with his children who were the greatest interest in his life, & b
2 CONC etween whom & himself there was the warmest devotion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our interest in our grand-children has been very deep & sincere -- a de
2 CONC sire for their real & best good being always our thought.  Jane, who ca
2 CONC me 8 yrs after Mary Helen, bro't a good deal of sunshine for us & Dr. R
2 CONC eid found her a comfort in what might have been rather lonely hours aft
2 CONC er he was less active.  May this child develop into a strong & noble wo
2 CONC man! -- & with her sisters be such as "Grandpa", & I, would have them t
2 CONC o be.
2 CONT
2 CONT My sons' wives I regard, not as "in-laws" but as daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that light I regard Julia too for she has been both daughter & siste
2 CONC r to me.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reid Turner was with us two years.
