1 NOTE Notes from Rebecca Betz:
2 CONT
2 CONT John decided at some point he could not take care of Grandpa and Loize a
2 CONC nymore so he sent them to the Wilsons. Grandpa was about 8 or 9.  The W
2 CONC ilsons truly felt Lizzie had married below her station and disliked Joh
2 CONC n greatly.  He was a drifter (according to Uncle DR) and never settled i
2 CONC n one place long.  Additionally, he fought on the Confederate side in t
2 CONC he war and Lizzie's folks were Union supporters.  You have to remember, M
2 CONC issouri was a border state and families were ripped apart because broth
2 CONC ers would go into the war on different sides.  Grandpa lived with the W
2 CONC ilsons and they tried to "clean him up" and send him to school.  Again a
2 CONC ccording to Uncle DR they would have sent him to any school he wanted t
2 CONC o attend but he decided to hit the road instead!  I have always been to
2 CONC ld that he left when he was 11 and moved into the Kelley Boarding House a
2 CONC t about 19 or 20.  That would be where he and Grandma met.  Grandpa mus
2 CONC t have had some schooling because he loved crossword puzzles and could d
2 CONC o math in his head with no effort. Also, if you look at some of the hou
2 CONC ses and buildings he built in Hannibal and Vandalia which are still sta
2 CONC nding, he had some knowledge beyond basic carpentry.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An additional note from Denise Crumpton:
2 CONT
2 CONT My grandfather said that he was offered an opportunity to go to school a
2 CONC nd be educated, but it was all too constricting for him, so he left at a
2 CONC ge 11 to go on his own.  Let me say here that Grandpa Allen possessed a b
2 CONC rilliant mind and could do anything.  He built many of the buildings in H
2 CONC annibal, MO and could sit down with a crossword puzzle and knock it out i
2 CONC n no time. However, being tied down inside a building never thrilled hi
2 CONC m!
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On Rich's marriage record from 1900 (from Ancestry.com), J.W. Wilson is l
2 CONC isted as his guardian.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Newspaper article (obtained from McCauley Family Tree at http://trees.a
2 CONC ncestry.com/tree/62925788/person/46114641005/photo):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. And Mrs. Allen To Have Anniversary Celebration Sunday
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. D. R. Allen, Sr., will celebrate their fiftieth wedding an
2 CONC niversary on Sunday, August 13, with a family dinner in their home, 124
2 CONC 8a Market street.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were married at Perry on August 14, 1900, by the Rev. Collin A. Mc
2 CONC Phethers, a Presbyterian minister.  Mrs. Allen was formerly Ida Kelly a
2 CONC nd lived at Perry many years, having moved there at the age of eight ye
2 CONC ars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Allen, nephew of Dr. J. W. Wilson, was reared in his home and lived f
2 CONC or a time at Rensselaer, later moving to Madisonville when Dr. Wilson e
2 CONC stablished his practice there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Allen have lived for 26 years in Hannibal where he has bee
2 CONC n a carpenter and contractor.
2 CONT
2 CONT They are the parents of six living children, Russell of Waukegan, Ill., M
2 CONC rs. Hazel Davis of Los Angeles, Calif., Mrs. R. T. Cole of Huntington P
2 CONC ark, Calif., Rev. D. R. Allen, Jr., of Hannibal, Melvin and William of B
2 CONC attle Creek, Mich.  One child died in infancy.  They also have nine gra
2 CONC ndchildren and two great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Expected here for the celebration are Mr. and Mrs. Melvin Allen and dau
2 CONC ghter of Battle Creek; Russel Allen, a grandson, Richard Allen and Mrs. A
2 CONC llen, and a granddaughter, Mrs. William Coburn and her husband, all of W
2 CONC aukegan; and Mr. and Mrs. York Dolbearer and two children of New Canton
2 CONC , Ill.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate is Daivd [sic] Richard Wilson.  Cause o
2 CONC f death was acute cardiac failure due to arteriosclerosis and senile ch
2 CONC anges (?).  Also, had Parkinson's disease.  Died at home (1300 A Market S
2 CONC t); had lived there three years.
