1 _UID 48BB7EB1C1274C7EAC4AEBA4BF0888CB8380
1 CHAN
2 DATE 19 JUL 2017
1 NOTE From http://www.450thbg.com/real/stories/b17.shtml
2 CONT
2 CONT My great uncle's name is William Roland Harkness. My father said that h
2 CONC e went by the name Roland. I am trying to get more information but have v
2 CONC ery little to go with as it all of his paperwork from that time period w
2 CONC as destroyed in a house fire during the late 1980's. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT This article was from the Shreveport Times Wed Oct 11, 1944. Title: Bul
2 CONC garia Prison was a rat hole. Athens La. flier tells of six months of "s
2 CONC imulated hell". With the 15th AAF in Italy on a mission over Sofia Bulg
2 CONC aria on Jan 24 1944, Sgt William R. Harkness's Flying Fortress was hit, b
2 CONC ecause of extremely bad weather and zero visibility, by bombs from the p
2 CONC lane above them. Sgt Harkness of Athens and five other members of the c
2 CONC rew were able to bail out of the disabled ship. Once on the ground, the m
2 CONC en assembled to plan their return to their own lines. Because of the ba
2 CONC d weather conditions their exact Whereabouts was unknown to themselves. T
2 CONC hey thought they were in Yugoslavia, and with that in mind started head
2 CONC ing west in hopes of reaching the coast and partisan aid. Shortly after t
2 CONC hey started walking they met civilians who seemed friendly. The civilia
2 CONC ns told the men that they were still in Bulgaria and after some talk ag
2 CONC reed to shelter them for the night and start them on their way to safet
2 CONC y the next day. "After spending the night in the Bulgarian Village" sai
2 CONC d Sergeant Harkness, "We Started out led by the civilians. Unfortunatel
2 CONC y we were intercepted by Bulgarian Soldiers who took us prisoners and m
2 CONC arched twelve miles to their Headquarters. One of our party had a broke
2 CONC n ankle and another a badly wrenched knee. All of us were pretty well b
2 CONC ruised up so we had to help each other along the road and use sticks to s
2 CONC upport ourselves. "Once at Bulgaria Headquarters we were questioned inc
2 CONC essantly for six days. We were threatened shouted at and generally mali
2 CONC gned while facing the interrogation officer. When we weren't being ques
2 CONC tioned, we were held in a cellar with nothing but boards to sleep on. T
2 CONC here was water on the floor and we had no blankets or other protection a
2 CONC gainst the biting cold. Our injuries received no medical attention desp
2 CONC ite our treatment and demands according to the Geneva Convention. As fo
2 CONC r food we got practically nothing. "We were then after six days taken t
2 CONC o Sofia, where we were again questioned and threatened for five more da
2 CONC ys. Here again we received no medical attention whatsoever. We were hel
2 CONC d in a dark and cold dungeon and had no protection against the freezing w
2 CONC eather. Food amounted to a piece of bread and some soup now and then. "
2 CONC We were moved to a prison camp where the conditions were almost animal l
2 CONC ike fifty men slept in an ill ventilated and filthy barracks. We were m
2 CONC ade to sleep on the floor. With but one lice and vermin infected blanke
2 CONC t as protection against the cold. Food here was limited to a very thin b
2 CONC roth twice a day accompanied one slice of old bread. After six months o
2 CONC f this simulated hell we were moved to another prison camp. "At the sec
2 CONC ond prison camp the conditions were a little better. The Bulgarians cou
2 CONC ld see their ultimate defeat coming. After a month we were released and b
2 CONC rought back to Italy when Bulgaria Capitulated. Sergeant Harkness whose w
2 CONC ife Mrs. Ophelia Harkness lives at Athens, Louisiana had 14 missions to h
2 CONC is credit when he went down.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE Military
2 DATE World War II
2 NOTE U.S. Army Air Corps
