1 NAME Adair W. /Miller/
2 GIVN Adair W.
2 SURN Miller
1 SEX M
1 _UID AFB4C6EE964A46EBB801445268150AC9C750
1 CHAN
2 DATE 20 JUN 2018
1 NOTE Boston Globe, 17 Mar 2007:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adair Miller, pilot, sailor, with a love of adventure
2 CONT
2 CONT If he had stayed on in Tahiti, life might have been different for Adair M
2 CONC iller.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perhaps he would have missed out flying DC-4s across the Atlantic durin
2 CONC g World War II or he might never have flown transcontinental trips as a T
2 CONC WA pilot.
2 CONT
2 CONT "He was not a man to talk much about himself," said his son Dusty of No
2 CONC rwalk, Conn.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he did talk, though, more than a hint of adventure came through in t
2 CONC he stories of his early 20s. Mr. Miller, who was a pilot with TWA for n
2 CONC early 30 years, died Feb. 10 in Berlin, Vt., in the home of one of his c
2 CONC hildren. He was 94, and his health had been declining in the last few m
2 CONC onths.
2 CONT
2 CONT "He was an outgoing, gregarious person, on his terms, not all the time, n
2 CONC ot flashy in any way," his son said. "If you got him going, on a boat o
2 CONC r at a cocktail party, he could get a lot of people laughing and certai
2 CONC nly enjoyed doing so."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Miller certainly had plenty of material.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of five children, he was born in Munich. His father was a physician
2 CONC , and before World War I his parents often lived abroad. With the appro
2 CONC ach of war, they returned to the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Growing up in New Rochelle, N.Y., Mr. Miller attended boarding schools, b
2 CONC ut it was never clear from family accounts and anecdotes from which or i
2 CONC f he graduated, his son said.
2 CONT
2 CONT But at 21, Mr. Miller set forth on a significant educational experience w
2 CONC hen he signed up in 1934 as a cadet on the full-rigged sailing ship the J
2 CONC oseph Conrad. The writer and adventurer Alan Villiers had renamed the s
2 CONC hip, which formerly had been used to train sailors in Denmark, and recr
2 CONC uited a crew of boys and young men to man the ship on a three-year, rou
2 CONC nd-the-world voyage.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the trip, Villiers wrote "The Cruise of the Conrad," an account t
2 CONC hat became a popular book in the late 1930s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Miller, however, did not stay with the Conrad for the full journey. W
2 CONC hen the ship arrived in Tahiti, he decided to remain on the island.
2 CONT
2 CONT "He liked it a lot," his son said with a chuckle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the stories Mr. Miller told in later years was the tale of a Fren
2 CONC chman he met in a bar, who "invited him to his house and wound up invit
2 CONC ing him to marry his daughter," his son said. Mr. Miller demurred, but s
2 CONC tayed on the island, even cashing in at least one ticket his father had s
2 CONC ent in hope of luring him home.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he at last returned to New York City, Mr. Miller tried his hand at i
2 CONC nvestment banking, a profession that seemed more than simply a hemisphe
2 CONC re away from his life on Tahiti.
2 CONT
2 CONT "It was probably a little too rigid for him, I would infer," his son sa
2 CONC id.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Miller turned to aviation, joining the Air Transport Command in 194
2 CONC 1 and flying planes to Canada. He later flew DC-4 aircraft from South A
2 CONC merica to Africa and the Middle East during World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, he became a pilot for TWA's intercontinental division, h
2 CONC is son said, and transferred to Boston in 1947, moving with his wife, J
2 CONC udith, to Andover. The two had met on a blind date during the war years
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT "I think the story was that he was supposed to go out with her roommate
2 CONC , and the roommate wanted to go out with someone else on that day, so t
2 CONC hat's how they got together," their son said. She was still in college, a
2 CONC nd Mr. Miller "still was showing some of the flash of his early-, mid-2
2 CONC 0s."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the last few years of flying for TWA, Mr. Miller began working with T
2 CONC urner Fisheries of Boston, using layover time on the West Coast to find r
2 CONC estaurants that might want to import East Coast fish, his son said. Mr. M
2 CONC iller retired from the airline in 1972, when he was 60, then worked for m
2 CONC ore than a decade with Turner Fisheries as a sales representative, meet
2 CONC ing with clients throughout the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Still enamored of the sea, Mr. Miller kept a 35-foot wooden yawl, the M
2 CONC onhegan II, at his summer home in Gloucester, where he was a member of t
2 CONC he Eastern Point Yacht Club for 55 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few months ago, Mr. Miller and his wife moved to Montpelier to be clo
2 CONC ser to their son, Conrad, who lives in nearby Berlin. Mr. Miller died a
2 CONC t Conrad's home.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his wife and his sons Dusty and Conrad, Mr. Miller leave
2 CONC s another son, Bryan of Singapore; three granddaughters; and a grandson
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service will be announced.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The name on his death certificate is Adair Miller.  Cause of death was p
2 CONC rostate cancer.  Died in Berlin, VT.
