1 NOTE Barre Montpelier Times Argus, 22 Sep 2005 (obtained from their web site
2 CONC ):
2 CONT
2 CONT Marion Miller Minewski
2 CONT
2 CONT BERLIN — Marion Miller Minewski, 91, died Sept. 12, 2005, at Woodridge N
2 CONC ursing Home in Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born July 29, 1914, in Munich, Germany, the youngest child and o
2 CONC nly daughter of Charles G. and Maude Adair Miller. She grew up in Conne
2 CONC cticut and attended Dana Hall and Katherine Gibbs schools. She was marr
2 CONC ied to Alex Minewski in 1945 and was later divorced.
2 CONT
2 CONT A lifelong artist, Marion was a potter, sculptor and painter. She lived a
2 CONC nd painted in Paris in the early 1950s and in New York City from 1951 u
2 CONC ntil moving to Vermont in 2001. At the young and impressionable age of 8
2 CONC 7, she adopted and provided for several hundred of Vermont's woodpecker
2 CONC s, goldfinches, cardinals, squirrels, chipmunks and a few turkeys and r
2 CONC accoons.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Zen Buddhist, she was a lover of all creatures, great and small, espe
2 CONC cially cats. She was passionate about politics and traveled extensively t
2 CONC o Europe, East Africa, Japan, Cambodia, Thailand and Singapore. Her mor
2 CONC e recent travels were her daily walks on Berlin Pond and the Montpelier B
2 CONC ike Path topped off with a creemee.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was blessed with exceptional caregivers as well as the attentive st
2 CONC aff at Woodridge Nursing Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include her brother, Adair Miller and his wife Judith; nephew
2 CONC s, Adair Miller, Jr., Bryan Miller, Conrad Miller and Charles Miller; n
2 CONC iece Chesley Miller, several great nieces and nephews and cousins. Two b
2 CONC rothers, Guy Miller and Charles G. Miller predeceased her.
2 CONT
2 CONT A memorial service will be held at a later date. In lieu of flowers, pl
2 CONC ease contribute to your favorite charity.
