1 _UID C117881E899147B0BF8BF23EFD95F642D384
1 CHAN
2 DATE 27 MAR 2017
1 NOTE It looks like he was named for his grandfather, who was a U.S. senator f
2 CONC rom Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Santa Cruz Evening News, 15 Aug 1917, p. 7 (obtained from Newspapers.co
2 CONC m):
2 CONT
2 CONT TRAVELS OF MRS. H. C. COOPER
2 CONT
2 CONT Word has been received from Mrs. H. C. Cooper who is visiting her son G
2 CONC eorge R. Dennis at his farm on Canough Creek thirty-six miles from Kaml
2 CONC oops, B. C.
2 CONT
2 CONT She says Mr. Dennis and his wife are the only Americans in this vicinit
2 CONC y and he is rapidly becoming a leader.  He is a great worker and has ac
2 CONC complished wonders on this land in the two years he has resided there.  H
2 CONC e has built two houses for himself and one for a neighbor, also a large b
2 CONC arn, many outbuildings, fenced most of the 160 acres, besides clearing a g
2 CONC ood many acres and looking after the stock and helping his neighbors.  H
2 CONC e and his wife get up at four o'clock in the morning and never stop wor
2 CONC king until dark which is at this time of the year 10 p. m.  They have a d
2 CONC ifficult task as the ground is full of rock--this however, with the tim
2 CONC ber, they have utilized in building.  It is a wild but beautiful countr
2 CONC y, wooded with fir, spruce and pine, also poplar and birch in spots.  T
2 CONC here are some beautiful lakes near here, through one of which we were o
2 CONC bliged to drive.  If Santa Cruz people had to ride over these roads the
2 CONC y would never think their own bad again.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is a very independent life here as taxes are only a few dollars on 1
2 CONC 70 acres and the only bill is for groceries, as they raise everything e
2 CONC lse.  At present there is not much fresh meat, but plenty of chickens, e
2 CONC ggs, cream, and butter, also ice which they get from the lake in winter s
2 CONC o we have ice cream every day.  In winter they kill steers and hogs and s
2 CONC hare with their neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ride from Vancouver here is [sic]
2 CONT
2 CONT The ride from Vancouver here along the Frazer is a fascinating one.  Th
2 CONC ere is a railroad on both sides of it and many tunnels.  Vancouver is a l
2 CONC arge and attractive city but in some ways much behind our cities in the s
2 CONC tates.  Its stores and business blocks looked very shabby to me.  I did
2 CONC n't see a store to compare with Mr. Leask's.  The people there must be f
2 CONC ond of sweets as there were two or three candy stores to every block.  T
2 CONC here is a beautiful park.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many families around here are subsisting on the war, as the Canadian go
2 CONC vernment is quite liberal.  One woman with two children gets about a hu
2 CONC ndred dollars a month, the husband never earned anything before; she ge
2 CONC ts a separation allowance for herself and two children, part of the sal
2 CONC ary and money from the patriotic society.
2 CONT
2 CONT We only get the mail here once a week; postage is .03 on letters and .0
2 CONC 2 on postals.
2 CONT
2 CONT I expect soon to leave for Banff, a celebrated resort in Alberta.  The r
2 CONC oad, I am told, is very picturesque, and leads through a tunnel fifteen m
2 CONC iles long, where the train is carried through by electricity.
1 BIRT
2 DATE 23 DEC 1882
