1 NOTE From Find A Grave (listing for Wilford Bacon Hoggatt):
2 CONT
2 CONT Alaska Territorial Governor. He graduated from the United States Naval A
2 CONC cademy in 1884 and remained in the Navy for 18 years, including service d
2 CONC uring the Spanish-American War. He graduated from Columbian Law School i
2 CONC n 1893 and also graduated from the Columbia University School of Mines. A
2 CONC fter leaving the Navy Hoggatt settled in Juneau, Alaska where his broth
2 CONC er and he became successful in the gold mining business. In 1884 Alaska w
2 CONC as designated a federal district with a civil government and a Governor a
2 CONC ppointed by President Chester A. Arthur. In 1906 he was appointed Gover
2 CONC nor and served for three years. During his term Hoggatt was responsible f
2 CONC or relocating Alaska's government from Sitka to Juneau, where it has re
2 CONC mained ever since. In 1925 he moved to New York City, where he lived in r
2 CONC etirement. His health began to fail in 1936, and he died at the Bronx V
2 CONC eterans Hospital. His second wife, Clarissa Eames Millard, was from Uti
2 CONC ca, and she decided to have him interred there.
