1 CHAN
2 DATE 20 JUL 2017
1 NOTE New York Times, 22 May 1981 (obtained from their web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT FORT BELVOIR, Va., May 21— Maj. Gen. Harry H. Vaughan, whose actions as c
2 CONC onfidant and aide-de-camp to President Truman a generation ago led to a s
2 CONC eries of national uproars, died here yesterday at DeWitt Army Hospital. H
2 CONC e was 87 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Vaughan's reputation ranged from that of White House court jest
2 CONC er to national menace, depending on the severity of the storm engulfing h
2 CONC im at a particular moment.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Vaughan traded barbs with the likes of Drew Pearson, the column
2 CONC ist, and Senator Joseph R. McCarthy, who accused the Presidential aide o
2 CONC f being an influence-peddling ''5 percenter,'' a term applied to those w
2 CONC ho offered to get Government contracts for businesses for a fee, usuall
2 CONC y 5 percent. The general, called to testify about his activities by a S
2 CONC enate committee, was reprimanded for accepting seven home freezers as g
2 CONC ifts for himself and highranking friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Mr. Truman was urged by Mr. Pearson to dismiss General Vaughan aft
2 CONC er it was disclosed that the general had accepted a medal from the neof
2 CONC ascist Argentine Government, the President retorted, ''No S.O.B. is goi
2 CONC ng to tell me who to have on my staff or in my Cabinet.'' The remark to
2 CONC uched off a national outburst of tongueclucking.
2 CONT
2 CONT Awards for Gallantry
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet General Vaughan, who retired from the Army in January 1953, was har
2 CONC dly a summer soldier. He was awarded two Silver Stars for gallantry and t
2 CONC he French Croix de Guerre for bravery in World War I, in which he serve
2 CONC d alongside Mr. Truman.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two men from Missouri met at Fort Sill, Okla., in 1917 when both we
2 CONC re young lieutenants in the field artillery. Lieutenant Vaughan managed t
2 CONC o extricate Lieutenant Truman from a dressing-down given by their briga
2 CONC de commander, and they became friends for life.
2 CONT
2 CONT They served in the Army Reserves between the wars and at the outbreak o
2 CONC f World War II, with Mr. Truman serving in the United States Senate, Mr
2 CONC . Vaughan returned to active duty as a lieutenant colonel. He was injur
2 CONC ed in a plane crash in Australia in 1943, and while being treated at Wa
2 CONC lter Reed Army Hospital in Washington was transferred to the staff of t
2 CONC he Senate Armed Services subcommittee investigating arms procurement th
2 CONC at was headed by Senator Truman.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Vaughan made many trips to battlefields as an investigator for t
2 CONC he committee, whose uncovering of deception by the munitions industry l
2 CONC ed to national prominence for Senator Truman, and the Senator's eventua
2 CONC l selection by President Roosevelt as his running mate in 1944.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aide to the Vice President
2 CONT
2 CONT After the election the new Vice President named then-Brigadier General V
2 CONC aughan as his military aide, a rare appointment for a reserve officer. I
2 CONC n Mr. Truman's eight years as Vice President and then President, he and G
2 CONC eneral Vaughan were the charter members of a group that met frequently t
2 CONC o play poker, swap stories and drink bourbon with branch water.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Truman and his aide often had lunch together and followed the meal w
2 CONC ith a swim in the White House pool. Once admonished for having too much i
2 CONC nfluence over the President, General Vaughan retorted: ''The President a
2 CONC nd I discuss numerous things, but my influence over him is doubtful. I d
2 CONC on't suppose anyone gives him more advice than I do, or has less of it u
2 CONC sed.''
2 CONT
2 CONT General Vaughan was born in Glasgow, Mo., on Nov. 26, 1893, and grew up i
2 CONC n St. Louis. He received a bachelor's degree from Westminster College i
2 CONC n Fulton, Mo., in 1916.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Vaughan, whose wife died in 1975, is survived by a son, a daugh
2 CONC ter and three grandchildren. Funeral services will be Saturday in Alexa
2 CONC ndria, Va.
